const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"A hall in the Duke's palace.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Duke, Egeon, Jailer, Officers and other Attendants.","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEON","lines":["Proceed, Solinus, to procure my fall,","And by the doom of death end woes and all."],"id":2,"m":"Go on, Solinus, bring about my ruin, and end my sorrows and everything else with a sentence of death."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Merchant of Syracusa, plead no more.","I am not partial to infringe our laws.","The enmity and discord which of late","Sprung from the rancorous outrage of your Duke","To merchants, our well-dealing countrymen,","Who, wanting guilders to redeem their lives,","Have seal'd his rigorous statutes with their bloods,","Excludes all pity from our threat'ning looks.","For since the mortal and intestine jars","'Twixt thy seditious countrymen and us,","It hath in solemn synods been decreed,","Both by the Syracusians and ourselves,","To admit no traffic to our adverse towns;","Nay more, if any born at Ephesus","Be seen at Syracusian marts and fairs;","Again, if any Syracusian born","Come to the bay of Ephesus, he dies,","His goods confiscate to the Duke's dispose,","Unless a thousand marks be levied","To quit the penalty and to ransom him.","Thy substance, valued at the highest rate,","Cannot amount unto a hundred marks;","Therefore by law thou art condemn'd to die."],"id":3,"m":"Merchant of Syracuse, stop pleading. I am not inclined to bend our laws. Your Duke has lately treated our honest merchants with spiteful cruelty: when they could not find the money to buy back their lives, he sealed his harsh laws with their blood. That hostility and conflict rules out any pity in my face. Ever since the deadly civil quarrels between your rebellious countrymen and us, it has been decreed in formal councils, by both the Syracusans and ourselves, that there will be no trade between our hostile cities. More than that: if anyone born in Ephesus is seen at the markets and fairs of Syracuse, or if anyone born in Syracuse comes into the bay of Ephesus, he dies, and his goods are seized for the Duke to dispose of, unless a thousand marks are raised to pay the penalty and ransom him. Your property, valued at the very highest, comes to less than a hundred marks. So by law you are condemned to die."},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEON","lines":["Yet this my comfort; when your words are done,","My woes end likewise with the evening sun."],"id":4,"m":"Yet I have this comfort: when your sentence is carried out, my sorrows will end with the setting sun."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Well, Syracusian, say in brief the cause","Why thou departedst from thy native home,","And for what cause thou cam'st to Ephesus."],"id":5,"m":"Well, man of Syracuse, tell me briefly why you left your home, and why you came to Ephesus."},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEON","lines":["A heavier task could not have been impos'd","Than I to speak my griefs unspeakable;","Yet, that the world may witness that my end","Was wrought by nature, not by vile offence,","I'll utter what my sorrow gives me leave.","In Syracusa was I born, and wed","Unto a woman happy but for me,","And by me, had not our hap been bad.","With her I liv'd in joy; our wealth increas'd","By prosperous voyages I often made","To Epidamnum, till my factor's death,","And the great care of goods at random left,","Drew me from kind embracements of my spouse;","From whom my absence was not six months old","Before herself (almost at fainting under","The pleasing punishment that women bear)","Had made provision for her following me,","And soon and safe arrived where I was.","There had she not been long but she became","A joyful mother of two goodly sons,","And, which was strange, the one so like the other","As could not be distinguish'd but by names.","That very hour, and in the self-same inn,","A mean woman was delivered","Of such a burden, male twins, both alike.","Those, for their parents were exceeding poor,","I bought, and brought up to attend my sons.","My wife, not meanly proud of two such boys,","Made daily motions for our home return.","Unwilling I agreed; alas, too soon","We came aboard.","A league from Epidamnum had we sail'd","Before the always-wind-obeying deep","Gave any tragic instance of our harm;","But longer did we not retain much hope;","For what obscured light the heavens did grant","Did but convey unto our fearful minds","A doubtful warrant of immediate death,","Which though myself would gladly have embrac'd,","Yet the incessant weepings of my wife,","Weeping before for what she saw must come,","And piteous plainings of the pretty babes,","That mourn'd for fashion, ignorant what to fear,","Forc'd me to seek delays for them and me.","And this it was (for other means was none).","The sailors sought for safety by our boat,","And left the ship, then sinking-ripe, to us.","My wife, more careful for the latter-born,","Had fast'ned him unto a small spare mast,","Such as sea-faring men provide for storms.","To him one of the other twins was bound,","Whilst I had been like heedful of the other.","The children thus dispos'd, my wife and I,","Fixing our eyes on whom our care was fix'd,","Fast'ned ourselves at either end the mast,","And, floating straight, obedient to the stream,","Was carried towards Corinth, as we thought.","At length the sun, gazing upon the earth,","Dispers'd those vapours that offended us,","And by the benefit of his wished light","The seas wax'd calm, and we discovered","Two ships from far, making amain to us,","Of Corinth that, of Epidaurus this.","But ere they came—O, let me say no more!","Gather the sequel by that went before."],"id":6,"m":"No harder task could be set me than to speak of griefs too great for words. Still, so that the world may see my death was caused by natural love and not by any wicked crime, I will tell as much as my sorrow allows. I was born in Syracuse, and married a woman who would have been happy if it weren't for me, and would have been happy with me if our luck had not been bad. I lived with her in joy, and our wealth grew through the profitable voyages I often made to Epidamnum. Then my agent there died, and the need to look after goods left unattended drew me away from my wife's loving arms. I had been gone less than six months when she, though nearly fainting under the pleasant burden that women carry, arranged to follow me, and arrived safe and soon where I was. She had not been there long before she became the joyful mother of two fine sons, and, strangely, the one so like the other that they could only be told apart by their names. In that same hour, at the very same inn, a poor woman gave birth to identical twin boys. Because their parents were extremely poor, I bought them and brought them up to serve my sons. My wife, very proud of our two boys, kept pressing for us to go home. I agreed, unwillingly, and all too soon we went aboard. We had sailed three miles from Epidamnum before the sea, which always obeys the wind, gave any sign of the disaster to come. But after that we didn't keep much hope, for the dim light the heavens gave us only told our frightened minds that death was near. I would gladly have accepted it, but my wife's constant weeping, as she cried in advance for what she saw was coming, and the pitiful wailing of the babies, who cried because she did without knowing what to fear, forced me to look for ways to delay death for them and me. This was what happened, since there was no other way out. The sailors escaped in our lifeboat and left the ship, ready to sink, to us. My wife, more anxious about the younger twin, had tied him to a small spare mast of the kind sailors carry for storms, and with him one of the other twins. I had done the same for the other two. With the children arranged like that, my wife and I, keeping our eyes on the ones we cared for, tied ourselves to either end of the mast, and, floating with the current, were carried towards Corinth, or so we thought. At last the sun shone on the earth and broke up the mists that had troubled us. In that welcome light the sea grew calm, and we saw two ships racing towards us from far off, one from Corinth, the other from Epidaurus. But before they reached us... O, let me say no more! Guess the rest from what came before."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Nay, forward, old man, do not break off so,","For we may pity, though not pardon thee."],"id":7,"m":"No, go on, old man, don't stop there. I may pity you, even if I cannot pardon you."},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEON","lines":["O, had the gods done so, I had not now","Worthily term'd them merciless to us.","For, ere the ships could meet by twice five leagues,","We were encountered by a mighty rock,","Which being violently borne upon,","Our helpful ship was splitted in the midst;","So that, in this unjust divorce of us,","Fortune had left to both of us alike","What to delight in, what to sorrow for.","Her part, poor soul, seeming as burdened","With lesser weight, but not with lesser woe,","Was carried with more speed before the wind,","And in our sight they three were taken up","By fishermen of Corinth, as we thought.","At length another ship had seiz'd on us;","And, knowing whom it was their hap to save,","Gave healthful welcome to their ship-wrack'd guests,","And would have reft the fishers of their prey,","Had not their bark been very slow of sail;","And therefore homeward did they bend their course.","Thus have you heard me sever'd from my bliss,","That by misfortunes was my life prolong'd","To tell sad stories of my own mishaps."],"id":8,"m":"O, if the gods had pitied us, I would not now rightly be calling them merciless! Before the ships could get within ten leagues of us, we were driven onto a great rock. We struck it so violently that our helpful mast was split in two, so that in this cruel separation Fortune left each of us something to delight in and something to grieve for. Her half, poor soul, carrying less weight though no less sorrow, was swept along faster by the wind, and before our eyes the three of them were picked up by fishermen from Corinth, as we thought. At last another ship rescued us. Knowing who they had saved, the crew made us welcome, shipwrecked as we were, and would have taken the others from the fishermen if their ship had not been so slow. So they turned and sailed for home. Now you have heard how I was parted from my happiness, and how bad luck kept me alive only to tell the sad story of my own misfortunes."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["And for the sake of them thou sorrowest for,","Do me the favour to dilate at full","What have befall'n of them and thee till now."],"id":9,"m":"For the sake of those you are grieving for, do me the favour of telling me in full what has happened to them and to you since then."},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEON","lines":["My youngest boy, and yet my eldest care,","At eighteen years became inquisitive","After his brother, and importun'd me","That his attendant, so his case was like,","Reft of his brother, but retain'd his name,","Might bear him company in the quest of him;","Whom whilst I laboured of a love to see,","I hazarded the loss of whom I lov'd.","Five summers have I spent in farthest Greece,","Roaming clean through the bounds of Asia,","And, coasting homeward, came to Ephesus,","Hopeless to find, yet loath to leave unsought","Or that or any place that harbours men.","But here must end the story of my life;","And happy were I in my timely death,","Could all my travels warrant me they live."],"id":10,"m":"My youngest boy, though he is my eldest care, began asking about his brother when he was eighteen. He begged me to let his servant, who was in the same situation, robbed of his brother but keeping his name, go with him to search for him. Because I longed to see the son I had lost, I risked losing the one I loved. I have spent five summers in the furthest parts of Greece, travelling right through Asia Minor, and coming home along the coast I reached Ephesus. I had no hope of finding them, yet I could not bear to leave this or any other inhabited place unsearched. But here my life's story must end. I would be happy to die now, if all my travels could assure me they are alive."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Hapless Egeon, whom the fates have mark'd","To bear the extremity of dire mishap;","Now, trust me, were it not against our laws,","Against my crown, my oath, my dignity,","Which princes, would they, may not disannul,","My soul should sue as advocate for thee.","But though thou art adjudged to the death,","And passed sentence may not be recall'd","But to our honour's great disparagement,","Yet will I favour thee in what I can.","Therefore, merchant, I'll limit thee this day","To seek thy health by beneficial help.","Try all the friends thou hast in Ephesus;","Beg thou, or borrow, to make up the sum,","And live; if no, then thou art doom'd to die.","Jailer, take him to thy custody."],"id":11,"m":"Unlucky Egeon, marked out by the fates to suffer the worst misfortune! Believe me, if it were not against our laws, my crown, my oath and my dignity, which princes cannot cancel even if they wish to, I would plead for you myself. But though you are sentenced to death, and the sentence cannot be withdrawn without great damage to my honour, I will still help you as far as I can. So, merchant, I give you this one day to save yourself by finding help. Try every friend you have in Ephesus. Beg or borrow to make up the sum, and live. If not, you are condemned to die. Jailer, take him into custody."},{"t":"sp","who":"JAILER","lines":["I will, my lord."],"id":12,"m":"I will, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEON","lines":["Hopeless and helpless doth Egeon wend,","But to procrastinate his lifeless end."],"id":13,"m":"Without hope or help, Egeon goes off, only to put off the death that will end his life."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":14},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"The mart.","id":15},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Antipholus and Dromio of Syracuse and a Merchant.","id":16},{"t":"sp","who":"MERCHANT","lines":["Therefore give out you are of Epidamnum,","Lest that your goods too soon be confiscate.","This very day a Syracusian merchant","Is apprehended for arrival here,","And, not being able to buy out his life,","According to the statute of the town","Dies ere the weary sun set in the west.","There is your money that I had to keep."],"id":17,"m":"So say you come from Epidamnum, or your goods will be seized straight away. This very day a merchant from Syracuse has been arrested for landing here, and since he can't buy his life, by the law of this town he dies before the tired sun sets in the west. Here is the money I was keeping for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Go bear it to the Centaur, where we host,","And stay there, Dromio, till I come to thee.","Within this hour it will be dinnertime;","Till that, I'll view the manners of the town,","Peruse the traders, gaze upon the buildings,","And then return and sleep within mine inn,","For with long travel I am stiff and weary.","Get thee away."],"id":18,"m":"Take it to the Centaur, where we're staying, and wait there till I come, Dromio. It will be dinnertime within the hour. Until then I'll look at the ways of the town, study the traders, gaze at the buildings, then go back and sleep at the inn, because I'm stiff and tired from travelling so long. Off you go."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Many a man would take you at your word,","And go indeed, having so good a mean."],"id":19,"m":"Many a man would take you at your word and go off for good, with such a good means of doing it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Dromio.","id":20},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["A trusty villain, sir, that very oft,","When I am dull with care and melancholy,","Lightens my humour with his merry jests.","What, will you walk with me about the town,","And then go to my inn and dine with me?"],"id":21,"m":"He's a trustworthy rascal, sir. Very often, when I'm low with worry and gloom, he cheers me up with his jokes. Will you walk round the town with me, then come back to my inn and have dinner with me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MERCHANT","lines":["I am invited, sir, to certain merchants,","Of whom I hope to make much benefit.","I crave your pardon. Soon, at five o'clock,","Please you, I'll meet with you upon the mart,","And afterward consort you till bedtime.","My present business calls me from you now."],"id":22,"m":"I'm invited by some merchants, sir, and I hope to do good business with them. Please excuse me. Later, at five o'clock, if you like, I'll meet you at the market and keep you company till bedtime. My business calls me away now."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Farewell till then: I will go lose myself,","And wander up and down to view the city."],"id":23,"m":"Goodbye till then. I'll go and lose myself and wander up and down looking at the city."},{"t":"sp","who":"MERCHANT","lines":["Sir, I commend you to your own content."],"id":24,"m":"Sir, I wish you all the contentment you could want."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Merchant.","id":25},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["He that commends me to mine own content","Commends me to the thing I cannot get.","I to the world am like a drop of water","That in the ocean seeks another drop,","Who, failing there to find his fellow forth,","Unseen, inquisitive, confounds himself.","So I, to find a mother and a brother,","In quest of them, unhappy, lose myself."],"id":26,"m":"He wishes me contentment, the one thing I cannot get. In this world I am like a drop of water searching the ocean for another drop: failing to find its fellow, it falls in, unseen and searching, and loses itself. So I, seeking a mother and a brother, lose myself in the search, unhappy as I am."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Dromio of Ephesus.","id":27},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","cont":1,"lines":["Here comes the almanac of my true date.","What now? How chance thou art return'd so soon?"],"id":28,"m":"Here comes the calendar of my own life. What's this? How come you're back so soon?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Return'd so soon? rather approach'd too late.","The capon burns, the pig falls from the spit;","The clock hath strucken twelve upon the bell;","My mistress made it one upon my cheek.","She is so hot because the meat is cold;","The meat is cold because you come not home;","You come not home because you have no stomach;","You have no stomach, having broke your fast;","But we that know what 'tis to fast and pray,","Are penitent for your default today."],"id":29,"m":"Back so soon? More like too late. The chicken's burning, the pig's falling off the spit, the clock has struck twelve, and my mistress has struck one on my cheek. She's hot because the food is cold; the food is cold because you haven't come home; you haven't come home because you're not hungry; you're not hungry because you've already eaten. But we who know what it is to fast and pray are being punished for your fault today."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Stop in your wind, sir, tell me this, I pray:","Where have you left the money that I gave you?"],"id":30,"m":"Hold your breath, sir, and tell me this, please: where have you left the money I gave you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["O, sixpence that I had o' Wednesday last","To pay the saddler for my mistress' crupper:","The saddler had it, sir, I kept it not."],"id":31,"m":"Oh, the sixpence I had last Wednesday to pay the saddler for my mistress's crupper? The saddler had it, sir, I didn't keep it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I am not in a sportive humour now.","Tell me, and dally not, where is the money?","We being strangers here, how dar'st thou trust","So great a charge from thine own custody?"],"id":32,"m":"I'm in no mood for jokes now. Tell me, and don't mess about: where is the money? We are strangers here, so how dare you let such a large sum out of your own keeping?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["I pray you jest, sir, as you sit at dinner:","I from my mistress come to you in post;","If I return, I shall be post indeed,","For she will score your fault upon my pate.","Methinks your maw, like mine, should be your clock,","And strike you home without a messenger."],"id":33,"m":"Please save your jokes till you're sitting at dinner. I've rushed here from my mistress. If I go back without you, I'll be a post indeed, for she'll chalk up your fault on my head. I'd have thought your stomach, like mine, would be your clock and send you home without a messenger."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Come, Dromio, come, these jests are out of season,","Reserve them till a merrier hour than this.","Where is the gold I gave in charge to thee?"],"id":34,"m":"Come on, Dromio, these jokes are out of place. Save them for a happier time. Where is the gold I left in your charge?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["To me, sir? why, you gave no gold to me!"],"id":35,"m":"With me, sir? Why, you gave me no gold!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Come on, sir knave, have done your foolishness,","And tell me how thou hast dispos'd thy charge."],"id":36,"m":"Come on, you rogue, stop this foolery, and tell me what you've done with what I gave you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["My charge was but to fetch you from the mart","Home to your house, the Phoenix, sir, to dinner.","My mistress and her sister stay for you."],"id":37,"m":"My charge was only to fetch you from the market to your house, the Phoenix, sir, for dinner. My mistress and her sister are waiting for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Now, as I am a Christian, answer me","In what safe place you have bestow'd my money,","Or I shall break that merry sconce of yours","That stands on tricks when I am undispos'd;","Where is the thousand marks thou hadst of me?"],"id":38,"m":"As I am a Christian, tell me where you have put my money safely, or I'll crack that jolly head of yours that insists on jokes when I'm not in the mood. Where are the thousand marks you had from me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["I have some marks of yours upon my pate,","Some of my mistress' marks upon my shoulders,","But not a thousand marks between you both.","If I should pay your worship those again,","Perchance you will not bear them patiently."],"id":39,"m":"I have some marks from you on my head and some marks from my mistress on my shoulders, but not a thousand marks between you. If I paid them back to you, maybe you wouldn't take it patiently."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Thy mistress' marks? what mistress, slave, hast thou?"],"id":40,"m":"Your mistress's marks? What mistress have you got, slave?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Your worship's wife, my mistress at the Phoenix;","She that doth fast till you come home to dinner,","And prays that you will hie you home to dinner."],"id":41,"m":"Your worship's wife, my mistress at the Phoenix: the one who is fasting till you come home to dinner and begs you to hurry home to dinner."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["What, wilt thou flout me thus unto my face,","Being forbid? There, take you that, sir knave."],"id":42,"m":"What, will you mock me to my face like this, when I've told you not to? There, take that, you rogue."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["What mean you, sir? for God's sake hold your hands.","Nay, an you will not, sir, I'll take my heels."],"id":43,"m":"What are you doing, sir? For God's sake, keep your hands off! No? If you won't, I'm off."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Dromio.","id":44},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Upon my life, by some device or other","The villain is o'er-raught of all my money.","They say this town is full of cozenage,","As nimble jugglers that deceive the eye,","Dark-working sorcerers that change the mind,","Soul-killing witches that deform the body,","Disguised cheaters, prating mountebanks,","And many such-like liberties of sin:","If it prove so, I will be gone the sooner.","I'll to the Centaur to go seek this slave.","I greatly fear my money is not safe."],"id":45,"m":"Upon my life, by some trick or other the rascal has been cheated of all my money. They say this town is full of trickery: quick-handed conjurors who fool the eye, sorcerers working in the dark who change people's minds, soul-destroying witches who deform the body, cheats in disguise, chattering quacks, and many other kinds of licensed sin. If that's what's happened, I'll leave all the sooner. I'll go to the Centaur to look for this slave. I'm very afraid my money isn't safe."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":46},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"The house of Antipholus of Ephesus.","id":47},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Adriana, wife to Antipholus (of Ephesus) with Luciana her sister.","id":48},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Neither my husband nor the slave return'd","That in such haste I sent to seek his master?","Sure, Luciana, it is two o'clock."],"id":49,"m":"Hasn't my husband come back, or the slave I sent in such a hurry to find his master? Surely, Luciana, it's two o'clock."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Perhaps some merchant hath invited him,","And from the mart he's somewhere gone to dinner.","Good sister, let us dine, and never fret;","A man is master of his liberty;","Time is their master, and when they see time,","They'll go or come. If so, be patient, sister."],"id":50,"m":"Perhaps some merchant has invited him, and he's gone from the market to dinner somewhere. Dear sister, let's eat and stop fretting. A man is master of his own freedom. Only time is their master, and they come and go as they see fit. So be patient, sister."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Why should their liberty than ours be more?"],"id":51,"m":"Why should they have more freedom than we do?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Because their business still lies out o' door."],"id":52,"m":"Because their business always lies outside the house."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Look when I serve him so, he takes it ill."],"id":53,"m":"But when I treat him like that, he takes it badly."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["O, know he is the bridle of your will."],"id":54,"m":"Oh, you should know that he is the bridle on your will."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["There's none but asses will be bridled so."],"id":55,"m":"Only donkeys let themselves be bridled like that."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Why, headstrong liberty is lash'd with woe.","There's nothing situate under heaven's eye","But hath his bound in earth, in sea, in sky.","The beasts, the fishes, and the winged fowls","Are their males' subjects, and at their controls.","Man, more divine, the masters of all these,","Lord of the wide world and wild wat'ry seas,","Indued with intellectual sense and souls,","Of more pre-eminence than fish and fowls,","Are masters to their females, and their lords:","Then let your will attend on their accords."],"id":56,"m":"Why, headstrong freedom is whipped with sorrow. There is nothing under heaven that doesn't have its limits, on earth, in the sea or in the sky. The animals, the fish and the birds are subject to their males and under their control. Men, who are closer to God and masters of all these, lords of the wide world and the wild seas, gifted with reason and souls and ranked above fish and birds, are masters of their women and their lords. So let your will wait on theirs."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["This servitude makes you to keep unwed."],"id":57,"m":"This idea of servitude is what keeps you unmarried."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Not this, but troubles of the marriage-bed."],"id":58,"m":"Not that, but the troubles of the marriage bed."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["But, were you wedded, you would bear some sway."],"id":59,"m":"But if you were married, you'd want some power."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Ere I learn love, I'll practise to obey."],"id":60,"m":"Before I learn to love, I'll practise obeying."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["How if your husband start some other where?"],"id":61,"m":"What if your husband went off somewhere else?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Till he come home again, I would forbear."],"id":62,"m":"I'd put up with it till he came home again."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Patience unmov'd! No marvel though she pause;","They can be meek that have no other cause.","A wretched soul bruis'd with adversity,","We bid be quiet when we hear it cry;","But were we burd'ned with like weight of pain,","As much, or more, we should ourselves complain:","So thou, that hast no unkind mate to grieve thee,","With urging helpless patience would relieve me:","But if thou live to see like right bereft,","This fool-begg'd patience in thee will be left."],"id":63,"m":"Patience that has never been tested! No wonder she can be calm: people find it easy to be meek when they have no cause not to be. When we hear a poor soul bruised by hardship cry out, we tell it to be quiet; but if we carried the same weight of pain, we would complain as much, or more. So you, with no unkind husband to hurt you, would comfort me by urging a patience that doesn't help. But if you live to see yourself wronged the same way, this foolish patience of yours will be dropped."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Well, I will marry one day, but to try.","Here comes your man, now is your husband nigh."],"id":64,"m":"Well, I'll marry one day, just to find out. Here comes your servant. Your husband must be near."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Dromio of Ephesus.","id":65},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Say, is your tardy master now at hand?"],"id":66,"m":"Tell me, is your late master on his way?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Nay, he's at two hands with me, and that my two ears can witness."],"id":67,"m":"No, he's been at war with me, at two hands, and both my ears can witness it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Say, didst thou speak with him? know'st thou his mind?"],"id":68,"m":"Tell me, did you speak to him? Do you know what he intends?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Ay, ay, he told his mind upon mine ear.","Beshrew his hand, I scarce could understand it."],"id":69,"m":"Yes, yes, he told me his mind on my ear. Curse his hand, I could hardly understand it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Spake he so doubtfully thou couldst not feel his meaning?"],"id":70,"m":"Did he speak so unclearly you couldn't grasp his meaning?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Nay, he struck so plainly I could too well feel his blows; and withal","so doubtfully that I could scarce understand them."],"id":71,"m":"No, he hit so plainly I could feel his blows all too well, and yet so confusingly that I could hardly make sense of them."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["But say, I prithee, is he coming home?","It seems he hath great care to please his wife."],"id":72,"m":"But tell me, please, is he coming home? He seems to care a great deal about pleasing his wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Why, mistress, sure my master is horn-mad."],"id":73,"m":"Why, mistress, my master is certainly horn-mad."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Horn-mad, thou villain?"],"id":74,"m":"Horn-mad, you rascal?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["I mean not cuckold-mad,","But sure he's stark mad.","When I desir'd him to come home to dinner,","He ask'd me for a thousand marks in gold.","\"'Tis dinner time,\" quoth I. \"My gold,\" quoth he.","\"Your meat doth burn\" quoth I. \"My gold,\" quoth he.","\"Will you come home?\" quoth I. \"My gold,\" quoth he.","\"Where is the thousand marks I gave thee, villain?\"","\"The pig\" quoth I \"is burn'd\". \"My gold,\" quoth he.","\"My mistress, sir,\" quoth I. \"Hang up thy mistress;","I know not thy mistress; out on thy mistress!\""],"id":75,"m":"I don't mean mad like a man with a cheating wife, but he's certainly stark mad. When I asked him to come home to dinner, he asked me for a thousand marks in gold. \"It's dinnertime,\" I said. \"My gold,\" he said. \"Your meat is burning,\" I said. \"My gold,\" he said. \"Will you come home?\" I said. \"My gold,\" he said. \"Where are the thousand marks I gave you, villain?\" \"The pig is burnt,\" I said. \"My gold,\" he said. \"My mistress, sir,\" I said. \"Hang your mistress! I don't know your mistress. To hell with your mistress!\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Quoth who?"],"id":76,"m":"Who said that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Quoth my master.","\"I know,\" quoth he, \"no house, no wife, no mistress.\"","So that my errand, due unto my tongue,","I thank him, I bare home upon my shoulders;","For, in conclusion, he did beat me there."],"id":77,"m":"My master said it. \"I know of no house, no wife, no mistress,\" he said. So the message that should have been carried home by my tongue, I carried home on my shoulders, thanks to him, because in the end he beat me there."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Go back again, thou slave, and fetch him home."],"id":78,"m":"Go back again, you slave, and bring him home."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Go back again, and be new beaten home?","For God's sake, send some other messenger."],"id":79,"m":"Go back again and be beaten home all over again? For God's sake, send some other messenger."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Back slave, or I will break thy pate across."],"id":80,"m":"Go back, slave, or I'll crack your head across."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["And he will bless that cross with other beating.","Between you I shall have a holy head."],"id":81,"m":"And he'll bless that cross with more beating. Between the two of you I'll have a holy head."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Hence, prating peasant. Fetch thy master home."],"id":82,"m":"Get out, you babbling peasant! Bring your master home."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Am I so round with you, as you with me,","That like a football you do spurn me thus?","You spurn me hence, and he will spurn me hither.","If I last in this service, you must case me in leather."],"id":83,"m":"Am I so round with you, as you are with me, that you kick me about like a football? You kick me there and he kicks me back here. If I'm to last in this job, you'll have to stitch me up in leather like a ball."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":84},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Fie, how impatience loureth in your face."],"id":85,"m":"Shame, how impatience scowls in your face!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["His company must do his minions grace,","Whilst I at home starve for a merry look.","Hath homely age th' alluring beauty took","From my poor cheek? then he hath wasted it.","Are my discourses dull? barren my wit?","If voluble and sharp discourse be marr'd,","Unkindness blunts it more than marble hard.","Do their gay vestments his affections bait?","That's not my fault; he's master of my state.","What ruins are in me that can be found","By him not ruin'd? Then is he the ground","Of my defeatures. My decayed fair","A sunny look of his would soon repair;","But, too unruly deer, he breaks the pale","And feeds from home; poor I am but his stale."],"id":86,"m":"His girlfriends must be enjoying his company, while I'm at home starving for one kind look from him. Has plain old age taken the attractive beauty from my poor cheek? Then he's the one who wasted it. Is my conversation dull, my wit barren? If my lively, sharp talk is spoilt, his unkindness has blunted it more than hard marble would. Do other women's bright clothes lure his affections? That isn't my fault: he controls my money. What ruins can be found in me that he hasn't ruined? So he is the cause of my lost looks. One sunny look from him would soon restore my faded beauty. But like a deer that won't be fenced in, he breaks out and feeds away from home, and poor I am only his laughing-stock."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Self-harming jealousy! fie, beat it hence."],"id":87,"m":"Jealousy that only hurts yourself! For shame, drive it away."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Unfeeling fools can with such wrongs dispense.","I know his eye doth homage otherwhere,","Or else what lets it but he would be here?","Sister, you know he promis'd me a chain;","Would that alone, a love he would detain,","So he would keep fair quarter with his bed.","I see the jewel best enamelled","Will lose his beauty; yet the gold bides still","That others touch, yet often touching will","Wear gold; and no man that hath a name","By falsehood and corruption doth it shame.","Since that my beauty cannot please his eye,","I'll weep what's left away, and weeping die."],"id":88,"m":"Only unfeeling fools could put up with such wrongs. I know his eye pays its respects somewhere else, or else what keeps him from being here? Sister, you know he promised me a chain. I'd give up that, the one gift he's holding back, if only he'd stay faithful to his marriage bed. I see that even the best enamelled jewel loses its beauty. The gold still lasts, though others handle it, but frequent handling will wear even gold away, and so any man with a good name is shamed by falsehood and corruption. Since my beauty can't please his eye, I'll weep away what's left of it, and die weeping."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["How many fond fools serve mad jealousy!"],"id":89,"m":"How many foolish people make themselves slaves to mad jealousy!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":90},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"A public place.","id":91},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Antipholus of Syracuse.","id":92},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["The gold I gave to Dromio is laid up","Safe at the Centaur, and the heedful slave","Is wander'd forth in care to seek me out.","By computation and mine host's report.","I could not speak with Dromio since at first","I sent him from the mart. See, here he comes."],"id":93,"m":"The gold I gave Dromio is safely locked up at the Centaur, and the careful slave has wandered off to look for me. By my reckoning and the innkeeper's account, I couldn't have spoken to Dromio since I first sent him from the market. Look, here he comes."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Dromio of Syracuse.","id":94},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","cont":1,"lines":["How now, sir! is your merry humour alter'd?","As you love strokes, so jest with me again.","You know no Centaur? you receiv'd no gold?","Your mistress sent to have me home to dinner?","My house was at the Phoenix? Wast thou mad,","That thus so madly thou didst answer me?"],"id":95,"m":"Well, sir! Has your jolly mood changed? If you like being hit, joke with me again. You don't know the Centaur? You got no gold? Your mistress sent you to bring me home to dinner? My house was at the Phoenix? Were you mad, to answer me so madly?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["What answer, sir? when spake I such a word?"],"id":96,"m":"What answer, sir? When did I say anything like that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Even now, even here, not half an hour since."],"id":97,"m":"Just now, right here, not half an hour ago."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I did not see you since you sent me hence,","Home to the Centaur with the gold you gave me."],"id":98,"m":"I haven't seen you since you sent me off to the Centaur with the gold you gave me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Villain, thou didst deny the gold's receipt,","And told'st me of a mistress and a dinner,","For which I hope thou felt'st I was displeas'd."],"id":99,"m":"Villain, you denied getting the gold and told me about a mistress and a dinner, and I hope you felt how annoyed I was about it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I am glad to see you in this merry vein.","What means this jest, I pray you, master, tell me?"],"id":100,"m":"I'm glad to see you in such a merry mood. What is this joke? Please tell me, master."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Yea, dost thou jeer and flout me in the teeth?","Think'st thou I jest? Hold, take thou that, and that."],"id":101,"m":"What, are you mocking me to my face? Do you think I'm joking? There, take that, and that."},{"t":"sd","text":"Beats Dromio.","id":102},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Hold, sir, for God's sake, now your jest is earnest.","Upon what bargain do you give it me?"],"id":103,"m":"Stop, sir, for God's sake! Now your joke's turned serious. What deal are you giving me this for?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Because that I familiarly sometimes","Do use you for my fool, and chat with you,","Your sauciness will jest upon my love,","And make a common of my serious hours.","When the sun shines let foolish gnats make sport,","But creep in crannies when he hides his beams.","If you will jest with me, know my aspect,","And fashion your demeanour to my looks,","Or I will beat this method in your sconce."],"id":104,"m":"Because I sometimes treat you like a friend, use you as my fool and chat with you, your cheekiness takes advantage of my kindness and treats my serious moments as fair game. When the sun shines, foolish gnats can play, but they should creep into cracks when it hides its rays. If you want to joke with me, read my face and match your behaviour to my looks, or I'll beat that lesson into your sconce."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Sconce, call you it? so you would leave battering, I had rather have it","a head. And you use these blows long, I must get a sconce for my head,","and ensconce it too, or else I shall seek my wit in my shoulders. But I","pray, sir, why am I beaten?"],"id":105,"m":"Sconce, do you call it? If you'd stop battering it, I'd rather it was a head. If you keep up these blows much longer, I'll have to get a fort for my head and ensconce it too, or I'll be looking for my brains in my shoulders. But please, sir, why am I being beaten?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Dost thou not know?"],"id":106,"m":"Don't you know?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Nothing, sir, but that I am beaten."],"id":107,"m":"Nothing, sir, except that I'm beaten."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Shall I tell you why?"],"id":108,"m":"Shall I tell you why?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Ay, sir, and wherefore; for they say, every why hath a wherefore."],"id":109,"m":"Yes, sir, and wherefore, for they say every why has a wherefore."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Why, first, for flouting me; and then wherefore,","For urging it the second time to me."],"id":110,"m":"Why, first, for mocking me; and then wherefore, for doing it a second time."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Was there ever any man thus beaten out of season,","When in the why and the wherefore is neither rhyme nor reason?","Well, sir, I thank you."],"id":111,"m":"Was any man ever beaten so out of season, when there's neither rhyme nor reason in the why and the wherefore? Well, sir, thank you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Thank me, sir, for what?"],"id":112,"m":"Thank me for what, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Marry, sir, for this something that you gave me for nothing."],"id":113,"m":"Why, sir, for this something you gave me for nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I'll make you amends next, to give you nothing for something.","But say, sir, is it dinner-time?"],"id":114,"m":"Next time I'll make it up to you by giving you nothing for something. But tell me, sir, is it dinnertime?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["No, sir; I think the meat wants that I have."],"id":115,"m":"No, sir. I think the meat lacks something I've had."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["In good time, sir, what's that?"],"id":116,"m":"Indeed, sir? What's that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Basting."],"id":117,"m":"Basting."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Well, sir, then 'twill be dry."],"id":118,"m":"Well, sir, then it will be dry."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["If it be, sir, I pray you eat none of it."],"id":119,"m":"If it is, sir, please don't eat any of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Your reason?"],"id":120,"m":"Your reason?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Lest it make you choleric, and purchase me another dry basting."],"id":121,"m":"In case it makes you angry and earns me another dry basting."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Well, sir, learn to jest in good time.","There's a time for all things."],"id":122,"m":"Well, sir, learn to joke at the right time. There's a time for everything."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I durst have denied that before you were so choleric."],"id":123,"m":"I'd have dared to deny that before you got so angry."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["By what rule, sir?"],"id":124,"m":"On what grounds, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Marry, sir, by a rule as plain as the plain bald pate of Father Time","himself."],"id":125,"m":"Why, sir, on grounds as plain as the plain bald head of Father Time himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Let's hear it."],"id":126,"m":"Let's hear it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["There's no time for a man to recover his hair that grows bald by","nature."],"id":127,"m":"There's no time for a man who goes bald naturally to get his hair back."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["May he not do it by fine and recovery?"],"id":128,"m":"Can't he get it back by a legal fine and recovery?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Yes, to pay a fine for a periwig, and recover the lost hair of another","man."],"id":129,"m":"Yes, by paying a fee for a wig and recovering the lost hair of another man."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Why is Time such a niggard of hair, being, as it is, so plentiful an","excrement?"],"id":130,"m":"Why is Time so stingy with hair, when it's something the body grows so plentifully?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Because it is a blessing that he bestows on beasts, and what he hath","scanted men in hair he hath given them in wit."],"id":131,"m":"Because it's a blessing he gives to animals, and what he's skimped on giving men in hair he's given them in brains."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Why, but there's many a man hath more hair than wit."],"id":132,"m":"Why, but there's many a man with more hair than brains."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Not a man of those but he hath the wit to lose his hair."],"id":133,"m":"Every one of them has the brains to lose his hair."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Why, thou didst conclude hairy men plain dealers without wit."],"id":134,"m":"Why, you've just concluded that hairy men are simple fellows without brains."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["The plainer dealer, the sooner lost. Yet he loseth it in a kind of","jollity."],"id":135,"m":"The simpler the fellow, the sooner he loses it. Still, he loses it while having a good time."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["For what reason?"],"id":136,"m":"For what reason?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["For two, and sound ones too."],"id":137,"m":"For two, and sound ones too."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Nay, not sound, I pray you."],"id":138,"m":"No, not sound ones, please."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Sure ones, then."],"id":139,"m":"Sure ones, then."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Nay, not sure, in a thing falsing."],"id":140,"m":"No, not sure, in something so unreliable."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Certain ones, then."],"id":141,"m":"Certain ones, then."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Name them."],"id":142,"m":"Name them."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["The one, to save the money that he spends in tiring; the other, that at","dinner they should not drop in his porridge."],"id":143,"m":"One, to save the money he spends on hairdressing; the other, so that it doesn't drop into his soup at dinner."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["You would all this time have proved there is no time for all things."],"id":144,"m":"All this time you were trying to prove there isn't a time for everything."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Marry, and did, sir; namely, e'en no time to recover hair lost by","nature."],"id":145,"m":"Yes, and I did, sir: namely, no time to recover hair lost naturally."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["But your reason was not substantial why there is no time to recover."],"id":146,"m":"But you didn't give a solid reason why there's no time to recover it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Thus I mend it: Time himself is bald, and therefore, to the world's end","will have bald followers."],"id":147,"m":"I'll put it right like this: Time himself is bald, so until the end of the world he'll have bald followers."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I knew 'twould be a bald conclusion.","But soft! who wafts us yonder?"],"id":148,"m":"I knew it would be a bald conclusion. But wait! Who's that waving to us over there?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Adriana and Luciana.","id":149},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Ay, ay, Antipholus, look strange and frown,","Some other mistress hath thy sweet aspects.","I am not Adriana, nor thy wife.","The time was once when thou unurg'd wouldst vow","That never words were music to thine ear,","That never object pleasing in thine eye,","That never touch well welcome to thy hand,","That never meat sweet-savour'd in thy taste,","Unless I spake, or look'd, or touch'd, or carv'd to thee.","How comes it now, my husband, O, how comes it,","That thou art then estranged from thyself?","Thyself I call it, being strange to me,","That, undividable, incorporate,","Am better than thy dear self's better part.","Ah, do not tear away thyself from me;","For know, my love, as easy mayst thou fall","A drop of water in the breaking gulf,","And take unmingled thence that drop again","Without addition or diminishing,","As take from me thyself, and not me too.","How dearly would it touch thee to the quick,","Should'st thou but hear I were licentious?","And that this body, consecrate to thee,","By ruffian lust should be contaminate?","Wouldst thou not spit at me, and spurn at me,","And hurl the name of husband in my face,","And tear the stain'd skin off my harlot brow,","And from my false hand cut the wedding-ring,","And break it with a deep-divorcing vow?","I know thou canst; and therefore, see thou do it.","I am possess'd with an adulterate blot;","My blood is mingled with the crime of lust;","For if we two be one, and thou play false,","I do digest the poison of thy flesh,","Being strumpeted by thy contagion.","Keep then fair league and truce with thy true bed,","I live distain'd, thou undishonoured."],"id":150,"m":"Yes, yes, Antipholus, look like a stranger and frown. Some other mistress gets your sweet looks. I'm not Adriana, or your wife. There was a time when you'd swear without being asked that no words were music to your ear, no sight pleasing to your eye, no touch welcome to your hand, no food tasty to you, unless I spoke, or looked, or touched, or carved it for you. How is it now, my husband, oh, how is it that you're so estranged from yourself? I call it your self, since you're estranged from me, and I, who am inseparable from you, one body with you, am better than the better part of your own dear self. Ah, don't tear yourself away from me. Know this, my love: you might as easily let a drop of water fall into the breaking sea and take that same drop out again, unmixed, with nothing added or lost, as take yourself away from me without taking me too. How deeply it would wound you if you heard I was unfaithful, and that this body, dedicated to you, had been defiled by some thug's lust! Wouldn't you spit at me and kick me, throw the word \"husband\" in my face, tear the stained skin off my whore's forehead, cut the wedding ring off my false hand and break it with a vow of divorce? I know you could; so see that you do it. I am stained with adultery; my blood is mixed with the crime of lust. For if we two are one, and you are unfaithful, then I take in the poison of your flesh and am made a whore by your infection. So keep faith and peace with your true marriage bed; then I live unstained, and you undishonoured."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Plead you to me, fair dame? I know you not.","In Ephesus I am but two hours old,","As strange unto your town as to your talk,","Who, every word by all my wit being scann'd,","Wants wit in all one word to understand."],"id":151,"m":"Are you pleading with me, lady? I don't know you. I've only been in Ephesus two hours, and I'm as much a stranger to your town as to your talk. I've examined every word with all my wits, and I can't understand a single one."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Fie, brother, how the world is chang'd with you.","When were you wont to use my sister thus?","She sent for you by Dromio home to dinner."],"id":152,"m":"Shame, brother, how you've changed! When did you ever treat my sister like this? She sent Dromio to bring you home to dinner."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["By Dromio?"],"id":153,"m":"Dromio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["By me?"],"id":154,"m":"Me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["By thee; and this thou didst return from him,","That he did buffet thee, and in his blows","Denied my house for his, me for his wife."],"id":155,"m":"You. And this is the answer you brought back from him: that he hit you, and with his blows denied my house was his and I was his wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Did you converse, sir, with this gentlewoman?","What is the course and drift of your compact?"],"id":156,"m":"Have you talked with this lady, sir? What's the point and purpose of your plot?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I, sir? I never saw her till this time."],"id":157,"m":"Me, sir? I never saw her before now."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Villain, thou liest, for even her very words","Didst thou deliver to me on the mart."],"id":158,"m":"Villain, you're lying. You gave me her very words at the market."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I never spake with her in all my life."],"id":159,"m":"I've never spoken to her in all my life."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["How can she thus, then, call us by our names?","Unless it be by inspiration."],"id":160,"m":"Then how can she call us by our names, unless by divine inspiration?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["How ill agrees it with your gravity","To counterfeit thus grossly with your slave,","Abetting him to thwart me in my mood;","Be it my wrong, you are from me exempt,","But wrong not that wrong with a more contempt.","Come, I will fasten on this sleeve of thine.","Thou art an elm, my husband, I a vine,","Whose weakness, married to thy stronger state,","Makes me with thy strength to communicate:","If aught possess thee from me, it is dross,","Usurping ivy, brier, or idle moss,","Who all, for want of pruning, with intrusion","Infect thy sap, and live on thy confusion."],"id":161,"m":"How badly it suits your dignity to put on such a crude act with your slave, egging him on to provoke me when I'm upset! It's wrong enough that you stay away from me; don't add insult to that injury. Come, I'll hold on to your sleeve. You are an elm, my husband, and I a vine: married to your strength, my weakness shares in it. If anything takes you from me, it's rubbish: parasitic ivy, briars or useless moss that, for lack of pruning, creep in, poison your sap and live off your ruin."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["To me she speaks; she moves me for her theme.","What, was I married to her in my dream?","Or sleep I now, and think I hear all this?","What error drives our eyes and ears amiss?","Until I know this sure uncertainty","I'll entertain the offer'd fallacy."],"id":162,"m":"She's talking to me; I'm her subject. What, did I marry her in a dream? Or am I asleep now and only think I hear all this? What error is leading our eyes and ears astray? Until I'm sure what this uncertainty is, I'll go along with the illusion on offer."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Dromio, go bid the servants spread for dinner."],"id":163,"m":"Dromio, go and tell the servants to lay the table for dinner."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["O, for my beads! I cross me for a sinner.","This is the fairy land; O spite of spites!","We talk with goblins, owls, and sprites;","If we obey them not, this will ensue:","They'll suck our breath, or pinch us black and blue."],"id":164,"m":"Oh, for my rosary! I cross myself, sinner that I am. This is fairyland. Oh, worst of all! We're talking to goblins, owls and spirits. If we don't obey them, this is what will happen: they'll suck the breath out of us, or pinch us black and blue."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Why prat'st thou to thyself, and answer'st not?","Dromio, thou drone, thou snail, thou slug, thou sot."],"id":165,"m":"Why are you muttering to yourself and not answering? Dromio, you drone, you snail, you slug, you idiot!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I am transformed, master, am I not?"],"id":166,"m":"I've been transformed, master, haven't I?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I think thou art in mind, and so am I."],"id":167,"m":"I think you have in your mind, and so have I."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Nay, master, both in mind and in my shape."],"id":168,"m":"No, master, in my mind and in my body."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Thou hast thine own form."],"id":169,"m":"You still have your own shape."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["No, I am an ape."],"id":170,"m":"No, I'm an ape."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["If thou art chang'd to aught, 'tis to an ass."],"id":171,"m":"If you've been changed into anything, it's a donkey."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["'Tis true; she rides me, and I long for grass.","'Tis so, I am an ass; else it could never be","But I should know her as well as she knows me."],"id":172,"m":"That's true: she rides me and I long for grass. That's it, I'm a donkey; otherwise I would surely know her as well as she knows me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Come, come, no longer will I be a fool,","To put the finger in the eye and weep","Whilst man and master laughs my woes to scorn.","Come, sir, to dinner; Dromio, keep the gate.","Husband, I'll dine above with you today,","And shrive you of a thousand idle pranks.","Sirrah, if any ask you for your master,","Say he dines forth, and let no creature enter.","Come, sister; Dromio, play the porter well."],"id":173,"m":"Come, come, I won't be a fool any longer, rubbing my eyes and crying while servant and master laugh at my sorrows. Come, sir, to dinner. Dromio, guard the gate. Husband, I'll dine upstairs with you today and make you confess a thousand silly tricks. Fellow, if anyone asks for your master, say he's dining out, and let nobody in. Come, sister. Dromio, be a good porter."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Am I in earth, in heaven, or in hell?","Sleeping or waking, mad, or well-advis'd?","Known unto these, and to myself disguis'd!","I'll say as they say, and persever so,","And in this mist at all adventures go."],"id":174,"m":"Am I on earth, in heaven or in hell? Asleep or awake, mad or in my right mind? Known to these people, and a mystery to myself! I'll say what they say and stick to it, and go wherever this fog takes me."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Master, shall I be porter at the gate?"],"id":175,"m":"Master, shall I be porter at the gate?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Ay; and let none enter, lest I break your pate."],"id":176,"m":"Yes, and let no one in, or I'll break your head."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Come, come, Antipholus, we dine too late."],"id":177,"m":"Come on, Antipholus, we're late for dinner."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":178},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"Before the house of Antipholus of Ephesus.","id":179},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Antipholus of Ephesus, his man Dromio of Ephesus, Angelo the goldsmith and Balthasar the merchant.","id":180},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Good Signior Angelo, you must excuse us all,","My wife is shrewish when I keep not hours.","Say that I linger'd with you at your shop","To see the making of her carcanet,","And that tomorrow you will bring it home.","But here's a villain that would face me down.","He met me on the mart, and that I beat him,","And charg'd him with a thousand marks in gold,","And that I did deny my wife and house.","Thou drunkard, thou, what didst thou mean by this?"],"id":181,"m":"Good Signor Angelo, you must make excuses for all of us. My wife gets sharp-tongued when I'm late. Say I stayed with you at your shop to watch her necklace being made, and that you'll bring it to the house tomorrow. But here's a rascal who'd insist to my face that he met me at the market, that I beat him and demanded a thousand marks in gold from him, and that I denied having a wife and a house. You drunkard, what did you mean by this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Say what you will, sir, but I know what I know.","That you beat me at the mart I have your hand to show;","If the skin were parchment, and the blows you gave were ink,","Your own handwriting would tell you what I think."],"id":182,"m":"Say what you like, sir, I know what I know. That you beat me at the market I've got your handwriting to prove. If my skin were parchment and your blows were ink, your own handwriting would tell you what I think."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["I think thou art an ass."],"id":183,"m":"I think you're a donkey."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Marry, so it doth appear","By the wrongs I suffer and the blows I bear.","I should kick, being kick'd; and being at that pass,","You would keep from my heels, and beware of an ass."],"id":184,"m":"Yes, it looks that way, from the wrongs I suffer and the blows I take. Being kicked, I ought to kick back, and if I did, you'd keep away from my heels and beware of a donkey."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["You're sad, Signior Balthasar; pray God our cheer","May answer my good will and your good welcome here."],"id":185,"m":"You're very serious, Signor Balthasar. I pray to God our food lives up to my goodwill and your welcome here."},{"t":"sp","who":"BALTHASAR","lines":["I hold your dainties cheap, sir, and your welcome dear."],"id":186,"m":"I value your food lightly, sir, and your welcome highly."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["O, Signior Balthasar, either at flesh or fish","A table full of welcome makes scarce one dainty dish."],"id":187,"m":"Oh, Signor Balthasar, whether it's meat or fish, a whole table of welcome hardly makes one tasty dish."},{"t":"sp","who":"BALTHASAR","lines":["Good meat, sir, is common; that every churl affords."],"id":188,"m":"Good food is common, sir; any peasant can provide it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["And welcome more common, for that's nothing but words."],"id":189,"m":"And a welcome is more common, because that's nothing but words."},{"t":"sp","who":"BALTHASAR","lines":["Small cheer and great welcome makes a merry feast."],"id":190,"m":"Plain food and a warm welcome make a happy feast."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Ay, to a niggardly host and more sparing guest.","But though my cates be mean, take them in good part;","Better cheer may you have, but not with better heart.","But soft; my door is lock'd. Go bid them let us in."],"id":191,"m":"Yes, for a stingy host and a guest who eats even less. But though my food is plain, take it in good spirit. You might get better food elsewhere, but not served more warmly. But wait, my door's locked. Go and tell them to let us in."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Maud, Bridget, Marian, Cicely, Gillian, Ginn!"],"id":192,"m":"Maud, Bridget, Marian, Cicely, Gillian, Ginn!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["[Within] Mome, malt-horse, capon, coxcomb, idiot, patch!","Either get thee from the door or sit down at the hatch:","Dost thou conjure for wenches, that thou call'st for such store","When one is one too many? Go, get thee from the door."],"id":193,"m":"Dope, carthorse, capon, coxcomb, idiot, fool! Either get away from the door or sit down at the half-door. Are you casting spells for girls, calling for so many, when one is one too many? Go on, get away from the door."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["What patch is made our porter? My master stays in the street."],"id":194,"m":"What fool has been made our porter? My master's waiting in the street."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Let him walk from whence he came, lest he catch cold on's feet."],"id":195,"m":"Let him walk back where he came from, or he'll catch cold in his feet."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Who talks within there? Ho, open the door."],"id":196,"m":"Who's that talking inside? Hey, open the door!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Right, sir, I'll tell you when an you'll tell me wherefore."],"id":197,"m":"All right, sir, I'll tell you when, if you'll tell me why."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Wherefore? For my dinner. I have not dined today."],"id":198,"m":"Why? For my dinner. I haven't eaten today."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Nor today here you must not; come again when you may."],"id":199,"m":"And you won't today, not here. Come back when you can."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["What art thou that keep'st me out from the house I owe?"],"id":200,"m":"Who are you, keeping me out of the house I own?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["The porter for this time, sir, and my name is Dromio."],"id":201,"m":"The porter for now, sir, and my name is Dromio."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["O villain, thou hast stolen both mine office and my name;","The one ne'er got me credit, the other mickle blame.","If thou hadst been Dromio today in my place,","Thou wouldst have chang'd thy face for a name, or thy name for an ass."],"id":202,"m":"Oh, you villain, you've stolen both my job and my name! One never earned me any credit, the other plenty of blame. If you'd been Dromio in my place today, you'd have swapped your face for another name, or your name for a donkey's."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Luce concealed from Antipholus of Ephesus and his companions.","id":203},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCE","lines":["[Within] What a coil is there, Dromio, who are those at the gate?"],"id":204,"m":"What's all this racket, Dromio? Who's that at the gate?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Let my master in, Luce."],"id":205,"m":"Let my master in, Luce."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCE","lines":["Faith, no, he comes too late,","And so tell your master."],"id":206,"m":"Honestly, no. He's come too late, and you can tell your master so."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["O Lord, I must laugh;","Have at you with a proverb:—Shall I set in my staff?"],"id":207,"m":"Oh Lord, I have to laugh. Here's a proverb for you: shall I plant my staff and settle in?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCE","lines":["Have at you with another: that's—When? can you tell?"],"id":208,"m":"Here's another for you: that's \"When? Can you tell me?\""},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["If thy name be called Luce,—Luce, thou hast answer'd him well."],"id":209,"m":"If your name is Luce, then Luce, you've answered him well."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Do you hear, you minion? you'll let us in, I hope?"],"id":210,"m":"Do you hear, you hussy? You'll let us in, I hope?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCE","lines":["I thought to have ask'd you."],"id":211,"m":"I meant to ask you that."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["And you said no."],"id":212,"m":"And you said no."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["So, come, help. Well struck, there was blow for blow."],"id":213,"m":"So, come on, help. Well struck! That was blow for blow."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Thou baggage, let me in."],"id":214,"m":"You slut, let me in."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCE","lines":["Can you tell for whose sake?"],"id":215,"m":"Can you tell me for whose sake I should?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Master, knock the door hard."],"id":216,"m":"Master, knock hard on the door."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCE","lines":["Let him knock till it ache."],"id":217,"m":"Let him knock till his hand aches."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["You'll cry for this, minion, if I beat the door down."],"id":218,"m":"You'll be sorry for this, hussy, if I beat the door down."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCE","lines":["What needs all that, and a pair of stocks in the town?"],"id":219,"m":"Why do that, when there's a pair of stocks in town for you?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Adriana concealed from Antipholus of Ephesus and his companions.","id":220},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["[Within] Who is that at the door that keeps all this noise?"],"id":221,"m":"Who's that at the door making all this noise?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["By my troth, your town is troubled with unruly boys."],"id":222,"m":"Honestly, your town is plagued with rowdy boys."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Are you there, wife? you might have come before."],"id":223,"m":"Is that you, wife? You might have come sooner."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Your wife, sir knave? go, get you from the door."],"id":224,"m":"Your wife, you rogue? Go on, get away from the door."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["If you went in pain, master, this knave would go sore."],"id":225,"m":"If you went in pain, master, this rogue would go sore."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Here is neither cheer, sir, nor welcome. We would fain have either."],"id":226,"m":"There's neither food nor welcome here, sir. We'd gladly have either."},{"t":"sp","who":"BALTHASAR","lines":["In debating which was best, we shall part with neither."],"id":227,"m":"While we were arguing about which was best, we've ended up with neither."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["They stand at the door, master; bid them welcome hither."],"id":228,"m":"They're standing at the door, master. Tell them they're welcome here."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["There is something in the wind, that we cannot get in."],"id":229,"m":"Something's in the wind, that we can't get in."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["You would say so, master, if your garments were thin.","Your cake here is warm within; you stand here in the cold.","It would make a man mad as a buck to be so bought and sold."],"id":230,"m":"You'd say so, master, if your clothes were thin. Your cake's warm inside, while you stand here in the cold. It would make a man mad as a buck to be tricked and betrayed like this."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Go, fetch me something, I'll break ope the gate."],"id":231,"m":"Go and fetch me something. I'll break the gate open."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Break any breaking here, and I'll break your knave's pate."],"id":232,"m":"Break anything here, and I'll break your rogue's head."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["A man may break a word with you, sir, and words are but wind;","Ay, and break it in your face, so he break it not behind."],"id":233,"m":"A man may break a word with you, sir, since words are only wind; yes, and break it in your face, as long as he doesn't break wind behind."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["It seems thou want'st breaking; out upon thee, hind!"],"id":234,"m":"Looks like you need breaking. Get away, you peasant!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Here's too much \"out upon thee\"; I pray thee, let me in."],"id":235,"m":"There's too much \"get away\" here. Please, let me in."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Ay, when fowls have no feathers and fish have no fin."],"id":236,"m":"Yes, when birds have no feathers and fish have no fins."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Well, I'll break in; go, borrow me a crow."],"id":237,"m":"Well, I'll break in. Go and borrow me a crowbar."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["A crow without feather; master, mean you so?","For a fish without a fin, there's a fowl without a feather.","If a crow help us in, sirrah, we'll pluck a crow together."],"id":238,"m":"A crow without feathers, master, is that what you mean? For a fish without a fin, here's a bird without a feather. If a crow helps us get in, fellow, we'll have a crow to pluck together."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Go, get thee gone; fetch me an iron crow."],"id":239,"m":"Go on, get going. Fetch me an iron crowbar."},{"t":"sp","who":"BALTHASAR","lines":["Have patience, sir. O, let it not be so:","Herein you war against your reputation,","And draw within the compass of suspect","The unviolated honour of your wife.","Once this,—your long experience of her wisdom,","Her sober virtue, years, and modesty,","Plead on her part some cause to you unknown;","And doubt not, sir, but she will well excuse","Why at this time the doors are made against you.","Be rul'd by me; depart in patience,","And let us to the Tiger all to dinner,","And about evening, come yourself alone","To know the reason of this strange restraint.","If by strong hand you offer to break in","Now in the stirring passage of the day,","A vulgar comment will be made of it;","And that supposed by the common rout","Against your yet ungalled estimation","That may with foul intrusion enter in,","And dwell upon your grave when you are dead;","For slander lives upon succession,","For ever hous'd where it gets possession."],"id":240,"m":"Be patient, sir. Oh, don't do this! You'd be fighting against your own good name and bringing your wife's spotless honour under suspicion. Consider this: your long experience of her good sense, her steady virtue, her maturity and modesty suggest she has some reason you don't know about. Don't doubt, sir, that she'll explain why the doors are shut against you right now. Take my advice: leave calmly, and let's all go to the Tiger for dinner. Then towards evening come back on your own to find out the reason for this strange lockout. If you try to break in by force now, at the busiest time of day, people will gossip about it, and the common crowd will make up some story against your reputation, which is still unspoiled. That story could worm its way in and linger over your grave when you are dead. For slander passes from one generation to the next, and lives for ever wherever it takes hold."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["You have prevail'd. I will depart in quiet,","And, in despite of mirth, mean to be merry.","I know a wench of excellent discourse,","Pretty and witty; wild, and yet, too, gentle;","There will we dine. This woman that I mean,","My wife (but, I protest, without desert)","Hath oftentimes upbraided me withal;","To her will we to dinner.—Get you home","And fetch the chain, by this I know 'tis made.","Bring it, I pray you, to the Porpentine,","For there's the house. That chain will I bestow","(Be it for nothing but to spite my wife)","Upon mine hostess there. Good sir, make haste.","Since mine own doors refuse to entertain me,","I'll knock elsewhere, to see if they'll disdain me."],"id":241,"m":"You've won. I'll leave quietly and, though I'm in no mood for fun, I mean to be merry. I know a woman who's an excellent talker, pretty and witty, lively and yet gentle too. We'll eat there. My wife has often nagged me about this woman, though I swear without reason. We'll go to her for dinner. (To Angelo) Go home and fetch the chain; I know it must be made by now. Please bring it to the Porcupine, because that's the house. I'll give that chain to the hostess there, if only to spite my wife. Hurry, good sir. Since my own doors refuse to welcome me, I'll knock somewhere else and see if they'll turn me away."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["I'll meet you at that place some hour hence."],"id":242,"m":"I'll meet you there in about an hour."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Do so; this jest shall cost me some expense."],"id":243,"m":"Do. This joke will cost me something."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":244},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"Before the house of Antipholus of Ephesus.","id":245},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Luciana with Antipholus of Syracuse.","id":246},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["And may it be that you have quite forgot","A husband's office? Shall, Antipholus,","Even in the spring of love, thy love-springs rot?","Shall love, in building, grow so ruinous?","If you did wed my sister for her wealth,","Then for her wealth's sake use her with more kindness;","Or if you like elsewhere, do it by stealth,","Muffle your false love with some show of blindness.","Let not my sister read it in your eye;","Be not thy tongue thy own shame's orator;","Look sweet, speak fair, become disloyalty;","Apparel vice like virtue's harbinger;","Bear a fair presence though your heart be tainted;","Teach sin the carriage of a holy saint,","Be secret-false. What need she be acquainted?","What simple thief brags of his own attaint?","'Tis double wrong to truant with your bed","And let her read it in thy looks at board.","Shame hath a bastard fame, well managed;","Ill deeds is doubled with an evil word.","Alas, poor women, make us but believe,","Being compact of credit, that you love us.","Though others have the arm, show us the sleeve;","We in your motion turn, and you may move us.","Then, gentle brother, get you in again;","Comfort my sister, cheer her, call her wife.","'Tis holy sport to be a little vain","When the sweet breath of flattery conquers strife."],"id":247,"m":"Can it really be that you have completely forgotten a husband's duty? Antipholus, will your love's young shoots rot in the very spring of love? Will love fall into ruins while it's still being built? If you married my sister for her money, then for her money's sake treat her more kindly. Or if you love someone else, do it in secret: hide your false love behind some sort of blindfold. Don't let my sister read it in your eyes; don't let your tongue announce your own shame. Look sweet, speak kindly, make disloyalty look good; dress vice up as if it came before virtue. Look decent even if your heart is tainted. Teach sin to behave like a holy saint. Be false in secret. Why does she need to know? What foolish thief boasts about his own crime? It's doubly wrong to be unfaithful to your bed and let her read it in your face at the table. Shame, if handled well, can pass for a kind of good name; bad deeds are made twice as bad by bad words. Alas, poor women! Since we are made to trust, just let us believe you love us. Even if others get your arm, let us have the sleeve. We turn as you move; you can sway us. So, gentle brother, go back inside. Comfort my sister, cheer her up, call her your wife. It's a holy game to tell a few white lies when sweet flattery puts an end to quarrelling."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Sweet mistress, what your name is else, I know not,","Nor by what wonder you do hit on mine;","Less in your knowledge and your grace you show not","Than our earth's wonder, more than earth divine.","Teach me, dear creature, how to think and speak;","Lay open to my earthy gross conceit,","Smother'd in errors, feeble, shallow, weak,","The folded meaning of your words' deceit.","Against my soul's pure truth why labour you","To make it wander in an unknown field?","Are you a god? would you create me new?","Transform me, then, and to your power I'll yield.","But if that I am I, then well I know","Your weeping sister is no wife of mine,","Nor to her bed no homage do I owe.","Far more, far more, to you do I decline.","O, train me not, sweet mermaid, with thy note","To drown me in thy sister's flood of tears.","Sing, siren, for thyself, and I will dote;","Spread o'er the silver waves thy golden hairs,","And as a bed I'll take thee, and there lie,","And, in that glorious supposition think","He gains by death that hath such means to die.","Let love, being light, be drowned if she sink!"],"id":248,"m":"Sweet lady, I don't know what your name is, nor by what miracle you know mine. Your knowledge and grace make you nothing less than a wonder of this earth, more divine than earthly. Teach me, dear creature, how to think and speak. Explain to my dull, earthly mind, lost in errors, feeble, shallow and weak, the hidden meaning of your puzzling words. Why are you trying to make my soul's plain truth wander off into unknown territory? Are you a goddess? Would you make me into someone new? Then transform me, and I'll give in to your power. But if I am who I am, then I know very well that your weeping sister is no wife of mine, and I owe her bed no loyalty. Far more, far more, I'm drawn to you. Oh, don't lure me with your song, sweet mermaid, to drown me in your sister's flood of tears. Sing for yourself, siren, and I'll adore you. Spread your golden hair over the silver waves, and I'll take it for my bed and lie there, and in that glorious dream think that a man who can die like that gains by dying. Let love, which is light, drown if it sinks!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["What, are you mad, that you do reason so?"],"id":249,"m":"What, are you mad, to talk like this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Not mad, but mated; how, I do not know."],"id":250,"m":"Not mad, but bewildered; how, I don't know."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["It is a fault that springeth from your eye."],"id":251,"m":"The fault comes from your eye."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["For gazing on your beams, fair sun, being by."],"id":252,"m":"From gazing at your rays, lovely sun, so close by."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Gaze where you should, and that will clear your sight."],"id":253,"m":"Look where you should look, and that will clear your sight."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["As good to wink, sweet love, as look on night."],"id":254,"m":"I might as well close my eyes, sweet love, as look at night."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Why call you me love? Call my sister so."],"id":255,"m":"Why do you call me love? Call my sister that."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Thy sister's sister."],"id":256,"m":"Your sister's sister."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["That's my sister."],"id":257,"m":"That's my sister."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["No,","It is thyself, mine own self's better part,","Mine eye's clear eye, my dear heart's dearer heart,","My food, my fortune, and my sweet hope's aim,","My sole earth's heaven, and my heaven's claim."],"id":258,"m":"No, it's you: the better part of my own self, my eye's clear eye, my dear heart's dearer heart, my food, my fortune, the target of my sweet hope, my only heaven on earth and my claim on heaven."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["All this my sister is, or else should be."],"id":259,"m":"My sister is all this, or should be."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Call thyself sister, sweet, for I aim thee;","Thee will I love, and with thee lead my life;","Thou hast no husband yet, nor I no wife.","Give me thy hand."],"id":260,"m":"Call yourself sister, sweetheart, for I'm aiming at you. I'll love you and spend my life with you. You don't have a husband yet, and I don't have a wife. Give me your hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["O, soft, sir, hold you still;","I'll fetch my sister to get her goodwill."],"id":261,"m":"Oh, wait, sir, stay where you are. I'll fetch my sister to get her blessing."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Luciana.","id":262},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Dromio of Syracuse.","id":263},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Why, how now, Dromio? where runn'st thou so fast?"],"id":264,"m":"Why, what's the matter, Dromio? Where are you running so fast?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Do you know me, sir? Am I Dromio? Am I your man? Am I myself?"],"id":265,"m":"Do you know me, sir? Am I Dromio? Am I your servant? Am I myself?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Thou art Dromio, thou art my man, thou art thyself."],"id":266,"m":"You're Dromio, you're my servant, you're yourself."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I am an ass, I am a woman's man, and besides myself."],"id":267,"m":"I'm a donkey, I'm a woman's man, and I'm beside myself."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["What woman's man? and how besides thyself?"],"id":268,"m":"What woman's man? And how are you beside yourself?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Marry, sir, besides myself, I am due to a woman, one that claims me,","one that haunts me, one that will have me."],"id":269,"m":"Why, sir, besides being myself, I belong to a woman: one who claims me, one who haunts me, one who will have me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["What claim lays she to thee?"],"id":270,"m":"What claim does she have on you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Marry, sir, such claim as you would lay to your horse, and she would","have me as a beast; not that I being a beast she would have me, but","that she being a very beastly creature lays claim to me."],"id":271,"m":"Why, sir, the sort of claim you'd make on your horse. She wants me as a beast: not that she'd want me because I'm a beast, but because she's such a beastly creature she lays claim to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["What is she?"],"id":272,"m":"Who is she?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["A very reverent body; ay, such a one as a man may not speak of without","he say \"sir-reverence\". I have but lean luck in the match, and yet is","she a wondrous fat marriage."],"id":273,"m":"A very reverend body; yes, the kind of person you can't mention without saying \"excuse me\". I have lean luck in the match, and yet it's an amazingly fat marriage."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["How dost thou mean a \"fat marriage\"?"],"id":274,"m":"What do you mean, a \"fat marriage\"?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Marry, sir, she's the kitchen wench, and all grease, and I know not","what use to put her to but to make a lamp of her and run from her by","her own light. I warrant her rags and the tallow in them will burn a","Poland winter. If she lives till doomsday, she'll burn a week longer","than the whole world."],"id":275,"m":"Why, sir, she's the kitchen maid, and all grease, and I don't know what use to make of her except turn her into a lamp and run away from her by her own light. I swear her rags and the fat in them would burn through a Polish winter. If she lives till Judgement Day, she'll burn a week longer than the whole world."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["What complexion is she of?"],"id":276,"m":"What's her complexion like?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Swart like my shoe, but her face nothing like so clean kept. For why?","she sweats, a man may go overshoes in the grime of it."],"id":277,"m":"Black as my shoe, but her face isn't kept anything like as clean. Why? Because she sweats so much a man could sink in the dirt over his shoes."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["That's a fault that water will mend."],"id":278,"m":"That's a fault water can fix."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["No, sir, 'tis in grain; Noah's flood could not do it."],"id":279,"m":"No, sir, it's dyed in. Noah's flood couldn't wash it out."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["What's her name?"],"id":280,"m":"What's her name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Nell, sir; but her name and three quarters, that's an ell and three","quarters, will not measure her from hip to hip."],"id":281,"m":"Nell, sir. But her name and three quarters, that's an ell and three quarters, wouldn't measure her from hip to hip."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Then she bears some breadth?"],"id":282,"m":"So she's quite broad?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["No longer from head to foot than from hip to hip. She is spherical,","like a globe. I could find out countries in her."],"id":283,"m":"She's no longer from head to foot than from hip to hip. She's round, like a globe. I could find countries on her."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["In what part of her body stands Ireland?"],"id":284,"m":"Where on her body is Ireland?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Marry, sir, in her buttocks; I found it out by the bogs."],"id":285,"m":"Why, sir, in her buttocks: I found it by the bogs."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Where Scotland?"],"id":286,"m":"Where's Scotland?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I found it by the barrenness, hard in the palm of the hand."],"id":287,"m":"I found it by the barren ground, hard in the palm of her hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Where France?"],"id":288,"m":"Where's France?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["In her forehead; armed and reverted, making war against her hair."],"id":289,"m":"In her forehead, armed and rebelling, making war against her hair."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Where England?"],"id":290,"m":"Where's England?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I looked for the chalky cliffs, but I could find no whiteness in them.","But I guess it stood in her chin, by the salt rheum that ran between","France and it."],"id":291,"m":"I looked for the chalk cliffs, but I couldn't find anything white about her. But I guess it was in her chin, from the salty snot that ran between France and it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Where Spain?"],"id":292,"m":"Where's Spain?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Faith, I saw it not; but I felt it hot in her breath."],"id":293,"m":"Honestly, I didn't see it, but I felt it hot on her breath."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Where America, the Indies?"],"id":294,"m":"Where are America and the Indies?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["O, sir, upon her nose, all o'er-embellished with rubies, carbuncles,","sapphires, declining their rich aspect to the hot breath of Spain, who","sent whole armadoes of carracks to be ballast at her nose."],"id":295,"m":"Oh, sir, on her nose, all decorated with rubies, carbuncles and sapphires, turning their rich faces towards the hot breath of Spain, which sent whole fleets of galleons to be loaded up at her nose."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Where stood Belgia, the Netherlands?"],"id":296,"m":"Where were Belgium and the Netherlands?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["O, sir, I did not look so low. To conclude: this drudge or diviner laid","claim to me, called me Dromio, swore I was assured to her, told me what","privy marks I had about me, as the mark of my shoulder, the mole in my","neck, the great wart on my left arm, that I, amazed, ran from her as a","witch. And, I think, if my breast had not been made of faith, and my","heart of steel, she had transformed me to a curtal dog, and made me","turn i' the wheel."],"id":297,"m":"Oh, sir, I didn't look that low. To sum up: this drudge, or witch, claimed me, called me Dromio, swore I was engaged to her, told me what private marks I had on me, like the mark on my shoulder, the mole on my neck, the big wart on my left arm, so that I was amazed and ran from her as from a witch. And I think if my breast hadn't been made of faith and my heart of steel, she'd have turned me into a dog with a docked tail and made me run in the turnspit wheel."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Go, hie thee presently, post to the road;","And if the wind blow any way from shore,","I will not harbour in this town tonight.","If any bark put forth, come to the mart,","Where I will walk till thou return to me.","If everyone knows us, and we know none,","'Tis time, I think, to trudge, pack and be gone."],"id":298,"m":"Go, hurry straight to the harbour. If the wind is blowing in any direction away from the shore, I won't stay in this town tonight. If any ship is sailing, come to the market, where I'll walk about till you come back to me. If everyone knows us and we know no one, it's time, I think, to pack up and go."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["As from a bear a man would run for life,","So fly I from her that would be my wife."],"id":299,"m":"As a man would run for his life from a bear, so I fly from the woman who'd be my wife."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":300},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["There's none but witches do inhabit here,","And therefore 'tis high time that I were hence.","She that doth call me husband, even my soul","Doth for a wife abhor. But her fair sister,","Possess'd with such a gentle sovereign grace,","Of such enchanting presence and discourse,","Hath almost made me traitor to myself.","But lest myself be guilty to self-wrong,","I'll stop mine ears against the mermaid's song."],"id":301,"m":"Only witches live here, so it's high time I left. My very soul hates the woman who calls me husband, as a wife. But her lovely sister, with such gentle, commanding grace and such enchanting looks and talk, has almost made me a traitor to myself. But so that I'm not guilty of wronging myself, I'll block my ears against the mermaid's song."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Angelo with the chain.","id":302},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Master Antipholus."],"id":303,"m":"Master Antipholus."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Ay, that's my name."],"id":304,"m":"Yes, that's my name."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["I know it well, sir. Lo, here is the chain;","I thought to have ta'en you at the Porpentine,","The chain unfinish'd made me stay thus long."],"id":305,"m":"I know it well, sir. Look, here's the chain. I meant to catch you at the Porcupine, but the chain wasn't finished, and that kept me so long."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["What is your will that I shall do with this?"],"id":306,"m":"What do you want me to do with this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["What please yourself, sir; I have made it for you."],"id":307,"m":"Whatever you like, sir. I made it for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Made it for me, sir! I bespoke it not."],"id":308,"m":"Made it for me, sir! I didn't order it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Not once, nor twice, but twenty times you have.","Go home with it, and please your wife withal,","And soon at supper-time I'll visit you,","And then receive my money for the chain."],"id":309,"m":"Not once or twice but twenty times you have. Take it home and please your wife with it, and soon, at suppertime, I'll call on you and get my money for the chain."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I pray you, sir, receive the money now,","For fear you ne'er see chain nor money more."],"id":310,"m":"Please, sir, take the money now, in case you never see the chain or the money again."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["You are a merry man, sir; fare you well."],"id":311,"m":"You're a joker, sir. Goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":312},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["What I should think of this I cannot tell,","But this I think, there's no man is so vain","That would refuse so fair an offer'd chain.","I see a man here needs not live by shifts,","When in the streets he meets such golden gifts.","I'll to the mart, and there for Dromio stay;","If any ship put out, then straight away."],"id":313,"m":"I can't tell what to make of this. But I do think there's no man so foolish that he'd refuse such a fine chain when it's offered. I see a man here doesn't need to live by his wits when he meets such golden gifts in the street. I'll go to the market and wait there for Dromio. If any ship is sailing, we're off at once."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":314},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"A public place.","id":315},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Merchant, Angelo and an Officer.","id":316},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND MERCHANT","lines":["You know since Pentecost the sum is due,","And since I have not much importun'd you,","Nor now I had not, but that I am bound","To Persia, and want guilders for my voyage;","Therefore make present satisfaction,","Or I'll attach you by this officer."],"id":317,"m":"You know the sum has been due since Whitsun, and I haven't pressed you much, and I wouldn't now, except that I'm bound for Persia and need money for my voyage. So pay me now, or I'll have this officer arrest you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Even just the sum that I do owe to you","Is growing to me by Antipholus,","And in the instant that I met with you","He had of me a chain; at five o'clock","I shall receive the money for the same.","Pleaseth you walk with me down to his house,","I will discharge my bond, and thank you too."],"id":318,"m":"The exact sum I owe you is owed to me by Antipholus. Just before I met you, he had a chain from me. At five o'clock I'll get the money for it. If you'll walk with me to his house, I'll pay off my debt, and thank you too."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Antipholus of Ephesus and Dromio of Ephesus from the Courtesan's.","id":319},{"t":"sp","who":"OFFICER","lines":["That labour may you save. See where he comes."],"id":320,"m":"You can save yourself the trouble. Look, here he comes."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["While I go to the goldsmith's house, go thou","And buy a rope's end; that will I bestow","Among my wife and her confederates","For locking me out of my doors by day.","But soft, I see the goldsmith; get thee gone;","Buy thou a rope, and bring it home to me."],"id":321,"m":"While I go to the goldsmith's house, you go and buy a rope's end. I'll use it on my wife and her accomplices for locking me out of my own house in broad daylight. But wait, I see the goldsmith. Off you go, buy a rope and bring it home to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["I buy a thousand pound a year! I buy a rope!"],"id":322,"m":"I buy a thousand pounds a year! I buy a rope!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Dromio.","id":323},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["A man is well holp up that trusts to you,","I promised your presence and the chain,","But neither chain nor goldsmith came to me.","Belike you thought our love would last too long","If it were chain'd together, and therefore came not."],"id":324,"m":"A man who trusts you gets a lot of help! I promised your company and the chain, but neither the chain nor the goldsmith turned up. I suppose you thought our love would last too long if it was chained together, so you didn't come."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Saving your merry humour, here's the note","How much your chain weighs to the utmost carat,","The fineness of the gold, and chargeful fashion,","Which doth amount to three odd ducats more","Than I stand debted to this gentleman.","I pray you, see him presently discharg'd,","For he is bound to sea, and stays but for it."],"id":325,"m":"Joking aside, here's the bill: how much your chain weighs to the last carat, the fineness of the gold and the costly workmanship. It comes to three ducats or so more than I owe this gentleman. Please pay him straight away, because he's about to go to sea and is only waiting for this."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["I am not furnished with the present money;","Besides, I have some business in the town.","Good signior, take the stranger to my house,","And with you take the chain, and bid my wife","Disburse the sum on the receipt thereof;","Perchance I will be there as soon as you."],"id":326,"m":"I don't have the cash on me, and besides, I have some business in town. Good signor, take the stranger to my house, take the chain with you, and tell my wife to pay the sum when she receives it. Maybe I'll be there as soon as you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Then you will bring the chain to her yourself."],"id":327,"m":"Then you'll bring the chain to her yourself?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["No, bear it with you, lest I come not time enough."],"id":328,"m":"No, take it with you, in case I'm not there in time."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Well, sir, I will. Have you the chain about you?"],"id":329,"m":"Well, sir, I will. Do you have the chain on you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["And if I have not, sir, I hope you have,","Or else you may return without your money."],"id":330,"m":"If I haven't, sir, I hope you have, or else you may go back without your money."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Nay, come, I pray you, sir, give me the chain;","Both wind and tide stays for this gentleman,","And I, to blame, have held him here too long."],"id":331,"m":"No, come on, sir, please give me the chain. Both wind and tide are waiting for this gentleman, and it's my fault I've kept him here so long."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Good Lord, you use this dalliance to excuse","Your breach of promise to the Porpentine.","I should have chid you for not bringing it,","But, like a shrew, you first begin to brawl."],"id":332,"m":"Good Lord, you're using this nonsense to excuse breaking your promise to come to the Porcupine. I should have scolded you for not bringing it, but like a nagging wife you start the quarrel first."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND MERCHANT","lines":["The hour steals on; I pray you, sir, dispatch."],"id":333,"m":"Time's passing. Please, sir, settle this."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["You hear how he importunes me. The chain!"],"id":334,"m":"You hear how he's pressing me. The chain!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Why, give it to my wife, and fetch your money."],"id":335,"m":"Why, give it to my wife and collect your money."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Come, come, you know I gave it you even now.","Either send the chain or send by me some token."],"id":336,"m":"Come, come, you know I gave it to you just now. Either send the chain or send some token of authority with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Fie, now you run this humour out of breath.","Come, where's the chain? I pray you, let me see it."],"id":337,"m":"Oh, now you're running this joke into the ground. Come on, where's the chain? Please, let me see it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND MERCHANT","lines":["My business cannot brook this dalliance.","Good sir, say whe'er you'll answer me or no;","If not, I'll leave him to the officer."],"id":338,"m":"My business can't put up with this time-wasting. Good sir, say whether you'll pay me or not. If not, I'll hand him over to the officer."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["I answer you? What should I answer you?"],"id":339,"m":"I pay you? What should I pay you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["The money that you owe me for the chain."],"id":340,"m":"The money you owe me for the chain."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["I owe you none till I receive the chain."],"id":341,"m":"I owe you nothing till I get the chain."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["You know I gave it you half an hour since."],"id":342,"m":"You know I gave it to you half an hour ago."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["You gave me none. You wrong me much to say so."],"id":343,"m":"You gave me nothing. You do me great wrong by saying so."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["You wrong me more, sir, in denying it.","Consider how it stands upon my credit."],"id":344,"m":"You do me more wrong, sir, by denying it. Think how my credit depends on it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND MERCHANT","lines":["Well, officer, arrest him at my suit."],"id":345,"m":"Well, officer, arrest him on my claim."},{"t":"sp","who":"OFFICER","lines":["I do, and charge you in the duke's name to obey me."],"id":346,"m":"I do, and order you in the Duke's name to obey me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["This touches me in reputation.","Either consent to pay this sum for me,","Or I attach you by this officer."],"id":347,"m":"This threatens my reputation. Either agree to pay this sum for me, or I'll have this officer arrest you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Consent to pay thee that I never had?","Arrest me, foolish fellow, if thou dar'st."],"id":348,"m":"Agree to pay you for something I never had? Arrest me, you fool, if you dare."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Here is thy fee; arrest him, officer.","I would not spare my brother in this case","If he should scorn me so apparently."],"id":349,"m":"Here's your fee. Arrest him, officer. I wouldn't spare my own brother in this situation if he scorned me so openly."},{"t":"sp","who":"OFFICER","lines":["I do arrest you, sir. You hear the suit."],"id":350,"m":"I arrest you, sir. You've heard the charge."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["I do obey thee till I give thee bail.","But, sirrah, you shall buy this sport as dear","As all the metal in your shop will answer."],"id":351,"m":"I'll obey you till I can pay bail. But you, fellow, will pay as dearly for this game as all the metal in your shop can cover."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Sir, sir, I shall have law in Ephesus,","To your notorious shame, I doubt it not."],"id":352,"m":"Sir, sir, I'll have the law on my side in Ephesus, to your public shame, I don't doubt."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Dromio of Syracuse from the bay.","id":353},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Master, there's a bark of Epidamnum","That stays but till her owner comes aboard,","And then, sir, bears away. Our fraughtage, sir,","I have convey'd aboard, and I have bought","The oil, the balsamum, and aqua-vitae.","The ship is in her trim; the merry wind","Blows fair from land; they stay for nought at all","But for their owner, master, and yourself."],"id":354,"m":"Master, there's a ship from Epidamnum that's only waiting till her owner comes aboard, and then, sir, she sails. I've had our luggage taken aboard, sir, and I've bought the oil, the balsam and the brandy. The ship's ready; a cheerful wind is blowing nicely offshore. They're waiting for nothing at all except their owner, master, and you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["How now? a madman? Why, thou peevish sheep,","What ship of Epidamnum stays for me?"],"id":355,"m":"What's this? A madman? Why, you stupid sheep, what ship from Epidamnum is waiting for me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["A ship you sent me to, to hire waftage."],"id":356,"m":"The ship you sent me to, to book our passage."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Thou drunken slave, I sent thee for a rope,","And told thee to what purpose and what end."],"id":357,"m":"You drunken slave, I sent you for a rope, and told you what for and why."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["You sent me for a rope's end as soon.","You sent me to the bay, sir, for a bark."],"id":358,"m":"You'd sooner have sent me to be hanged with a rope's end. You sent me to the bay, sir, for a ship."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["I will debate this matter at more leisure,","And teach your ears to list me with more heed.","To Adriana, villain, hie thee straight:","Give her this key, and tell her in the desk","That's cover'd o'er with Turkish tapestry","There is a purse of ducats; let her send it.","Tell her I am arrested in the street,","And that shall bail me. Hie thee, slave; be gone.","On, officer, to prison till it come."],"id":359,"m":"I'll argue about this when I've more time, and teach your ears to listen to me more carefully. Go straight to Adriana, you rascal. Give her this key, and tell her that in the desk covered with the Turkish tapestry there's a purse of ducats; she must send it. Tell her I've been arrested in the street, and that will pay my bail. Hurry, slave, go! On to prison, officer, till it comes."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Merchant, Angelo, Officer and Antipholus of Ephesus.","id":360},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["To Adriana, that is where we din'd,","Where Dowsabel did claim me for her husband.","She is too big, I hope, for me to compass.","Thither I must, although against my will,","For servants must their masters' minds fulfil."],"id":361,"m":"To Adriana: that's where we had dinner, where Dowsabel claimed me as her husband. She's too big, I hope, for me to get my arms round. I have to go there, though it's against my will, because servants must carry out their masters' wishes."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":362},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"The house of Antipholus of Ephesus.","id":363},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Adriana and Luciana.","id":364},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Ah, Luciana, did he tempt thee so?","Might'st thou perceive austerely in his eye","That he did plead in earnest, yea or no?","Look'd he or red or pale, or sad or merrily?","What observation mad'st thou in this case","Of his heart's meteors tilting in his face?"],"id":365,"m":"Oh, Luciana, did he try to seduce you like that? Could you tell from his eyes, seriously, whether he was pleading in earnest or not? Did he look flushed or pale, serious or cheerful? What did you notice of his heart's passions flashing across his face like meteors?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["First he denied you had in him no right."],"id":366,"m":"First he denied that you had any right to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["He meant he did me none; the more my spite."],"id":367,"m":"He meant he did me no right; all the more spite to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Then swore he that he was a stranger here."],"id":368,"m":"Then he swore that he was a stranger here."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["And true he swore, though yet forsworn he were."],"id":369,"m":"And he swore the truth, even though he broke his vows."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Then pleaded I for you."],"id":370,"m":"Then I pleaded for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["And what said he?"],"id":371,"m":"And what did he say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["That love I begg'd for you he begg'd of me."],"id":372,"m":"That he begged me for the love I was begging for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["With what persuasion did he tempt thy love?"],"id":373,"m":"How did he try to win your love?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["With words that in an honest suit might move.","First he did praise my beauty, then my speech."],"id":374,"m":"With words that might have moved me if his suit were honest. First he praised my beauty, then my way of speaking."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Did'st speak him fair?"],"id":375,"m":"Did you speak kindly to him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Have patience, I beseech."],"id":376,"m":"Be patient, I beg you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["I cannot, nor I will not hold me still.","My tongue, though not my heart, shall have his will.","He is deformed, crooked, old, and sere,","Ill-fac'd, worse bodied, shapeless everywhere;","Vicious, ungentle, foolish, blunt, unkind,","Stigmatical in making, worse in mind."],"id":377,"m":"I can't keep quiet, and I won't. My tongue, if not my heart, shall have its way. He's deformed, crooked, old and withered, ugly in face and worse in body, misshapen everywhere; wicked, rude, foolish, blunt, unkind, marked by nature as a bad man and worse in his mind."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Who would be jealous then of such a one?","No evil lost is wail'd when it is gone."],"id":378,"m":"Then who would be jealous of someone like that? Nobody mourns the loss of something bad."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Ah, but I think him better than I say,","And yet would herein others' eyes were worse:","Far from her nest the lapwing cries away;","My heart prays for him, though my tongue do curse."],"id":379,"m":"Ah, but I think better of him than I say, and yet I wish other women's eyes thought worse of him. The lapwing cries out far from her nest to draw people away from it: my heart prays for him, though my tongue curses him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Dromio of Syracuse.","id":380},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Here, go; the desk, the purse, sweet now, make haste."],"id":381,"m":"Here, go on: the desk, the purse! Sweet lady, hurry now."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["How hast thou lost thy breath?"],"id":382,"m":"How did you get so out of breath?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["By running fast."],"id":383,"m":"By running fast."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Where is thy master, Dromio? is he well?"],"id":384,"m":"Where's your master, Dromio? Is he all right?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["No, he's in Tartar limbo, worse than hell.","A devil in an everlasting garment hath him,","One whose hard heart is button'd up with steel;","A fiend, a fairy, pitiless and rough;","A wolf, nay worse, a fellow all in buff;","A back-friend, a shoulder-clapper, one that countermands","The passages of alleys, creeks, and narrow lands;","A hound that runs counter, and yet draws dryfoot well,","One that, before the judgement, carries poor souls to hell."],"id":385,"m":"No, he's in a hellish jail, worse than hell itself. A devil in a garment that never wears out has got him, one whose hard heart is buttoned up in steel: a fiend, a goblin, pitiless and rough; a wolf, no, worse, a fellow all in leather; a false friend who taps you on the shoulder, one who blocks off alleys, creeks and narrow lanes; a hound that runs the wrong way and yet follows a scent well; one who carries poor souls to hell before their judgement."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Why, man, what is the matter?"],"id":386,"m":"Why, man, what's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I do not know the matter. He is 'rested on the case."],"id":387,"m":"I don't know the matter. He's been arrested on a case."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["What, is he arrested? Tell me at whose suit?"],"id":388,"m":"What, he's arrested? Tell me, on whose charge?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I know not at whose suit he is arrested, well;","But he's in a suit of buff which 'rested him, that can I tell.","Will you send him, mistress, redemption, the money in his desk?"],"id":389,"m":"I don't know on whose charge he's arrested, but he's been arrested by a man in a leather suit, that much I can tell you. Will you send him his redemption, mistress, the money in his desk?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Go fetch it, sister. This I wonder at,"],"id":390,"m":"Fetch it, sister. I'm amazed at this,"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Luciana.","id":391},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","cont":1,"lines":["Thus he unknown to me should be in debt.","Tell me, was he arrested on a band?"],"id":392,"m":"that he should be in debt without my knowing. Tell me, was he arrested over a bond?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Not on a band, but on a stronger thing;","A chain, a chain. Do you not hear it ring?"],"id":393,"m":"Not over a bond, but over something stronger: a chain, a chain. Can't you hear it ringing?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["What, the chain?"],"id":394,"m":"What, the chain?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["No, no, the bell, 'tis time that I were gone.","It was two ere I left him, and now the clock strikes one."],"id":395,"m":"No, no, the bell. It's time I was gone. It was two before I left him, and now the clock strikes one."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["The hours come back! That did I never hear."],"id":396,"m":"The hours going backwards! I never heard of that."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["O yes, if any hour meet a sergeant, 'a turns back for very fear."],"id":397,"m":"Oh yes, if any hour meets a sergeant, it turns back out of pure fear."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["As if time were in debt. How fondly dost thou reason!"],"id":398,"m":"As if time could be in debt! How foolishly you argue!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Time is a very bankrupt, and owes more than he's worth to season.","Nay, he's a thief too. Have you not heard men say","That time comes stealing on by night and day?","If he be in debt and theft, and a sergeant in the way,","Hath he not reason to turn back an hour in a day?"],"id":399,"m":"Time is completely bankrupt and owes more than he's worth to the occasion. No, he's a thief too. Haven't you heard people say that time comes stealing on, night and day? If he's in debt and a thief, and there's a sergeant in his way, hasn't he reason to turn back an hour in the day?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Luciana.","id":400},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Go, Dromio, there's the money, bear it straight,","And bring thy master home immediately.","Come, sister, I am press'd down with conceit;","Conceit, my comfort and my injury."],"id":401,"m":"Go, Dromio, there's the money. Take it straight to him, and bring your master home at once. Come, sister, I'm weighed down by my imagination: imagination, my comfort and my injury."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":402},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"III","loc":"A public place.","id":403},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Antipholus of Syracuse.","id":404},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["There's not a man I meet but doth salute me","As if I were their well-acquainted friend,","And everyone doth call me by my name.","Some tender money to me, some invite me;","Some other give me thanks for kindnesses;","Some offer me commodities to buy.","Even now a tailor call'd me in his shop,","And show'd me silks that he had bought for me,","And therewithal took measure of my body.","Sure, these are but imaginary wiles,","And Lapland sorcerers inhabit here."],"id":405,"m":"Every man I meet greets me as if I were a close friend, and everyone calls me by my name. Some offer me money, some invite me to their homes, others thank me for kindnesses, some offer me goods to buy. Just now a tailor called me into his shop, showed me silks he had bought for me, and took my measurements as well. Surely these are imaginary tricks, and Lapland sorcerers live here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Dromio of Syracuse.","id":406},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Master, here's the gold you sent me for.","What, have you got the picture of old Adam new apparelled?"],"id":407,"m":"Master, here's the gold you sent me for. What, have you got rid of the image of old Adam in his new clothes?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["What gold is this? What Adam dost thou mean?"],"id":408,"m":"What gold is this? What Adam do you mean?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Not that Adam that kept the paradise, but that Adam that keeps the","prison; he that goes in the calf's skin that was killed for the","Prodigal; he that came behind you, sir, like an evil angel, and bid you","forsake your liberty."],"id":409,"m":"Not the Adam who kept paradise, but the Adam who keeps the prison: the one who goes about in the skin of the calf that was killed for the Prodigal Son; the one who came up behind you, sir, like a bad angel, and told you to give up your freedom."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I understand thee not."],"id":410,"m":"I don't understand you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["No? Why, 'tis a plain case: he that went like a bass-viol in a case of","leather; the man, sir, that, when gentlemen are tired, gives them a","sob, and 'rests them; he, sir, that takes pity on decayed men and gives","them suits of durance; he that sets up his rest to do more exploits","with his mace than a morris-pike."],"id":411,"m":"No? Why, it's a plain case: the one who went about like a bass viol in a leather case; the man, sir, who, when gentlemen are tired, gives them a tap and arrests them; the one, sir, who takes pity on ruined men and gives them suits that last; the one who's determined to do more damage with his mace than a soldier with a pike."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["What! thou mean'st an officer?"],"id":412,"m":"What! You mean an officer?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Ay, sir, the sergeant of the band; he that brings any man to answer it","that breaks his band; one that thinks a man always going to bed, and","says, \"God give you good rest.\""],"id":413,"m":"Yes, sir, the sergeant of the bond: the one who makes any man who breaks his bond answer for it; one who thinks every man is going to bed, and says, \"God give you good rest.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Well, sir, there rest in your foolery. Is there any ship puts forth","tonight? may we be gone?"],"id":414,"m":"Well, sir, let your foolery rest there. Is there any ship sailing tonight? Can we leave?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Why, sir, I brought you word an hour since that the bark Expedition","put forth tonight, and then were you hindered by the sergeant to tarry","for the hoy Delay. Here are the angels that you sent for to deliver","you."],"id":415,"m":"Why, sir, I told you an hour ago that the ship Expedition was sailing tonight, and then you were held up by the sergeant and had to wait for the barge Delay. Here are the gold angels you sent for to set you free."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["The fellow is distract, and so am I,","And here we wander in illusions.","Some blessed power deliver us from hence!"],"id":416,"m":"The fellow's out of his mind, and so am I, and we're wandering here among illusions. May some holy power get us out of here!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Courtesan.","id":417},{"t":"sp","who":"COURTESAN","lines":["Well met, well met, Master Antipholus.","I see, sir, you have found the goldsmith now.","Is that the chain you promis'd me today?"],"id":418,"m":"Well met, well met, Master Antipholus. I see, sir, you've found the goldsmith at last. Is that the chain you promised me today?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Satan, avoid! I charge thee, tempt me not."],"id":419,"m":"Get behind me, Satan! I order you, don't tempt me."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Master, is this Mistress Satan?"],"id":420,"m":"Master, is this Mistress Satan?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["It is the devil."],"id":421,"m":"It's the devil."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Nay, she is worse, she is the devil's dam; and here she comes in the","habit of a light wench, and thereof comes that the wenches say \"God","damn me\", that's as much to say, \"God make me a light wench.\" It is","written they appear to men like angels of light. Light is an effect of","fire, and fire will burn; ergo, light wenches will burn. Come not near","her."],"id":422,"m":"No, she's worse: she's the devil's mother. And here she comes dressed as a light woman, which is why the girls say \"God damn me\", which is the same as saying \"God make me a light woman\". It's written that devils appear to men as angels of light. Light comes from fire, and fire burns; so light women will burn. Don't go near her."},{"t":"sp","who":"COURTESAN","lines":["Your man and you are marvellous merry, sir.","Will you go with me? We'll mend our dinner here."],"id":423,"m":"You and your man are wonderfully merry, sir. Will you come with me? We'll top up our dinner here."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Master, if you do, expect spoon-meat, or bespeak a long spoon."],"id":424,"m":"Master, if you do, expect soft food eaten with a spoon, or order a long spoon."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Why, Dromio?"],"id":425,"m":"Why, Dromio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Marry, he must have a long spoon that must eat with the devil."],"id":426,"m":"Why, a man needs a long spoon if he's going to eat with the devil."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Avoid then, fiend! What tell'st thou me of supping?","Thou art, as you are all, a sorceress.","I conjure thee to leave me and be gone."],"id":427,"m":"Away then, fiend! Why are you talking to me about supper? You're a sorceress, like all of you here. I command you to leave me and go."},{"t":"sp","who":"COURTESAN","lines":["Give me the ring of mine you had at dinner,","Or, for my diamond, the chain you promis'd,","And I'll be gone, sir, and not trouble you."],"id":428,"m":"Give me back my ring you had at dinner, or, in return for my diamond, the chain you promised, and I'll go, sir, and not bother you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Some devils ask but the paring of one's nail,","A rush, a hair, a drop of blood, a pin,","A nut, a cherry-stone; but she, more covetous,","Would have a chain.","Master, be wise; and if you give it her,","The devil will shake her chain and fright us with it."],"id":429,"m":"Some devils only ask for a fingernail clipping, a rush, a hair, a drop of blood, a pin, a nut, a cherry stone. But she, greedier, wants a chain. Master, be sensible. If you give it to her, the devil will rattle her chain and frighten us with it."},{"t":"sp","who":"COURTESAN","lines":["I pray you, sir, my ring, or else the chain;","I hope you do not mean to cheat me so."],"id":430,"m":"Please, sir, my ring, or else the chain. I hope you don't intend to cheat me like this."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Avaunt, thou witch! Come, Dromio, let us go."],"id":431,"m":"Away, you witch! Come, Dromio, let's go."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Fly pride, says the peacock. Mistress, that you know."],"id":432,"m":"\"Beware of pride,\" says the peacock. Mistress, you know all about that."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Antipholus of Syracuse and Dromio of Syracuse.","id":433},{"t":"sp","who":"COURTESAN","lines":["Now, out of doubt Antipholus is mad,","Else would he never so demean himself.","A ring he hath of mine worth forty ducats,","And for the same he promis'd me a chain;","Both one and other he denies me now.","The reason that I gather he is mad,","Besides this present instance of his rage,","Is a mad tale he told today at dinner","Of his own doors being shut against his entrance.","Belike his wife, acquainted with his fits,","On purpose shut the doors against his way.","My way is now to hie home to his house,","And tell his wife that, being lunatic,","He rush'd into my house and took perforce","My ring away. This course I fittest choose,","For forty ducats is too much to lose."],"id":434,"m":"Now there's no doubt Antipholus is mad, or he would never behave like this. He has a ring of mine worth forty ducats, and in return he promised me a chain. Now he denies me both. Besides this present outburst, what makes me think he's mad is a mad story he told at dinner today about his own doors being shut against him. I suppose his wife, knowing about his fits, shut the doors against him on purpose. My plan now is to hurry to his house and tell his wife that, in a fit of lunacy, he rushed into my house and took my ring by force. This is the best course to take, because forty ducats is too much to lose."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":435},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"IV","loc":"A street.","id":436},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Antipholus of Ephesus with an Officer.","id":437},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Fear me not, man, I will not break away:","I'll give thee ere I leave thee so much money,","To warrant thee, as I am 'rested for.","My wife is in a wayward mood today,","And will not lightly trust the messenger","That I should be attach'd in Ephesus;","I tell you 'twill sound harshly in her ears."],"id":438,"m":"Don't worry about me, man, I won't run away. Before I leave you I'll give you as much money as I'm arrested for, as your guarantee. My wife is in a difficult mood today and won't easily believe the messenger who says I've been arrested in Ephesus. I tell you, it'll sound harsh in her ears."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Dromio of Ephesus with a rope's end.","id":439},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","cont":1,"lines":["Here comes my man. I think he brings the money.","How now, sir! have you that I sent you for?"],"id":440,"m":"Here comes my man. I think he's bringing the money. Well, sir! Have you got what I sent you for?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Here's that, I warrant you, will pay them all."],"id":441,"m":"Here's something, I promise you, that will pay them all back."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["But where's the money?"],"id":442,"m":"But where's the money?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Why, sir, I gave the money for the rope."],"id":443,"m":"Why, sir, I gave the money for the rope."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Five hundred ducats, villain, for a rope?"],"id":444,"m":"Five hundred ducats, you villain, for a rope?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["I'll serve you, sir, five hundred at the rate."],"id":445,"m":"I'll get you five hundred ropes at that price, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["To what end did I bid thee hie thee home?"],"id":446,"m":"What did I tell you to hurry home for?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["To a rope's end, sir; and to that end am I return'd."],"id":447,"m":"For a rope's end, sir, and for that end I've come back."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["And to that end, sir, I will welcome you."],"id":448,"m":"And for that end, sir, I'll welcome you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Beating him.","id":449},{"t":"sp","who":"OFFICER","lines":["Good sir, be patient."],"id":450,"m":"Good sir, be patient."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Nay, 'tis for me to be patient. I am in adversity."],"id":451,"m":"No, I'm the one who needs to be patient. I'm the one suffering."},{"t":"sp","who":"OFFICER","lines":["Good now, hold thy tongue."],"id":452,"m":"Come on now, hold your tongue."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Nay, rather persuade him to hold his hands."],"id":453,"m":"No, persuade him to hold his hands instead."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Thou whoreson, senseless villain."],"id":454,"m":"You son of a whore, you senseless villain!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["I would I were senseless, sir, that I might not feel your blows."],"id":455,"m":"I wish I was senseless, sir, so I couldn't feel your blows."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Thou art sensible in nothing but blows, and so is an ass."],"id":456,"m":"You don't sense anything except blows, and neither does a donkey."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["I am an ass indeed; you may prove it by my long ears. I have served him","from the hour of my nativity to this instant, and have nothing at his","hands for my service but blows. When I am cold, he heats me with","beating; when I am warm he cools me with beating. I am waked with it","when I sleep, raised with it when I sit, driven out of doors with it","when I go from home, welcomed home with it when I return. Nay, I bear","it on my shoulders as a beggar wont her brat; and I think when he hath","lamed me, I shall beg with it from door to door."],"id":457,"m":"I'm a donkey all right: you can tell by my long ears. I've served him from the hour I was born till now, and got nothing from him for my service but blows. When I'm cold, he warms me up with a beating; when I'm warm, he cools me down with a beating. I'm woken up with it when I'm asleep, got up with it when I'm sitting down, driven out of the door with it when I go out, welcomed home with it when I come back. Why, I carry it on my shoulders like a beggar woman carries her child, and I think when he's crippled me I'll go begging with it from door to door."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Adriana, Luciana, Courtesan and a Schoolmaster called Pinch.","id":458},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Come, go along, my wife is coming yonder."],"id":459,"m":"Come on, let's go. My wife's coming over there."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Mistress, respice finem, respect your end, or rather, the prophesy","like the parrot, \"Beware the rope's end.\""],"id":460,"m":"Mistress, \"respice finem\", consider your end; or rather, as the parrot's prophecy goes, \"Beware the rope's end.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Wilt thou still talk?"],"id":461,"m":"Will you keep talking?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Beats him.","id":462},{"t":"sp","who":"COURTESAN","lines":["How say you now? Is not your husband mad?"],"id":463,"m":"What do you say now? Isn't your husband mad?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["His incivility confirms no less.","Good Doctor Pinch, you are a conjurer;","Establish him in his true sense again,","And I will please you what you will demand."],"id":464,"m":"His violence proves nothing less. Good Doctor Pinch, you know how to cast out spirits. Restore him to his right mind, and I'll pay you whatever you ask."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Alas, how fiery and how sharp he looks!"],"id":465,"m":"Oh dear, how wild and fierce he looks!"},{"t":"sp","who":"COURTESAN","lines":["Mark how he trembles in his ecstasy."],"id":466,"m":"See how he trembles in his fit."},{"t":"sp","who":"PINCH","lines":["Give me your hand, and let me feel your pulse."],"id":467,"m":"Give me your hand and let me feel your pulse."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["There is my hand, and let it feel your ear."],"id":468,"m":"There's my hand, and let it feel your ear."},{"t":"sp","who":"PINCH","lines":["I charge thee, Satan, hous'd within this man,","To yield possession to my holy prayers,","And to thy state of darkness hie thee straight.","I conjure thee by all the saints in heaven."],"id":469,"m":"I command you, Satan, living inside this man, to give up your hold to my holy prayers and hurry straight back to your realm of darkness. I command you by all the saints in heaven."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Peace, doting wizard, peace; I am not mad."],"id":470,"m":"Quiet, you senile wizard, quiet! I'm not mad."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["O, that thou wert not, poor distressed soul!"],"id":471,"m":"Oh, if only you weren't, poor troubled soul!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["You minion, you, are these your customers?","Did this companion with the saffron face","Revel and feast it at my house today,","Whilst upon me the guilty doors were shut,","And I denied to enter in my house?"],"id":472,"m":"You hussy, are these your clients? Did this fellow with the yellow face party and feast at my house today, while the guilty doors were shut on me and I was refused entry to my own house?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["O husband, God doth know you din'd at home,","Where would you had remain'd until this time,","Free from these slanders and this open shame."],"id":473,"m":"Oh husband, God knows you dined at home, and I wish you'd stayed there till now, free from these slanders and this public shame."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Din'd at home? Thou villain, what sayest thou?"],"id":474,"m":"Dined at home? You villain, what do you say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Sir, sooth to say, you did not dine at home."],"id":475,"m":"Sir, to tell the truth, you didn't dine at home."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Were not my doors lock'd up and I shut out?"],"id":476,"m":"Weren't my doors locked and I shut out?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Perdy, your doors were lock'd, and you shut out."],"id":477,"m":"By God, your doors were locked, and you were shut out."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["And did not she herself revile me there?"],"id":478,"m":"And didn't she herself abuse me there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Sans fable, she herself revil'd you there."],"id":479,"m":"No word of a lie, she abused you there herself."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Did not her kitchen-maid rail, taunt, and scorn me?"],"id":480,"m":"Didn't her kitchen maid rail at me, taunt me and scorn me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Certes, she did, the kitchen-vestal scorn'd you."],"id":481,"m":"Certainly she did: the kitchen-goddess scorned you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["And did not I in rage depart from thence?"],"id":482,"m":"And didn't I leave there in a rage?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["In verity, you did; my bones bear witness,","That since have felt the vigour of his rage."],"id":483,"m":"In truth you did. My bones can bear witness, since they've felt the force of his rage ever since."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Is't good to soothe him in these contraries?"],"id":484,"m":"Is it a good idea to humour him in these contradictions?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PINCH","lines":["It is no shame; the fellow finds his vein,","And yielding to him, humours well his frenzy."],"id":485,"m":"There's no harm in it. The fellow has worked out his mood, and by agreeing with him, he's humouring his madness well."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Thou hast suborn'd the goldsmith to arrest me."],"id":486,"m":"You've bribed the goldsmith to have me arrested."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Alas! I sent you money to redeem you","By Dromio here, who came in haste for it."],"id":487,"m":"Oh! I sent you money to free you, by Dromio here, who came rushing for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Money by me? Heart and goodwill you might,","But surely, master, not a rag of money."],"id":488,"m":"Money by me? Love and goodwill maybe, but certainly, master, not a scrap of money."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Went'st not thou to her for a purse of ducats?"],"id":489,"m":"Didn't you go to her for a purse of ducats?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["He came to me, and I deliver'd it."],"id":490,"m":"He came to me, and I gave it to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["And I am witness with her that she did."],"id":491,"m":"And I'm a witness with her that she did."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["God and the rope-maker bear me witness","That I was sent for nothing but a rope."],"id":492,"m":"God and the rope-maker are my witnesses that I was sent for nothing but a rope."},{"t":"sp","who":"PINCH","lines":["Mistress, both man and master is possess'd,","I know it by their pale and deadly looks.","They must be bound and laid in some dark room."],"id":493,"m":"Mistress, both man and master are possessed. I can tell by their pale, deathly looks. They must be tied up and put in some dark room."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Say, wherefore didst thou lock me forth today,","And why dost thou deny the bag of gold?"],"id":494,"m":"Tell me, why did you lock me out today, and why do you deny the bag of gold?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["I did not, gentle husband, lock thee forth."],"id":495,"m":"I didn't lock you out, dear husband."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["And gentle master, I receiv'd no gold;","But I confess, sir, that we were lock'd out."],"id":496,"m":"And dear master, I received no gold. But I admit, sir, that we were locked out."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Dissembling villain, thou speak'st false in both."],"id":497,"m":"Lying villain, you're lying in both."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Dissembling harlot, thou art false in all,","And art confederate with a damned pack","To make a loathsome abject scorn of me.","But with these nails I'll pluck out these false eyes","That would behold in me this shameful sport."],"id":498,"m":"Lying whore, you're false in everything, and in league with a damned gang to make me a disgusting object of scorn. But I'll pluck out those false eyes with these nails, for wanting to see me made a shameful joke."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter three or four, and offer to bind him. He strives.","id":499},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["O, bind him, bind him; let him not come near me."],"id":500,"m":"Oh, tie him up, tie him up! Don't let him near me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PINCH","lines":["More company; the fiend is strong within him."],"id":501,"m":"More men! The devil is strong in him."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Ay me, poor man, how pale and wan he looks!"],"id":502,"m":"Oh dear, poor man, how pale and sickly he looks!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["What, will you murder me? Thou jailer, thou,","I am thy prisoner. Wilt thou suffer them","To make a rescue?"],"id":503,"m":"What, will you murder me? You, jailer, I'm your prisoner. Will you let them snatch me from you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OFFICER","lines":["Masters, let him go.","He is my prisoner, and you shall not have him."],"id":504,"m":"Gentlemen, let him go. He's my prisoner, and you can't have him."},{"t":"sp","who":"PINCH","lines":["Go, bind this man, for he is frantic too."],"id":505,"m":"Go and tie up this man too, because he's raving as well."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["What wilt thou do, thou peevish officer?","Hast thou delight to see a wretched man","Do outrage and displeasure to himself?"],"id":506,"m":"What do you think you're doing, you stubborn officer? Do you enjoy seeing a wretched man do himself violence and harm?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OFFICER","lines":["He is my prisoner. If I let him go,","The debt he owes will be requir'd of me."],"id":507,"m":"He's my prisoner. If I let him go, I'll be made to pay the debt he owes."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["I will discharge thee ere I go from thee;","Bear me forthwith unto his creditor,","And knowing how the debt grows, I will pay it.","Good master doctor, see him safe convey'd","Home to my house. O most unhappy day!"],"id":508,"m":"I'll settle with you before I leave you. Take me straight to his creditor, and once I know how the debt arose, I'll pay it. Good master doctor, see that he's taken safely home to my house. Oh, most unhappy day!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["O most unhappy strumpet!"],"id":509,"m":"Oh, most unhappy whore!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Master, I am here enter'd in bond for you."],"id":510,"m":"Master, I've been tied up here on your account."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Out on thee, villain! wherefore dost thou mad me?"],"id":511,"m":"Damn you, villain! Why are you driving me mad?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Will you be bound for nothing? Be mad, good master; cry, \"the devil\"."],"id":512,"m":"Will you be tied up for nothing? Go mad, good master: shout \"the devil!\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["God help, poor souls, how idly do they talk!"],"id":513,"m":"God help them, poor souls, what nonsense they talk!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Go bear him hence. Sister, go you with me."],"id":514,"m":"Take him away. Sister, you come with me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Pinch and Assistants, with Antipholus of Ephesus and Dromio of Ephesus.","id":515},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","cont":1,"lines":["Say now, whose suit is he arrested at?"],"id":516,"m":"Now tell me, on whose claim was he arrested?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OFFICER","lines":["One Angelo, a goldsmith; do you know him?"],"id":517,"m":"A man called Angelo, a goldsmith. Do you know him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["I know the man. What is the sum he owes?"],"id":518,"m":"I know the man. How much does my husband owe?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OFFICER","lines":["Two hundred ducats."],"id":519,"m":"Two hundred ducats."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Say, how grows it due?"],"id":520,"m":"Tell me, what's it for?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OFFICER","lines":["Due for a chain your husband had of him."],"id":521,"m":"For a chain your husband had from him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["He did bespeak a chain for me, but had it not."],"id":522,"m":"He did order a chain for me, but he never got it."},{"t":"sp","who":"COURTESAN","lines":["When as your husband, all in rage, today","Came to my house and took away my ring,","The ring I saw upon his finger now,","Straight after did I meet him with a chain."],"id":523,"m":"When your husband came to my house today in a rage and took away my ring, the ring I saw on his finger just now, straight afterwards I met him with a chain."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["It may be so, but I did never see it.","Come, jailer, bring me where the goldsmith is,","I long to know the truth hereof at large."],"id":524,"m":"That may be, but I never saw it. Come, jailer, take me to the goldsmith. I'm longing to know the whole truth of this."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Antipholus of Syracuse with his rapier drawn, and Dromio of Syracuse.","id":525},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["God, for thy mercy, they are loose again!"],"id":526,"m":"God have mercy, they've got loose again!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["And come with naked swords. Let's call more help","To have them bound again."],"id":527,"m":"And they're coming with swords drawn! Let's call more help to tie them up again."},{"t":"sp","who":"OFFICER","lines":["Away, they'll kill us."],"id":528,"m":"Run, they'll kill us!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt, as fast as may be, frighted.","id":529},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I see these witches are afraid of swords."],"id":530,"m":"I see these witches are afraid of swords."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["She that would be your wife now ran from you."],"id":531,"m":"The woman who wanted to be your wife has just run away from you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Come to the Centaur, fetch our stuff from thence.","I long that we were safe and sound aboard."],"id":532,"m":"Go to the Centaur and fetch our luggage from there. I can't wait to be safe and sound on board."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Faith, stay here this night, they will surely do us no harm; you saw","they speak us fair, give us gold. Methinks they are such a gentle","nation that, but for the mountain of mad flesh that claims marriage of","me, I could find in my heart to stay here still and turn witch."],"id":533,"m":"Honestly, let's stay here tonight. They certainly won't harm us: you saw they speak kindly to us and give us gold. I think they're such a gentle nation that, if it wasn't for the mountain of mad flesh who claims me in marriage, I could find it in my heart to stay here for good and become a witch."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I will not stay tonight for all the town;","Therefore away, to get our stuff aboard."],"id":534,"m":"I wouldn't stay tonight for the whole town. So come on, let's get our luggage aboard."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":535},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"A street before a priory.","id":536},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Merchant and Angelo.","id":537},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["I am sorry, sir, that I have hinder'd you,","But I protest he had the chain of me,","Though most dishonestly he doth deny it."],"id":538,"m":"I'm sorry, sir, that I've held you up, but I swear he had the chain from me, though he very dishonestly denies it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND MERCHANT","lines":["How is the man esteem'd here in the city?"],"id":539,"m":"What do people think of the man here in the city?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Of very reverend reputation, sir,","Of credit infinite, highly belov'd,","Second to none that lives here in the city.","His word might bear my wealth at any time."],"id":540,"m":"He has a very respected reputation, sir, unlimited credit, he's much loved, second to no one living in this city. His word would be good for all my wealth at any time."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND MERCHANT","lines":["Speak softly. Yonder, as I think, he walks."],"id":541,"m":"Speak quietly. I think that's him walking over there."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Antipholus of Syracuse and Dromio of Syracuse.","id":542},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["'Tis so; and that self chain about his neck","Which he forswore most monstrously to have.","Good sir, draw near to me, I'll speak to him.","Signior Antipholus, I wonder much","That you would put me to this shame and trouble,","And not without some scandal to yourself,","With circumstance and oaths so to deny","This chain, which now you wear so openly.","Beside the charge, the shame, imprisonment,","You have done wrong to this my honest friend,","Who, but for staying on our controversy,","Had hoisted sail and put to sea today.","This chain you had of me, can you deny it?"],"id":543,"m":"It is, and wearing round his neck the very chain he so outrageously swore he never had. Good sir, come closer to me. I'll speak to him. Signor Antipholus, I'm amazed that you would put me to such shame and trouble, and not without some scandal to yourself, by denying so elaborately and with such oaths this chain that you're now wearing so openly. Besides the cost, the shame and the arrest, you've wronged my honest friend here, who would have set sail and put out to sea today if he hadn't stayed for our dispute. You had this chain from me: can you deny it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I think I had: I never did deny it."],"id":544,"m":"I think I did. I never denied it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND MERCHANT","lines":["Yes, that you did, sir, and forswore it too."],"id":545,"m":"Yes, you did, sir, and swore falsely about it too."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Who heard me to deny it or forswear it?"],"id":546,"m":"Who heard me deny it or swear falsely?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND MERCHANT","lines":["These ears of mine, thou know'st, did hear thee.","Fie on thee, wretch. 'Tis pity that thou liv'st","To walk where any honest men resort."],"id":547,"m":"These ears of mine heard you, as you know. Shame on you, wretch! It's a pity you're alive to walk where honest men go."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Thou art a villain to impeach me thus;","I'll prove mine honour and mine honesty","Against thee presently, if thou dar'st stand."],"id":548,"m":"You're a villain to accuse me like this. I'll prove my honour and honesty against you right now, if you dare to stand and fight."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND MERCHANT","lines":["I dare, and do defy thee for a villain."],"id":549,"m":"I dare, and I challenge you as a villain."},{"t":"sd","text":"They draw.","id":550},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Adriana, Luciana, Courtesan and others.","id":551},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Hold, hurt him not, for God's sake, he is mad.","Some get within him, take his sword away.","Bind Dromio too, and bear them to my house."],"id":552,"m":"Stop, don't hurt him, for God's sake! He's mad. Someone get close to him and take his sword away. Tie up Dromio too, and carry them to my house."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Run, master, run, for God's sake, take a house.","This is some priory; in, or we are spoil'd."],"id":553,"m":"Run, master, run, for God's sake! Get inside a building. This is some priory. In, or we're done for!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Antipholus of Syracuse and Dromio of Syracuse to the priory.","id":554},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lady Abbess.","id":555},{"t":"sp","who":"ABBESS","lines":["Be quiet, people. Wherefore throng you hither?"],"id":556,"m":"Quiet, everyone. Why are you crowding here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["To fetch my poor distracted husband hence.","Let us come in, that we may bind him fast","And bear him home for his recovery."],"id":557,"m":"To fetch my poor, deranged husband from here. Let us in, so we can tie him up securely and take him home to recover."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["I knew he was not in his perfect wits."],"id":558,"m":"I knew he wasn't in his right mind."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND MERCHANT","lines":["I am sorry now that I did draw on him."],"id":559,"m":"Now I'm sorry I drew my sword on him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABBESS","lines":["How long hath this possession held the man?"],"id":560,"m":"How long has this fit held the man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["This week he hath been heavy, sour, sad,","And much different from the man he was.","But till this afternoon his passion","Ne'er brake into extremity of rage."],"id":561,"m":"All this week he's been heavy, sour and sad, and very different from the man he used to be. But until this afternoon his disorder never broke out into such extreme rage."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABBESS","lines":["Hath he not lost much wealth by wreck of sea?","Buried some dear friend? Hath not else his eye","Stray'd his affection in unlawful love?","A sin prevailing much in youthful men","Who give their eyes the liberty of gazing?","Which of these sorrows is he subject to?"],"id":562,"m":"Hasn't he lost a lot of money in a shipwreck? Buried some dear friend? Or has his eye led his affections astray into unlawful love, a sin very common in young men who let their eyes wander freely? Which of these sorrows is he suffering from?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["To none of these, except it be the last,","Namely, some love that drew him oft from home."],"id":563,"m":"None of these, unless it's the last: some love that often drew him away from home."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABBESS","lines":["You should for that have reprehended him."],"id":564,"m":"You should have scolded him for that."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Why, so I did."],"id":565,"m":"Why, I did."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABBESS","lines":["Ay, but not rough enough."],"id":566,"m":"Yes, but not harshly enough."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["As roughly as my modesty would let me."],"id":567,"m":"As harshly as my modesty would let me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABBESS","lines":["Haply in private."],"id":568,"m":"Perhaps only in private."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["And in assemblies too."],"id":569,"m":"And in company too."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABBESS","lines":["Ay, but not enough."],"id":570,"m":"Yes, but not enough."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["It was the copy of our conference.","In bed he slept not for my urging it;","At board he fed not for my urging it;","Alone, it was the subject of my theme;","In company I often glanced it;","Still did I tell him it was vile and bad."],"id":571,"m":"It was the theme of all our conversation. In bed, he couldn't sleep because I kept on about it. At the table, he couldn't eat because I kept on about it. Alone, it was the subject I talked about; in company, I often hinted at it. I always told him it was vile and wicked."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABBESS","lines":["And thereof came it that the man was mad.","The venom clamours of a jealous woman","Poisons more deadly than a mad dog's tooth.","It seems his sleeps were hindered by thy railing,","And thereof comes it that his head is light.","Thou say'st his meat was sauc'd with thy upbraidings.","Unquiet meals make ill digestions;","Thereof the raging fire of fever bred,","And what's a fever but a fit of madness?","Thou say'st his sports were hinder'd by thy brawls.","Sweet recreation barr'd, what doth ensue","But moody and dull melancholy,","Kinsman to grim and comfortless despair,","And at her heels a huge infectious troop","Of pale distemperatures and foes to life?","In food, in sport, and life-preserving rest","To be disturb'd would mad or man or beast.","The consequence is, then, thy jealous fits","Hath scar'd thy husband from the use of's wits."],"id":572,"m":"And that's how the man came to be mad. The venomous ranting of a jealous woman poisons more deadly than a mad dog's bite. It seems your nagging kept him from sleeping, and that's why his head is light. You say his food was seasoned with your scolding. Disturbed meals make for bad digestion; that breeds the raging fire of fever, and what is fever but a fit of madness? You say your quarrelling spoiled his pleasures. When sweet recreation is barred, what follows but moody, dull melancholy, close relative of grim, comfortless despair, followed by a huge infectious troop of pale diseases that are enemies to life? Being disturbed in food, in pleasure and in life-giving sleep would drive man or beast mad. The result is that your jealous fits have frightened your husband out of his wits."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["She never reprehended him but mildly,","When he demean'd himself rough, rude, and wildly.","Why bear you these rebukes and answer not?"],"id":573,"m":"She only ever scolded him gently, when he behaved roughly, rudely and wildly. Why do you take these rebukes and not answer back?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["She did betray me to my own reproof.","Good people, enter and lay hold on him."],"id":574,"m":"She has tricked me into blaming myself. Good people, go in and seize him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABBESS","lines":["No, not a creature enters in my house."],"id":575,"m":"No, not one creature enters my house."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Then let your servants bring my husband forth."],"id":576,"m":"Then let your servants bring my husband out."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABBESS","lines":["Neither. He took this place for sanctuary,","And it shall privilege him from your hands","Till I have brought him to his wits again,","Or lose my labour in assaying it."],"id":577,"m":"Neither. He took this place as a sanctuary, and it will protect him from your hands until I've brought him back to his senses, or wasted my efforts trying."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["I will attend my husband, be his nurse,","Diet his sickness, for it is my office,","And will have no attorney but myself;","And therefore let me have him home with me."],"id":578,"m":"I'll look after my husband, nurse him and treat his sickness, because it's my duty. I'll have no one act for me but myself, so let me take him home with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABBESS","lines":["Be patient, for I will not let him stir","Till I have used the approved means I have,","With wholesome syrups, drugs, and holy prayers,","To make of him a formal man again.","It is a branch and parcel of mine oath,","A charitable duty of my order;","Therefore depart, and leave him here with me."],"id":579,"m":"Be patient, because I won't let him stir until I've used the tested remedies I have, healthy syrups, drugs and holy prayers, to make him a normal man again. It is part of my vows, a charitable duty of my order. So go, and leave him here with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["I will not hence and leave my husband here;","And ill it doth beseem your holiness","To separate the husband and the wife."],"id":580,"m":"I won't go away and leave my husband here. And it ill becomes your holiness to separate husband and wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABBESS","lines":["Be quiet and depart. Thou shalt not have him."],"id":581,"m":"Be quiet and go. You will not have him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Abbess.","id":582},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Complain unto the duke of this indignity."],"id":583,"m":"Complain to the Duke about this outrage."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Come, go. I will fall prostrate at his feet,","And never rise until my tears and prayers","Have won his grace to come in person hither","And take perforce my husband from the abbess."],"id":584,"m":"Come, let's go. I'll throw myself at his feet and never get up until my tears and prayers have persuaded his grace to come here himself and take my husband from the abbess by force."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND MERCHANT","lines":["By this, I think, the dial points at five.","Anon, I'm sure, the Duke himself in person","Comes this way to the melancholy vale,","The place of death and sorry execution","Behind the ditches of the abbey here."],"id":585,"m":"I think by now the sundial points to five. Soon, I'm sure, the Duke himself will come this way to the gloomy valley, the place of death and sad executions behind the abbey ditches here."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["Upon what cause?"],"id":586,"m":"What for?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND MERCHANT","lines":["To see a reverend Syracusian merchant,","Who put unluckily into this bay","Against the laws and statutes of this town,","Beheaded publicly for his offence."],"id":587,"m":"To see a respected merchant from Syracuse, who unluckily came into this bay against the laws and statutes of this town, publicly beheaded for his offence."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["See where they come. We will behold his death."],"id":588,"m":"Look, here they come. We'll watch his death."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Kneel to the Duke before he pass the abbey."],"id":589,"m":"Kneel to the Duke before he passes the abbey."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Duke, attended; Egeon, bareheaded; with the Headsman and other Officers.","id":590},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Yet once again proclaim it publicly,","If any friend will pay the sum for him,","He shall not die; so much we tender him."],"id":591,"m":"Announce it publicly once more: if any friend will pay the sum for him, he will not die. That's how much we care for him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Justice, most sacred duke, against the abbess!"],"id":592,"m":"Justice, most sacred Duke, against the abbess!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["She is a virtuous and a reverend lady,","It cannot be that she hath done thee wrong."],"id":593,"m":"She is a virtuous and respected lady. It can't be that she has wronged you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["May it please your grace, Antipholus, my husband,","Who I made lord of me and all I had","At your important letters, this ill day","A most outrageous fit of madness took him;","That desp'rately he hurried through the street,","With him his bondman all as mad as he,","Doing displeasure to the citizens","By rushing in their houses, bearing thence","Rings, jewels, anything his rage did like.","Once did I get him bound and sent him home,","Whilst to take order for the wrongs I went,","That here and there his fury had committed.","Anon, I wot not by what strong escape,","He broke from those that had the guard of him,","And with his mad attendant and himself,","Each one with ireful passion, with drawn swords,","Met us again, and, madly bent on us,","Chased us away; till raising of more aid,","We came again to bind them. Then they fled","Into this abbey, whither we pursued them.","And here the abbess shuts the gates on us,","And will not suffer us to fetch him out,","Nor send him forth that we may bear him hence.","Therefore, most gracious duke, with thy command","Let him be brought forth and borne hence for help."],"id":594,"m":"May it please your grace: Antipholus, my husband, whom I made lord of myself and all I owned at your urgent request, was seized by a most outrageous fit of madness on this bad day. He rushed wildly through the street, with his servant just as mad as he, offending the citizens by bursting into their houses and carrying off rings, jewels, anything his rage fancied. Once I had him tied up and sent home, while I went to settle the damage his fury had done here and there. Then, by I don't know what violent escape, he broke away from the men guarding him, and he and his mad servant, both furious, with drawn swords, met us again, came madly at us and chased us away, until we raised more help and came back to tie them up. Then they fled into this abbey, where we chased them. And here the abbess shuts the gates on us and won't let us fetch him out, or send him out so we can take him away. So, most gracious Duke, at your command let him be brought out and taken away for help."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Long since thy husband serv'd me in my wars,","And I to thee engag'd a prince's word,","When thou didst make him master of thy bed,","To do him all the grace and good I could.","Go, some of you, knock at the abbey gate,","And bid the lady abbess come to me.","I will determine this before I stir."],"id":595,"m":"Long ago your husband served me in my wars, and when you made him your husband I gave you my word as a prince to do him all the favour and good I could. Some of you, go and knock at the abbey gate and tell the lady abbess to come to me. I'll settle this before I move."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":596},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["O mistress, mistress, shift and save yourself.","My master and his man are both broke loose,","Beaten the maids a-row, and bound the doctor,","Whose beard they have singed off with brands of fire,","And ever as it blazed they threw on him","Great pails of puddled mire to quench the hair.","My master preaches patience to him, and the while","His man with scissors nicks him like a fool;","And sure (unless you send some present help)","Between them they will kill the conjurer."],"id":597,"m":"Oh mistress, mistress, run and save yourself! My master and his man have both broken loose, beaten the maids one after another and tied up the doctor. They've singed his beard off with burning sticks, and every time it caught fire they threw great buckets of muddy water on him to put his hair out. My master preaches patience to him, while his man cuts his hair with scissors like a fool's. Surely, unless you send help at once, between them they'll kill the conjuror."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Peace, fool, thy master and his man are here,","And that is false thou dost report to us."],"id":598,"m":"Quiet, fool. Your master and his man are here, and what you're telling us is false."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Mistress, upon my life, I tell you true.","I have not breath'd almost since I did see it.","He cries for you, and vows, if he can take you,","To scorch your face and to disfigure you."],"id":599,"m":"Mistress, on my life, I'm telling the truth. I've hardly taken a breath since I saw it. He's shouting for you and swears that if he catches you he'll scorch your face and disfigure you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Cry within.","id":600},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","cont":1,"lines":["Hark, hark, I hear him, mistress. Fly, be gone!"],"id":601,"m":"Listen, listen, I can hear him, mistress. Run, go!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Come, stand by me, fear nothing. Guard with halberds."],"id":602,"m":"Come, stand by me, don't be afraid. Guard us with your halberds."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Ay me, it is my husband. Witness you","That he is borne about invisible.","Even now we hous'd him in the abbey here,","And now he's there, past thought of human reason."],"id":603,"m":"Oh dear, it's my husband! You're witnesses that he is being carried about invisibly. A moment ago we drove him into the abbey here, and now he's there, beyond anything human reason can explain."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Antipholus and Dromio of Ephesus.","id":604},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Justice, most gracious duke; O, grant me justice!","Even for the service that long since I did thee","When I bestrid thee in the wars, and took","Deep scars to save thy life; even for the blood","That then I lost for thee, now grant me justice."],"id":605,"m":"Justice, most gracious Duke, oh, grant me justice! For the sake of the service I did you long ago, when I stood over you in battle and took deep wounds to save your life, for the sake of the blood I lost for you then, grant me justice now."},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEON","lines":["Unless the fear of death doth make me dote,","I see my son Antipholus and Dromio."],"id":606,"m":"Unless the fear of death is making me see things, I see my son Antipholus and Dromio."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Justice, sweet prince, against that woman there.","She whom thou gav'st to me to be my wife;","That hath abused and dishonour'd me","Even in the strength and height of injury.","Beyond imagination is the wrong","That she this day hath shameless thrown on me."],"id":607,"m":"Justice, sweet prince, against that woman there, the one you gave me as my wife, who has abused and dishonoured me with the most extreme injury. The wrong she has shamelessly done me today is beyond imagining."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Discover how, and thou shalt find me just."],"id":608,"m":"Reveal how, and you'll find me just."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["This day, great duke, she shut the doors upon me","While she with harlots feasted in my house."],"id":609,"m":"Today, great Duke, she shut the doors on me while she feasted with lowlifes in my house."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["A grievous fault. Say, woman, didst thou so?"],"id":610,"m":"A serious offence. Tell me, woman, did you do that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["No, my good lord. Myself, he, and my sister","Today did dine together. So befall my soul","As this is false he burdens me withal."],"id":611,"m":"No, my good lord. He, my sister and I dined together today. May my soul suffer if what he accuses me of isn't false."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIANA","lines":["Ne'er may I look on day nor sleep on night","But she tells to your highness simple truth."],"id":612,"m":"May I never see daylight or sleep at night again if she isn't telling your highness the plain truth."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["O perjur'd woman! They are both forsworn.","In this the madman justly chargeth them."],"id":613,"m":"Oh, lying woman! They're both perjuring themselves. In this the madman accuses them rightly."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["My liege, I am advised what I say,","Neither disturb'd with the effect of wine,","Nor heady-rash, provok'd with raging ire,","Albeit my wrongs might make one wiser mad.","This woman lock'd me out this day from dinner.","That goldsmith there, were he not pack'd with her,","Could witness it, for he was with me then,","Who parted with me to go fetch a chain,","Promising to bring it to the Porpentine,","Where Balthasar and I did dine together.","Our dinner done, and he not coming thither,","I went to seek him. In the street I met him,","And in his company that gentleman.","There did this perjur'd goldsmith swear me down","That I this day of him receiv'd the chain,","Which, God he knows, I saw not. For the which","He did arrest me with an officer.","I did obey, and sent my peasant home","For certain ducats. He with none return'd.","Then fairly I bespoke the officer","To go in person with me to my house.","By th' way we met","My wife, her sister, and a rabble more","Of vile confederates. Along with them","They brought one Pinch, a hungry lean-faced villain,","A mere anatomy, a mountebank,","A threadbare juggler, and a fortune-teller;","A needy, hollow-ey'd, sharp-looking wretch;","A living dead man. This pernicious slave,","Forsooth, took on him as a conjurer,","And gazing in mine eyes, feeling my pulse,","And with no face (as 'twere) outfacing me,","Cries out, I was possess'd. Then altogether","They fell upon me, bound me, bore me thence,","And in a dark and dankish vault at home","There left me and my man, both bound together,","Till gnawing with my teeth my bonds in sunder,","I gain'd my freedom and immediately","Ran hither to your Grace, whom I beseech","To give me ample satisfaction","For these deep shames and great indignities."],"id":614,"m":"My lord, I know what I'm saying. I'm not affected by wine, or rash and hot-headed with raging anger, though my wrongs might drive a wiser man mad. This woman locked me out from dinner today. That goldsmith there, if he weren't in league with her, could confirm it, because he was with me then. He left me to fetch a chain, promising to bring it to the Porcupine, where Balthasar and I had dinner together. When dinner was over and he hadn't come, I went to look for him. I met him in the street, with that gentleman in his company. There this lying goldsmith swore to my face that I had received the chain from him today, which, God knows, I never saw. For that he had me arrested by an officer. I obeyed, and sent my slave home for some ducats. He came back with none. Then I politely asked the officer to come with me to my house himself. On the way we met my wife, her sister and a whole rabble of vile accomplices. They brought along one Pinch, a hungry, lean-faced villain, a walking skeleton, a quack, a threadbare conjurer and fortune-teller, a needy, hollow-eyed, sharp-faced wretch, a living dead man. This poisonous slave, if you please, set himself up as an exorcist, and gazing into my eyes, feeling my pulse, and staring me down with his non-existent face, as it were, cried out that I was possessed. Then they all fell on me together, tied me up, carried me off, and left me and my man tied together in a dark, damp cellar at home, until I gnawed through my bonds with my teeth, won my freedom and ran straight here to your grace. I beg you to give me full satisfaction for these deep humiliations and great insults."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["My lord, in truth, thus far I witness with him,","That he din'd not at home, but was lock'd out."],"id":615,"m":"My lord, truly, I can confirm this much: he didn't dine at home, but was locked out."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["But had he such a chain of thee, or no?"],"id":616,"m":"But did he have such a chain from you or not?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["He had, my lord, and when he ran in here","These people saw the chain about his neck."],"id":617,"m":"He did, my lord, and when he ran in here these people saw the chain round his neck."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND MERCHANT","lines":["Besides, I will be sworn these ears of mine","Heard you confess you had the chain of him,","After you first forswore it on the mart,","And thereupon I drew my sword on you;","And then you fled into this abbey here,","From whence I think you are come by miracle."],"id":618,"m":"Besides, I'll swear these ears of mine heard you admit you had the chain from him, after you first denied it at the market, and at that I drew my sword on you. Then you fled into this abbey here, and I think you've come out of it by a miracle."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["I never came within these abbey walls,","Nor ever didst thou draw thy sword on me.","I never saw the chain, so help me heaven;","And this is false you burden me withal."],"id":619,"m":"I never came inside these abbey walls, and you never drew your sword on me. I never saw the chain, so help me heaven, and what you accuse me of is false."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Why, what an intricate impeach is this!","I think you all have drunk of Circe's cup.","If here you hous'd him, here he would have been.","If he were mad, he would not plead so coldly.","You say he din'd at home, the goldsmith here","Denies that saying. Sirrah, what say you?"],"id":620,"m":"Why, what a tangled accusation this is! I think you've all drunk from Circe's cup. If you drove him in here, he'd be in here. If he were mad, he wouldn't plead so calmly. You say he dined at home; the goldsmith here denies it. Fellow, what do you say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Sir, he dined with her there, at the Porpentine."],"id":621,"m":"Sir, he dined with her there, at the Porcupine."},{"t":"sp","who":"COURTESAN","lines":["He did, and from my finger snatch'd that ring."],"id":622,"m":"He did, and snatched that ring off my finger."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["'Tis true, my liege, this ring I had of her."],"id":623,"m":"It's true, my lord. I had this ring from her."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Saw'st thou him enter at the abbey here?"],"id":624,"m":"Did you see him go into the abbey here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COURTESAN","lines":["As sure, my liege, as I do see your grace."],"id":625,"m":"As surely, my lord, as I see your grace."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Why, this is strange. Go call the abbess hither.","I think you are all mated, or stark mad."],"id":626,"m":"Why, this is strange. Go and call the abbess here. I think you're all bewildered, or stark mad."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit one to the Abbess.","id":627},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEON","lines":["Most mighty Duke, vouchsafe me speak a word;","Haply I see a friend will save my life","And pay the sum that may deliver me."],"id":628,"m":"Most mighty Duke, allow me to say a word. By luck I see a friend who will save my life and pay the sum that will free me."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Speak freely, Syracusian, what thou wilt."],"id":629,"m":"Speak freely, man of Syracuse. Say what you want."},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEON","lines":["Is not your name, sir, call'd Antipholus?","And is not that your bondman Dromio?"],"id":630,"m":"Isn't your name Antipholus, sir? And isn't that your servant Dromio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Within this hour I was his bondman, sir,","But he, I thank him, gnaw'd in two my cords.","Now am I Dromio, and his man, unbound."],"id":631,"m":"An hour ago I was his bondman, sir, but he, thank him, gnawed my cords in two. Now I'm Dromio, his man, unbound."},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEON","lines":["I am sure you both of you remember me."],"id":632,"m":"I'm sure you both remember me."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Ourselves we do remember, sir, by you.","For lately we were bound as you are now.","You are not Pinch's patient, are you, sir?"],"id":633,"m":"We're reminded of ourselves, sir, by looking at you, because we were bound just now as you are. You're not one of Pinch's patients, are you, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEON","lines":["Why look you strange on me? you know me well."],"id":634,"m":"Why do you look at me like a stranger? You know me well."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["I never saw you in my life till now."],"id":635,"m":"I never saw you in my life till now."},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEON","lines":["O! grief hath chang'd me since you saw me last,","And careful hours with time's deformed hand,","Have written strange defeatures in my face.","But tell me yet, dost thou not know my voice?"],"id":636,"m":"Oh, grief has changed me since you saw me last, and anxious hours, with time's disfiguring hand, have written strange changes on my face. But tell me, don't you know my voice?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Neither."],"id":637,"m":"No."},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEON","lines":["Dromio, nor thou?"],"id":638,"m":"Dromio, don't you either?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["No, trust me, sir, nor I."],"id":639,"m":"No, believe me, sir, I don't."},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEON","lines":["I am sure thou dost."],"id":640,"m":"I'm sure you do."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Ay, sir, but I am sure I do not, and whatsoever a man denies, you are","now bound to believe him."],"id":641,"m":"Yes, sir, but I'm sure I don't; and whatever a man denies, you're now bound to believe him."},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEON","lines":["Not know my voice! O time's extremity,","Hast thou so crack'd and splitted my poor tongue","In seven short years that here my only son","Knows not my feeble key of untun'd cares?","Though now this grained face of mine be hid","In sap-consuming winter's drizzled snow,","And all the conduits of my blood froze up,","Yet hath my night of life some memory,","My wasting lamps some fading glimmer left,","My dull deaf ears a little use to hear.","All these old witnesses, I cannot err,","Tell me thou art my son Antipholus."],"id":642,"m":"Not know my voice! Oh, cruel time, have you so cracked and split my poor voice in seven short years that my only son here doesn't know my feeble, worn-out tone? Though my lined face is now hidden under the drizzling snow of winter, which dries up life, and all the channels of my blood are frozen, my life's night still has some memory, my dimming eyes some glimmer of light, my dull, deaf ears a little hearing. All these old witnesses tell me, and I can't be wrong, that you are my son Antipholus."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["I never saw my father in my life."],"id":643,"m":"I never saw my father in my life."},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEON","lines":["But seven years since, in Syracusa, boy,","Thou know'st we parted; but perhaps, my son,","Thou sham'st to acknowledge me in misery."],"id":644,"m":"But you know we parted in Syracuse only seven years ago, boy. Or perhaps, my son, you're ashamed to acknowledge me in my misery."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["The duke and all that know me in the city,","Can witness with me that it is not so.","I ne'er saw Syracusa in my life."],"id":645,"m":"The Duke and everyone who knows me in the city can bear witness that it's not so. I've never seen Syracuse in my life."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["I tell thee, Syracusian, twenty years","Have I been patron to Antipholus,","During which time he ne'er saw Syracusa.","I see thy age and dangers make thee dote."],"id":646,"m":"I tell you, man of Syracuse, I have been Antipholus's patron for twenty years, and in that time he has never seen Syracuse. I see your age and danger make you rave."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Abbess with Antipholus of Syracuse and Dromio of Syracuse.","id":647},{"t":"sp","who":"ABBESS","lines":["Most mighty duke, behold a man much wrong'd."],"id":648,"m":"Most mighty Duke, look at a man who has been badly wronged."},{"t":"sd","text":"All gather to see them.","id":649},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["I see two husbands, or mine eyes deceive me."],"id":650,"m":"I see two husbands, or my eyes deceive me."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["One of these men is genius to the other;","And so of these, which is the natural man,","And which the spirit? Who deciphers them?"],"id":651,"m":"One of these men is the other's guardian spirit, and the same with these two. Which is the real man, and which the spirit? Who can tell them apart?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I, sir, am Dromio, command him away."],"id":652,"m":"I, sir, am Dromio. Order him away."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["I, sir, am Dromio, pray let me stay."],"id":653,"m":"I, sir, am Dromio. Please let me stay."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Egeon, art thou not? or else his ghost?"],"id":654,"m":"Aren't you Egeon? Or else his ghost?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["O, my old master, who hath bound him here?"],"id":655,"m":"Oh, my old master! Who has tied him up here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ABBESS","lines":["Whoever bound him, I will loose his bonds,","And gain a husband by his liberty.","Speak, old Egeon, if thou be'st the man","That hadst a wife once called Emilia,","That bore thee at a burden two fair sons.","O, if thou be'st the same Egeon, speak,","And speak unto the same Emilia!"],"id":656,"m":"Whoever tied him up, I will untie his bonds and win back a husband by freeing him. Speak, old Egeon, if you're the man who once had a wife called Emilia, who bore you two fine sons at one birth. Oh, if you're that same Egeon, speak, and speak to that same Emilia!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Why, here begins his morning story right:","These two Antipholus', these two so like,","And these two Dromios, one in semblance,","Besides her urging of her wreck at sea.","These are the parents to these children,","Which accidentally are met together."],"id":657,"m":"Why, here his story from this morning begins to come right. These two Antipholuses, so alike, and these two Dromios, identical in looks, besides what she says about her shipwreck: these are the parents of these children, who have met together by chance."},{"t":"sp","who":"EGEON","lines":["If I dream not, thou art Emilia.","If thou art she, tell me where is that son","That floated with thee on the fatal raft?"],"id":658,"m":"If I'm not dreaming, you are Emilia. If you are, tell me, where is the son who floated with you on that fatal raft?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ABBESS","lines":["By men of Epidamnum, he and I","And the twin Dromio, all were taken up;","But, by and by, rude fishermen of Corinth","By force took Dromio and my son from them,","And me they left with those of Epidamnum.","What then became of them I cannot tell;","I to this fortune that you see me in."],"id":659,"m":"He and I and the twin Dromio were all picked up by men from Epidamnum. But soon after, rough fishermen from Corinth took Dromio and my son from them by force, and left me with the men of Epidamnum. What became of them after that I can't tell. I came to the life you see me in now."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Antipholus, thou cam'st from Corinth first?"],"id":660,"m":"Antipholus, you came from Corinth originally?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["No, sir, not I, I came from Syracuse."],"id":661,"m":"No, sir, not me. I came from Syracuse."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Stay, stand apart, I know not which is which."],"id":662,"m":"Wait, stand apart. I don't know which is which."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["I came from Corinth, my most gracious lord."],"id":663,"m":"I came from Corinth, my most gracious lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["And I with him."],"id":664,"m":"And I came with him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Brought to this town by that most famous warrior,","Duke Menaphon, your most renowned uncle."],"id":665,"m":"Brought to this town by that most famous warrior, Duke Menaphon, your much celebrated uncle."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["Which of you two did dine with me today?"],"id":666,"m":"Which of you two had dinner with me today?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I, gentle mistress."],"id":667,"m":"I did, gentle lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["And are not you my husband?"],"id":668,"m":"And aren't you my husband?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["No, I say nay to that."],"id":669,"m":"No, I say no to that."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["And so do I, yet did she call me so;","And this fair gentlewoman, her sister here,","Did call me brother. What I told you then,","I hope I shall have leisure to make good,","If this be not a dream I see and hear."],"id":670,"m":"And so do I, though she called me that, and this lovely lady, her sister here, called me brother. (To Luciana) I hope I'll have time to make good what I told you then, if this isn't a dream I'm seeing and hearing."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["That is the chain, sir, which you had of me."],"id":671,"m":"That is the chain, sir, that you had from me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["I think it be, sir. I deny it not."],"id":672,"m":"I think it is, sir. I don't deny it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["And you, sir, for this chain arrested me."],"id":673,"m":"And you, sir, had me arrested for this chain."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANGELO","lines":["I think I did, sir. I deny it not."],"id":674,"m":"I think I did, sir. I don't deny it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ADRIANA","lines":["I sent you money, sir, to be your bail","By Dromio, but I think he brought it not."],"id":675,"m":"I sent you money for your bail, sir, by Dromio, but I think he didn't bring it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["No, none by me."],"id":676,"m":"No, none by me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["This purse of ducats I receiv'd from you,","And Dromio my man did bring them me.","I see we still did meet each other's man,","And I was ta'en for him, and he for me,","And thereupon these errors are arose."],"id":677,"m":"I received this purse of ducats from you, and my man Dromio brought it to me. I see we kept meeting each other's servant, and I was taken for him, and he for me, and that's how these errors came about."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["These ducats pawn I for my father here."],"id":678,"m":"I offer these ducats as ransom for my father here."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["It shall not need, thy father hath his life."],"id":679,"m":"There's no need. Your father's life is spared."},{"t":"sp","who":"COURTESAN","lines":["Sir, I must have that diamond from you."],"id":680,"m":"Sir, I must have that diamond back from you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["There, take it, and much thanks for my good cheer."],"id":681,"m":"There, take it, and many thanks for my good meal."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABBESS","lines":["Renowned duke, vouchsafe to take the pains","To go with us into the abbey here,","And hear at large discoursed all our fortunes;","And all that are assembled in this place,","That by this sympathised one day's error","Have suffer'd wrong, go, keep us company,","And we shall make full satisfaction.","Thirty-three years have I but gone in travail","Of you, my sons, and till this present hour","My heavy burden ne'er delivered.","The duke, my husband, and my children both,","And you, the calendars of their nativity,","Go to a gossips' feast, and go with me.","After so long grief, such nativity."],"id":682,"m":"Renowned Duke, please be good enough to come with us into the abbey here and hear the full story of all our fortunes. And all of you gathered here who have suffered wrong through the shared errors of this one day, come and keep us company, and we will make full amends. For thirty-three years I have been in labour with you, my sons, and only at this hour have I been delivered of my heavy burden. The Duke, my husband and both my children, and you two, born on the same day as them, come to a christening feast, come with me. After such long grief, such a birth!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["With all my heart, I'll gossip at this feast."],"id":683,"m":"With all my heart, I'll be a godparent at this feast."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt except the two Dromios and two Brothers.","id":684},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Master, shall I fetch your stuff from shipboard?"],"id":685,"m":"Master, shall I fetch your luggage from the ship?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","lines":["Dromio, what stuff of mine hast thou embark'd?"],"id":686,"m":"Dromio, what luggage of mine have you put on board?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Your goods that lay at host, sir, in the Centaur."],"id":687,"m":"Your goods that were left at the inn, sir, at the Centaur."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","lines":["He speaks to me; I am your master, Dromio.","Come, go with us. We'll look to that anon.","Embrace thy brother there, rejoice with him."],"id":688,"m":"He's talking to me. I'm your master, Dromio. Come with us; we'll see to that later. Embrace your brother there, and celebrate with him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Antipholus of Syracuse and Antipholus of Ephesus.","id":689},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["There is a fat friend at your master's house,","That kitchen'd me for you today at dinner.","She now shall be my sister, not my wife."],"id":690,"m":"There's a fat friend at your master's house who served me dinner in the kitchen today, thinking I was you. Now she'll be my sister, not my wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Methinks you are my glass, and not my brother.","I see by you I am a sweet-faced youth.","Will you walk in to see their gossiping?"],"id":691,"m":"I think you're my mirror, not my brother. I can see from you that I'm a good-looking young man. Will you go in and watch the celebrations?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["Not I, sir, you are my elder."],"id":692,"m":"Not me, sir. You're older than me."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["That's a question, how shall we try it?"],"id":693,"m":"That's the question. How shall we settle it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","lines":["We'll draw cuts for the senior. Till then, lead thou first."],"id":694,"m":"We'll draw straws for who's the elder. Till then, you go first."},{"t":"sp","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","lines":["Nay, then, thus:","We came into the world like brother and brother,","And now let's go hand in hand, not one before another."],"id":695,"m":"No, then, like this: we came into the world as brother and brother, so now let's go hand in hand, not one in front of the other."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":696}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"Antipholus of Syracuse","group":"The family of Egeon","desc":"The younger twin, brought up in Syracuse by his father. At eighteen he set out with his servant to find his lost brother and mother, and has been searching for years. He lands in Ephesus on the day the play takes place, pretending to come from Epidamnum to escape the law against Syracusans. Everyone in the town seems to know him: a wife claims him, a goldsmith gives him a chain, strangers hand him money. He decides the town is full of witches and sorcerers, falls in love with Luciana, and spends the day trying to leave by ship. He finally takes refuge in the priory, where his mother is abbess."},{"name":"Antipholus of Ephesus","group":"The family of Egeon","desc":"The elder twin, rescued from the shipwreck by fishermen of Corinth and brought to Ephesus by the Duke's uncle, Menaphon. He fought for the Duke and was rewarded with a rich wife, Adriana. A respected citizen, he is locked out of his own house at dinnertime, dines in anger with a courtesan, promises her the chain he ordered for his wife, is arrested when he refuses to pay for a chain he never received, and is tied up and shut in a dark room as a madman. He escapes and demands justice from the Duke."},{"name":"Dromio of Syracuse","group":"The servants","desc":"Twin of Dromio of Ephesus, bought as a baby to serve Antipholus of Syracuse. Witty and quick with puns, he jokes his way through the beatings he receives for errors that are not his. He keeps the door while the wrong master dines inside, flees from the fat kitchen maid who claims him as her husband, and fetches the bail money for a master he has never seen arrested."},{"name":"Dromio of Ephesus","group":"The servants","desc":"Twin of Dromio of Syracuse and servant of Antipholus of Ephesus. Sent to fetch his master to dinner, he finds the wrong one and is beaten; sent to buy a rope, he is beaten again for not bringing money he never had. He complains that he has received nothing but blows all his life, and is tied up with his master as a madman."},{"name":"Adriana","group":"Ephesus","desc":"Wife of Antipholus of Ephesus. She is angry that her husband is late for dinner and suspects he is unfaithful; she argues with her sister about whether wives must obey. Mistaking his twin for him, she pleads with him passionately and takes him home to dinner, locking out her real husband. When her husband is arrested she sends money for his bail, then has him tied up as a madman. The Abbess tricks her into admitting she has nagged him without end, and blames his 'madness' on her jealousy."},{"name":"Luciana","group":"Ephesus","desc":"Adriana's unmarried sister. She preaches patience and obedience to her sister, and advises the man she believes is her brother-in-law to be discreet if he must be unfaithful. Antipholus of Syracuse falls in love with her; at the end he hopes to make good what he told her."},{"name":"Egeon","group":"The family of Egeon","desc":"An old merchant of Syracuse. Shipwrecked long ago, he lost his wife, one son and one servant; later his other son left to search for them. After years of searching he reaches Ephesus, where any Syracusan must die unless he pays a thousand marks. The Duke gives him until sunset to raise the money. At the end, on the way to his execution, he recognises (as he thinks) his son, but Antipholus of Ephesus does not know him."},{"name":"The Abbess","group":"The family of Egeon","desc":"Emilia, Egeon's lost wife, separated from her husband in the shipwreck and later from her son by fishermen of Corinth. She became abbess of the priory at Ephesus. She shelters Antipholus of Syracuse, lectures Adriana on the harm her jealousy has done, refuses to give him up, and finally brings out the second pair of twins and reveals who she is."},{"name":"The Duke","group":"Ephesus","desc":"Solinus, ruler of Ephesus. Bound by the law against Syracusans, he still pities Egeon and gives him a day to find the ransom. In the last scene he tries to judge the tangle of accusations, decides everyone has 'drunk of Circe's cup', and finally pardons Egeon without payment."},{"name":"Angelo","group":"Ephesus","desc":"A goldsmith of Ephesus who makes the chain Antipholus of Ephesus ordered for his wife. He hands it to the wrong twin, then has the right one arrested for refusing to pay."},{"name":"Balthasar","group":"Ephesus","desc":"A merchant of Ephesus and guest of Antipholus of Ephesus. When his host is locked out, he persuades him not to break the door down, for the sake of his own and his wife's reputation."},{"name":"The Courtesan","group":"Ephesus","desc":"A woman of Ephesus at whose house, the Porpentine, Antipholus of Ephesus dines after he is locked out. She gives him a ring worth forty ducats in exchange for the promised chain. When the other twin calls her a devil and refuses to give her the ring or the chain, she decides he is mad and tells his wife."},{"name":"Pinch","group":"Ephesus","desc":"Doctor Pinch, a schoolmaster and conjurer whom Adriana brings to cure her husband. He tries to drive out the devil he believes possesses him, and has master and servant tied up. They escape and singe off his beard."},{"name":"Luce","group":"The servants","desc":"Luce, Adriana's servant, joins Dromio of Syracuse in mocking Antipholus of Ephesus through the locked door. Dromio of Syracuse describes a huge, greasy kitchen maid, Nell, who claims him as the man promised to her; many editors take her to be Luce."},{"name":"The Merchant","group":"Ephesus","desc":"A merchant who befriends Antipholus of Syracuse when he lands, warns him to say he comes from Epidamnum, and returns his money."},{"name":"The Second Merchant","group":"Ephesus","desc":"A merchant to whom Angelo owes money. About to sail for Persia, he has Angelo arrest Antipholus of Ephesus, and later draws his sword on Antipholus of Syracuse for denying the chain."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"Egeon, a merchant of Syracuse, stands before Solinus, Duke of Ephesus. Because the two cities are at war over trade, any Syracusan found in Ephesus must pay a thousand marks or die, and Egeon has nothing like that sum. Asked why he came, he tells his story: his wife gave birth to identical twin sons at Epidamnum, and he bought a poor woman's identical twin boys to serve them. On the voyage home a storm wrecked the ship; he and his wife tied themselves and the children to a mast, which split on a rock. He, one son and one servant were rescued by one ship; his wife and the other two by fishermen. At eighteen the son he raised went to search for his twin with his servant; Egeon has spent five years searching for them. Moved, the Duke gives him until sunset to raise the money.","key":["The opening is a death sentence: the comedy is framed by real danger and a deadline at sunset.","Egeon's long story gives the audience everything they need: two sets of identical twins, separated, each pair with one name.","The Duke pities Egeon but will not break the law: justice and mercy are set against each other from the start."],"quiz":[{"q":"What must a Syracusan in Ephesus pay to escape death?","o":["A hundred marks","A thousand marks","Five hundred ducats","Nothing; he is simply banished"],"a":1},{"q":"How were the family separated?","o":["Pirates attacked them","The mast they were tied to split on a rock","They were sold as slaves","The father left them in Epidamnum"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Duke grant Egeon?","o":["A pardon","A trial","One day to raise the money","Passage home to Syracuse"],"a":2}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"Antipholus of Syracuse and his servant Dromio have just landed. A friendly merchant warns them to say they come from Epidamnum, since a Syracusan is to die today, and returns their money; Antipholus sends Dromio to lodge it at the Centaur inn. Alone, he compares himself to a drop of water searching the ocean for another drop. Dromio of Ephesus arrives, sent by his mistress to fetch his master home to dinner. Antipholus thinks it is his own Dromio and demands his gold; Dromio talks of a wife and a burning dinner. Antipholus beats him and he runs off. Antipholus concludes the town is full of cheats and sorcerers, and hurries to the inn to check his money.","key":["The first error: master and the wrong servant talk at cross purposes, each sure the other is joking.","Antipholus's 'drop of water' speech gives the comedy a sad centre: he feels he is losing himself in the search for his family.","Money and dinner drive the confusion: one man wants his gold, the other wants him home to eat."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does Antipholus of Syracuse send his money?","o":["The Phoenix","The Centaur","The Porpentine","The Tiger"],"a":1},{"q":"Which Dromio comes to fetch Antipholus to dinner?","o":["Dromio of Syracuse","Dromio of Ephesus","Both Dromios together","Neither; it is Luce"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Antipholus decide about Ephesus?","o":["It is full of cheats and sorcerers","It is the friendliest town he has visited","His brother must live there","It is a good place to settle"],"a":0}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"At home, Adriana is impatient: her husband is late for dinner. Her unmarried sister Luciana says men have more freedom than women and that wives should obey; Adriana mocks the patience of someone who has never been tested. Dromio of Ephesus comes back, ears ringing, and reports that his master denied having a house or a wife and asked only for a thousand marks. Adriana beats him and sends him back. She then complains that her husband neglects her, has lost interest in her looks and wit, and is spending his time with other women; he even promised her a chain and has not given it.","key":["Luciana and Adriana debate marriage: obedience and patience against a wife's right to her husband's love.","Dromio of Ephesus reports, word for word, the conversation of the wrong master, which convinces Adriana her husband is unfaithful.","The chain is mentioned for the first time, as a promised gift Adriana fears will go elsewhere."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Luciana argue?","o":["That women should be free as men","That husbands are masters of their wives","That Adriana should leave her husband","That jealousy is healthy"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Dromio say his master asked for?","o":["His dinner","A thousand marks in gold","His wife","A rope"],"a":1},{"q":"What has Adriana's husband promised her?","o":["A ring","A chain","A new house","A journey"],"a":1}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"Antipholus of Syracuse has found his gold safe at the Centaur. His own Dromio arrives and denies ever mentioning a wife or a dinner, so Antipholus beats him for joking. They make peace and trade puns about time and baldness. Then Adriana and Luciana appear. Adriana reproaches him at length as her husband, saying that his infidelity stains her too, since they are one flesh. Antipholus has never seen her before, yet she knows his name and his servant's. Bewildered, he wonders if he is dreaming or mad, and decides to go along with it. Dromio thinks they have entered fairyland. Adriana takes Antipholus in to dinner and sets Dromio to guard the door and let no one in.","key":["Now the other servant is beaten for the first one's words: violence follows the errors.","Adriana's speech on marriage as the mixing of two drops of water echoes Antipholus's own image from 1.2.","Antipholus chooses to 'entertain the offer'd fallacy': he goes along with the illusion, setting up the lockout."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Antipholus of Syracuse beat his own Dromio?","o":["For losing the gold","For denying the joke about a wife and dinner","For being late","For flirting"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Adriana argue about marriage?","o":["Husband and wife are one, so his infidelity stains her","Marriage is a business deal","She wants a divorce","Women should rule their husbands"],"a":0},{"q":"What job does Adriana give Dromio of Syracuse?","o":["Cook","Porter at the gate","Messenger to the goldsmith","Guard for the chain"],"a":1}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"Antipholus of Ephesus comes home with Dromio of Ephesus, Angelo the goldsmith and Balthasar, a merchant, asking Angelo to excuse his lateness to his sharp-tongued wife by saying he was watching her chain being made. He finds his door locked. From inside, Dromio of Syracuse, then the maid Luce and finally Adriana refuse to let him in and insult him; they believe their master is already at dinner. Antipholus wants to break the door down, but Balthasar persuades him that a public brawl would damage his wife's reputation and his own. Angry, he decides to dine with a courtesan at the Porpentine, and tells Angelo to bring the chain there: he will give it to her, to spite his wife.","key":["The lockout is the play's comic centre: a man shut out of his own house by a servant who looks like his own.","The two Dromios trade insults in rhyming lines through the door, a contest of wit with neither able to see the other.","Out of spite, the chain meant for Adriana is promised to the Courtesan, which sets up the arrests of Act 4."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who refuses to open the door from inside?","o":["Dromio of Syracuse, Luce and Adriana","Luciana and the Courtesan","Pinch","The Abbess"],"a":0},{"q":"Who persuades Antipholus not to break in?","o":["Angelo","Balthasar","Dromio of Ephesus","The Duke"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Antipholus of Ephesus decide to do with the chain?","o":["Sell it","Give it to the Courtesan","Give it to Luciana","Return it to Angelo"],"a":1}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"Outside the house, Luciana scolds the man she thinks is her brother-in-law for neglecting her sister, and advises him that if he must be unfaithful he should hide it. Antipholus of Syracuse declares that he loves her, not her sister, and calls her a mermaid and a siren; shocked, she goes to fetch Adriana. Dromio of Syracuse runs in: a huge kitchen maid claims he is engaged to her, and knows his private marks. He describes her as a globe on which he can find the countries of Europe. Antipholus sends him to find a ship and decides to leave at once. Angelo arrives and hands him the gold chain, saying he will collect the money at supper. Antipholus takes it.","key":["Antipholus falls in love with Luciana, the one person in Ephesus who wants him to go back to his 'wife'.","Dromio's comic account of the kitchen maid mirrors his master's: each is claimed by a woman he has never met.","The chain reaches the wrong twin, and Angelo now believes Antipholus has it."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Luciana advise the man she thinks is Adriana's husband?","o":["To leave Ephesus","To hide any unfaithfulness and be kind to her sister","To divorce Adriana","To confess to the Duke"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Antipholus of Syracuse call Luciana?","o":["A witch","A mermaid and a siren","A shrew","A goddess of war"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Angelo give Antipholus of Syracuse?","o":["A ring","A purse","The gold chain","A letter"],"a":2}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"A merchant about to sail for Persia demands the money Angelo owes him, and an officer stands ready to arrest Angelo. Angelo says Antipholus owes him exactly that sum for the chain. Antipholus of Ephesus arrives, sends his Dromio to buy a rope's end to beat his wife with, and scolds Angelo for not bringing the chain to the Porpentine. Angelo insists he has already given it to him. Neither will back down; the merchant has Angelo arrested, and Angelo has Antipholus arrested. Then Dromio of Syracuse arrives to say a ship is ready to sail. Antipholus, thinking he is his own Dromio, sends him to Adriana for a purse of ducats from his desk, to pay his bail.","key":["The chain now creates a chain of debts: the merchant, Angelo and Antipholus each owe the next.","Both men are honest and both are certain, so the error turns into public disgrace and arrest.","Antipholus sends the wrong Dromio, who has never heard of a rope, to fetch the bail money."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where is the merchant about to sail?","o":["Syracuse","Persia","Corinth","Epidamnum"],"a":1},{"q":"Why is Antipholus of Ephesus arrested?","o":["For breaking into his house","For refusing to pay for the chain","For being a Syracusan","For attacking Pinch"],"a":1},{"q":"What does he send Dromio of Syracuse to fetch?","o":["A rope","A purse of ducats from his desk","The Duke","A ship's captain"],"a":1}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"Luciana tells Adriana how her 'husband' declared his love for her. Adriana calls him every ugly name she can think of, then admits she still loves him. Dromio of Syracuse runs in, out of breath: his master has been arrested by an officer in a leather coat. Adriana sends Luciana for the purse of ducats and gives it to Dromio to bail out her husband.","key":["Adriana's insults turn into a confession: 'My heart prays for him, though my tongue do curse.'","Dromio's comic description of the officer turns the arrest into a hellish nightmare.","The bail money now goes to the wrong Antipholus."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Luciana tell Adriana?","o":["That her husband courted Luciana","That her husband is dead","That the chain is lost","That Dromio stole the gold"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Adriana say of her husband after insulting him?","o":["She will divorce him","She still loves him","She never loved him","She will go to the Duke"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Adriana give Dromio of Syracuse?","o":["The chain","A purse of ducats for bail","A letter for the Duke","A rope"],"a":1}]},"a4s3":{"sum":"Antipholus of Syracuse marvels that everyone in Ephesus greets him by name, offers him money and goods, even measures him for clothes: the town must be full of sorcerers. Dromio of Syracuse brings him the gold for his bail, confusing him further. The Courtesan arrives, sees the chain and asks for it, or for the ring she gave him at dinner. Master and servant call her the devil and a witch, and run away. She concludes Antipholus is mad, and decides to tell his wife that he took her ring by force, so as to get her forty ducats back.","key":["Antipholus's speech shows the strange generosity of Ephesus: he is given everything his brother is owed.","The Courtesan becomes a 'devil' in the eyes of the frightened travellers.","Each error now produces a new explanation: witchcraft for the visitor, madness for the townspeople."],"quiz":[{"q":"What has a tailor done for Antipholus of Syracuse?","o":["Sold him a cloak","Measured him for silks he had bought for him","Refused to serve him","Lent him money"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Courtesan want back?","o":["Her purse","Her ring, or the chain in its place","Her letters","Her necklace"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Courtesan decide to do?","o":["Go to the Duke","Tell his wife he is mad and took her ring","Have him arrested","Forget the ring"],"a":1}]},"a4s4":{"sum":"Antipholus of Ephesus, under arrest, waits for his bail money. Dromio of Ephesus arrives with the rope's end he was sent for, and is beaten for not bringing the money. Adriana, Luciana and the Courtesan arrive with Doctor Pinch, a schoolmaster and conjurer, to cure his madness. Antipholus accuses his wife of locking him out and denying the gold; she insists he dined at home and that she sent the money. Pinch declares master and servant possessed, and they are tied up and taken home to a dark room. Adriana promises the officer she will pay the debt, and learns it is for a chain; the Courtesan says she saw her husband wearing one, though Adriana never has. Then Antipholus of Syracuse and his Dromio come in with swords drawn; everyone runs, thinking the madmen have escaped. The travellers decide to leave Ephesus that night.","key":["Both sides are telling the truth, which makes each look mad to the other.","Pinch's exorcism treats confusion as possession: the play's comic violence reaches its peak.","The two sets of twins come closest to meeting here, separated only by an exit and an entrance."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Dromio of Ephesus bring his master?","o":["The bail money","A rope's end","The chain","A letter from Adriana"],"a":1},{"q":"Who is Doctor Pinch?","o":["A physician from Syracuse","A schoolmaster and conjurer","A priest","The Duke's officer"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does everyone run away at the end of the scene?","o":["The Duke arrives","Antipholus and Dromio of Syracuse enter with drawn swords","A fire breaks out","The officer draws his sword"],"a":1}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"Angelo and the merchant see Antipholus of Syracuse wearing the chain and accuse him of lying; he draws his sword on the merchant. Adriana arrives to take him home as a madman, and he and Dromio flee into the priory. The Abbess refuses to hand him over, and gets Adriana to admit she nagged her husband constantly, which she says has driven him mad. Adriana appeals to the Duke, who is passing on his way to execute Egeon. A messenger reports that her husband has escaped and singed Pinch's beard; then Antipholus of Ephesus himself arrives and demands justice against his wife. Witnesses contradict one another. Egeon recognises his son, but this Antipholus does not know him. Then the Abbess brings out the other Antipholus and Dromio. The twins stand side by side, the Abbess reveals that she is Emilia, Egeon's wife, and the errors are explained. The Duke pardons Egeon, the chain and ring are returned, and everyone goes into the abbey to celebrate. The two Dromios go in last, hand in hand.","key":["The final scene gathers every character in one place, so the errors can only be solved when both pairs of twins are seen together.","The Abbess's lecture blames Adriana's jealousy, though the audience knows the real cause of the 'madness'.","The family's reunion turns the threatened death at sunset into a 'nativity': a second birth after thirty-three years of grief."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where do Antipholus and Dromio of Syracuse take refuge?","o":["The Centaur","The priory","The Porpentine","A ship"],"a":1},{"q":"Who is the Abbess?","o":["Adriana's mother","Emilia, Egeon's wife","The Duke's sister","The Courtesan's aunt"],"a":1},{"q":"How does the play end?","o":["With a wedding in the street","With the two Dromios going in hand in hand","With Egeon's execution","With Antipholus of Syracuse sailing away"],"a":1}]}},"notes":{"2":"The play opens with a death sentence, unusual for a comedy. Egeon's rhyming couplet makes his despair sound formal and final.","3":"Syracuse and Ephesus are trading rivals at war. The law is harsh but even-handed: Syracuse has the same law against Ephesians. A thousand marks was a huge sum.","4":"The 'evening sun' sets the clock for the whole play. Everything happens in one day, as classical comedy required.","6":"Egeon's story is the play's 'exposition': it tells the audience everything they need to follow the errors. Notice that the two servant twins were bought from a poor woman to serve the two sons.","8":"The split mast divides the family exactly in half, a son and a servant with each parent. 'Fortune' is the power that separates, and will reunite, them.","10":"Egeon's 'youngest boy' is Antipholus of Syracuse. Both he and his Dromio took the names of their lost brothers, which is why each pair shares a name.","11":"The Duke cannot pardon Egeon without damaging his honour: the conflict between law and mercy runs through the frame story.","17":"The merchant's warning tells the audience that Antipholus of Syracuse is in the same danger as his father, though neither knows the other is in town.","26":"The 'drop of water' speech is the emotional heart of the play: Antipholus fears that searching for his family will make him lose his own identity.","28":"An 'almanac of my true date' is a calendar: Dromio was born in the same hour as Antipholus, so he is a living record of his age.","29":"The first error. Dromio of Ephesus speaks in a chain of rhyming, linked clauses ('She is so hot because the meat is cold...'), a comic pattern called anadiplosis.","39":"The pun on 'marks' (coins and bruises) is typical of the Dromios: they turn their beatings into wordplay.","45":"Ephesus was famous in the Bible (Acts 19) for its sorcerers and magic books. Antipholus's list prepares for his belief that the town is bewitched.","50":"Luciana's view that men have more liberty than women was the official teaching of the time, set out in sermons on marriage.","56":"Luciana's speech echoes St Paul's letter to the Ephesians (5:22-24), which tells wives to submit to their husbands. Shakespeare sets his play in that very city.","63":"Adriana argues that patience is easy for those who have never been tested. Luciana's 'Well, I will marry one day, but to try' keeps the debate open.","73":"'Horn-mad' means mad as a bull; but horns were also the sign of a cuckold, a man whose wife is unfaithful, which is why Adriana reacts so sharply.","75":"Dromio of Ephesus reports the wrong master's words exactly. The audience knows why; Adriana does not.","83":"A football was a leather ball kicked about roughly in street games. Dromio's complaint captures his place, kicked between master and mistress.","86":"Adriana's long complaint reveals her insecurity: she fears she has grown old and dull, and blames her husband for her lost looks.","88":"The chain first appears here, as a promised gift. Adriana's image of gold worn away by touching suggests reputation damaged by gossip.","104":"Antipholus compares Dromio to a gnat that plays in sunshine but should hide in bad weather: a servant must read his master's mood.","105":"A 'sconce' is a head, but also a small fort; Dromio puns that he will need to 'ensconce' his head to protect it.","125":"The bald 'Father Time' joke was a common image: Time was shown as an old man with a forelock and a bald back of the head.","128":"'Fine and recovery' was a legal procedure for transferring property; Antipholus puns on recovering hair.","150":"Adriana's great speech on marriage. Her image of a drop of water that cannot be taken back out of the sea deliberately echoes Antipholus's 'drop of water' in 1.2: she sees marriage as two people mixed into one.","151":"Antipholus answers in calm, puzzled verse. He has been in Ephesus only two hours.","161":"The elm and the vine were a classical image of marriage: the vine (wife) grows up the strong tree (husband). Adriana fears 'usurping ivy' is choking their marriage.","162":"'Until I know this sure uncertainty, I'll entertain the offer'd fallacy': Antipholus chooses to play along, which causes the lockout.","164":"Dromio thinks they are in fairyland, where spirits pinch mortals black and blue. The play keeps offering supernatural explanations for natural confusion.","174":"Antipholus questions his own sanity in rhyming couplets: 'Known unto these, and to myself disguis'd!'","181":"The goldsmith Angelo's name suggests an 'angel', a gold coin. The Ephesian Antipholus asks him to cover for his lateness: he too has something to hide from his wife.","192":"The doorway scene is in loose rhyming lines of uneven length, an old-fashioned comic verse that suits the slanging match.","202":"The two Dromios meet without seeing each other, divided by the door: each claims the same name.","207":"'Shall I set in my staff?' meant 'shall I settle in here?' Luce answers with another proverb meaning 'never'.","240":"Balthasar's speech on reputation is serious advice in the middle of the farce: slander, once started, can never be undone.","241":"The Ephesian Antipholus decides to give the chain to the Courtesan 'to spite my wife', setting up the whole tangle of Act 4.","247":"Luciana's advice is surprisingly cynical: if you must be unfaithful, at least hide it. Her speech is in alternately rhymed quatrains, like a sonnet.","248":"Antipholus answers in the same form, turning Luciana's advice into a love poem. A siren lured sailors to their death with song; he would gladly drown for her.","250":"'Mated' means bewildered, but also matched or married: Antipholus's pun hints that he would like to be married to Luciana.","258":"Antipholus uses the same language of one-flesh marriage that Adriana used to him: 'mine own self's better part'.","271":"Dromio's story of the kitchen maid parodies his master's experience: both are claimed by a woman who knows them, and both run away.","283":"The globe joke compares the maid's body to a map of the world, with jokes about Ireland's bogs, Scotland's poverty and France's civil wars.","289":"'Making war against her hair' puns on France's war against its heir, Henry of Navarre (1589-93). This is one of the few clues to the date of the play.","297":"The maid knows Dromio's private marks: a mark on his shoulder, a mole and a wart. Recognition by marks is a classic device of romance; here it is played for laughs.","301":"Antipholus calls Luciana a 'mermaid' again. He resolves to resist her song, but he does not leave without her for long.","309":"Angelo hands the chain to the wrong twin. Antipholus's offer to pay now is honest; Angelo laughs it off.","313":"'Here needs not live by shifts': Antipholus finds Ephesus wonderfully generous, while his brother is about to find it cruel.","318":"The chain of debt: the merchant is owed by Angelo, who is owed (he thinks) by Antipholus.","321":"The rope's end is for beating his wife and her 'confederates'. The rope becomes a running joke in Act 4.","332":"Antipholus of Ephesus accuses Angelo of behaving 'like a shrew' who starts the quarrel first.","354":"Dromio of Syracuse has done exactly what his master told him in 3.2: found a ship. He brings the news to the wrong master.","359":"The 'desk that's cover'd o'er with Turkish tapestry' is the kind of exact domestic detail that makes the farce believable.","361":"'Dowsabel' is a mock-romantic name (from the French for sweet and lovely) for the fat kitchen maid.","377":"Adriana's insults are a list of every possible ugliness. Her next speech takes them back.","379":"The lapwing was thought to cry loudly away from its nest to mislead those looking for it. Adriana's tongue says the opposite of her heart.","385":"Dromio describes the arresting officer as a devil. Officers wore buff (leather) coats; 'shoulder-clapper' refers to the tap on the shoulder of an arrest.","395":"The clock striking 'one' after 'two' is Dromio's joke about time running backwards. Time is the play's other great subject.","405":"Antipholus of Syracuse is treated as if he were his brother by the whole town. Lapland was thought to be a home of witches.","409":"The officer wore a buff coat of calfskin, so Dromio calls him 'old Adam' (dressed in skins after the Fall) and the calf killed for the Prodigal Son.","422":"Dromio's mock logic (light comes from fire, fire burns, so 'light' wenches will burn) puns on 'light' meaning immoral and on venereal disease.","426":"A proverb: you need a long spoon to sup with the devil, that is, keep your distance from dangerous people.","434":"The Courtesan's reasoning is logical given what she knows. Each new error produces a new 'explanation' of madness.","457":"Dromio of Ephesus's complaint about a lifetime of beatings is funny, but it also shows how servants were treated.","460":"'Respice finem' (Latin: consider your end) was a solemn motto; Dromio twists it into 'respect your rope's end', the hangman's rope.","464":"Doctor Pinch is a schoolmaster and conjurer: in the 1590s, exorcism of the 'possessed' was a live and controversial subject.","469":"Pinch's exorcism treats confusion as possession by Satan.","485":"Pinch advises humouring a madman by agreeing with him, a common belief about how to treat madness.","493":"Madmen were 'bound and laid in some dark room': this was standard treatment. Malvolio suffers the same in Twelfth Night.","498":"Antipholus of Ephesus is now as violent as the madman he is said to be. The more the truth is denied, the madder he looks.","533":"Dromio of Syracuse sees the good side of Ephesus: everyone speaks kindly and gives them gold.","553":"A priory offered sanctuary: a fugitive could not be taken by force from a holy place.","562":"The Abbess asks the questions a doctor of the mind would ask. Her probing traps Adriana into admitting that she nagged him.","572":"The Abbess blames Adriana's 'jealous fits' for her husband's madness. The audience knows he is not mad at all, so the lecture is both wise and comically misplaced.","585":"Five o'clock: the deadline approaches. The place of execution is right behind the abbey where the reunion will happen.","594":"Adriana gives the Duke a version of the day that is accurate from her point of view, yet completely wrong.","597":"Singeing off Pinch's beard and cutting his hair 'like a fool' was a way of shaming him as a fool.","603":"Adriana thinks her husband is being carried about invisibly: the supernatural explanation reaches even the townspeople.","605":"Antipholus of Ephesus reminds the Duke he saved his life in battle, which explains the Duke's favour to him.","614":"Antipholus's account is completely true, and he insists he is not drunk or mad. Pinch is described as a 'living dead man', a famous comic portrait.","620":"Circe was the enchantress in Homer's Odyssey who turned men into pigs with a magic drink. The Duke's view: everyone is bewitched.","642":"Egeon's lament: he thinks his son is ashamed to know him. In fact, this is the son he has not seen since he was a baby.","650":"Adriana's line sums up the whole comedy: 'I see two husbands, or mine eyes deceive me.'","651":"A 'genius' was a guardian spirit or double. The Duke cannot tell which twin is the real man.","656":"The Abbess reveals she is Emilia, Egeon's wife. She has been at the abbey all along, unknown to her son in the same town.","659":"Emilia explains how she lost her son: men of Epidamnum rescued them all, but fishermen of Corinth took the boys from her.","677":"Antipholus of Syracuse explains the whole muddle in five lines: 'I was ta'en for him, and he for me'.","682":"Emilia's 'thirty-three years' does not quite match the ages given earlier; it may be a slip, but it fits the image of a long labour before a new birth.","691":"The play ends not with lovers but with two servants. Dromio of Ephesus sees himself in his brother as in a mirror.","695":"'Hand in hand, not one before another': the last line replaces the order of masters and servants with equality between brothers."},"gloss":{"doom":"judgement, sentence","partial":"inclined, biased","guilders":"Dutch coins; money","intestine":"internal, civil","jars":"quarrels","synods":"councils","marks":"coins worth two-thirds of a pound; also bruises","levied":"raised (of money)","factor":"agent, business manager","sinking-ripe":"ready to sink","amain":"at full speed","bark":"a ship","hap":"luck, fortune","reft":"robbed, taken away","importun'd":"begged","disannul":"cancel","wend":"go","procrastinate":"put off","confiscate":"confiscated, seized","mart":"market","villain":"rascal (often affectionate); also servant","humour":"mood","confounds":"loses, destroys","almanac":"calendar","capon":"a castrated cockerel; a fool","crupper":"a strap under a horse's tail to keep the saddle in place","pate":"head","maw":"stomach","sconce":"head; also a small fort","flout":"mock","cozenage":"cheating","mountebanks":"quacks, fake doctors","o'er-raught":"cheated, swindled","minions":"darlings, mistresses; also hussies","stale":"laughing-stock; decoy","defeatures":"spoiled features, disfigurements","enamelled":"coated with enamel","horn-mad":"mad as a bull; mad with jealousy","quoth":"said","prating":"chattering","spurn":"kick","loureth":"frowns","carcanet":"a jewelled necklace","excrement":"outgrowth (hair, nails)","niggard":"miser","periwig":"a wig","tiring":"hairdressing","porridge":"soup, stew","aspects":"looks, glances","licentious":"unfaithful, immoral","contaminate":"contaminated","distain'd":"stained (many editions read unstain'd, unstained, which makes better sense)","drone":"a lazy male bee","sot":"fool","shrive":"hear the confession of","fallacy":"illusion, mistaken belief","cates":"delicacies","dainties":"delicacies","churl":"peasant","mome":"blockhead","malt-horse":"a heavy brewer's horse; a dolt","coxcomb":"a fool's cap; a fool","patch":"fool, clown","hatch":"half-door","coil":"fuss, uproar","baggage":"slut, hussy","stocks":"a wooden frame for locking offenders' feet","hind":"peasant, servant","crow":"crowbar; also the bird","ungalled":"unhurt, spotless","Porpentine":"porcupine (the Courtesan's house)","harbinger":"forerunner","attaint":"disgrace, guilt","compact":"made up","siren":"a mythical sea-creature whose song lured sailors to their death","mated":"bewildered; also matched or married","wink":"close the eyes","sir-reverence":"save your reverence, excuse me (said before something improper)","swart":"dark, black","rheum":"mucus, snot","carracks":"large merchant ships, galleons","armadoes":"fleets","privy":"private, secret","curtal":"with a docked tail","hie":"hurry","trudge":"go","shifts":"tricks","Pentecost":"Whitsun, the seventh Sunday after Easter","attach":"arrest","chargeful":"costly","ducats":"gold coins","dalliance":"time-wasting","brook":"put up with","waftage":"passage by sea","fraughtage":"luggage, cargo","balsamum":"balm, healing ointment","aqua-vitae":"strong spirits, brandy","sere":"withered, dry","stigmatical":"marked out by deformity","lapwing":"a bird that cries away from its nest to mislead intruders","limbo":"a region bordering hell; slang for prison","buff":"strong leather, worn by officers","back-friend":"a false friend; one who comes from behind","dryfoot":"by the scent of the foot alone","band":"bond, a written promise to pay","angels":"gold coins","morris-pike":"a large pike, a 'Moorish' spear","hoy":"a small coastal boat","distract":"mad, deranged","avaunt":"get away","spoon-meat":"soft food for babies and invalids","perforce":"by force","respice finem":"(Latin) consider your end","ecstasy":"madness, a fit","doting":"senile, foolish","saffron":"yellow","perdy":"by God","sans":"without","certes":"certainly","suborn'd":"bribed to do wrong","frantic":"mad","peevish":"foolish, stubborn","impeach":"accuse; accusation","forswore":"swore falsely, denied on oath","abject":"wretched, contemptible","halberds":"long-handled axe-spears","puddled":"muddy","mire":"mud","anatomy":"a skeleton","pernicious":"wicked, harmful","Circe":"the enchantress of the Odyssey who turned men into pigs","bondman":"slave, servant","grained":"lined, furrowed","conduits":"channels, veins","genius":"a guardian spirit or double","gossips":"godparents; a gossips' feast was a christening party","nativity":"birth","glass":"mirror","cuts":"straws (drawn for lots)"}};
const PARTS={"3":[[0,7,"Merchant of Syracuse, stop pleading. I am not inclined to bend our laws. Your Duke has lately treated our honest merchants with spiteful cruelty: when they could not find the money to buy back their lives, he sealed his harsh laws with their blood. That hostility and conflict rules out any pity in my face."],[8,19,"Ever since the deadly civil quarrels between your rebellious countrymen and us, it has been decreed in formal councils, by both the Syracusans and ourselves, that there will be no trade between our hostile cities. More than that: if anyone born in Ephesus is seen at the markets and fairs of Syracuse, or if anyone born in Syracuse comes into the bay of Ephesus, he dies, and his goods are seized for the Duke to dispose of, unless a thousand marks are raised to pay the penalty and ransom him."],[20,22,"Your property, valued at the very highest, comes to less than a hundred marks. So by law you are condemned to die."]],"6":[[0,4,"No harder task could be set me than to speak of griefs too great for words. Still, so that the world may see my death was caused by natural love and not by any wicked crime, I will tell as much as my sorrow allows."],[5,17,"I was born in Syracuse, and married a woman who would have been happy if it weren't for me, and would have been happy with me if our luck had not been bad. I lived with her in joy, and our wealth grew through the profitable voyages I often made to Epidamnum. Then my agent there died, and the need to look after goods left unattended drew me away from my wife's loving arms. I had been gone less than six months when she, though nearly fainting under the pleasant burden that women carry, arranged to follow me, and arrived safe and soon where I was."],[18,30,"She had not been there long before she became the joyful mother of two fine sons, and, strangely, the one so like the other that they could only be told apart by their names. In that same hour, at the very same inn, a poor woman gave birth to identical twin boys. Because their parents were extremely poor, I bought them and brought them up to serve my sons. My wife, very proud of our two boys, kept pressing for us to go home. I agreed, unwillingly, and all too soon we went aboard."],[31,43,"We had sailed three miles from Epidamnum before the sea, which always obeys the wind, gave any sign of the disaster to come. But after that we didn't keep much hope, for the dim light the heavens gave us only told our frightened minds that death was near. I would gladly have accepted it, but my wife's constant weeping, as she cried in advance for what she saw was coming, and the pitiful wailing of the babies, who cried because she did without knowing what to fear, forced me to look for ways to delay death for them and me."],[44,56,"This was what happened, since there was no other way out. The sailors escaped in our lifeboat and left the ship, ready to sink, to us. My wife, more anxious about the younger twin, had tied him to a small spare mast of the kind sailors carry for storms, and with him one of the other twins. I had done the same for the other two. With the children arranged like that, my wife and I, keeping our eyes on the ones we cared for, tied ourselves to either end of the mast, and, floating with the current, were carried towards Corinth, or so we thought."],[57,64,"At last the sun shone on the earth and broke up the mists that had troubled us. In that welcome light the sea grew calm, and we saw two ships racing towards us from far off, one from Corinth, the other from Epidaurus. But before they reached us... O, let me say no more! Guess the rest from what came before."]],"8":[[0,8,"O, if the gods had pitied us, I would not now rightly be calling them merciless! Before the ships could get within ten leagues of us, we were driven onto a great rock. We struck it so violently that our helpful mast was split in two, so that in this cruel separation Fortune left each of us something to delight in and something to grieve for."],[9,19,"Her half, poor soul, carrying less weight though no less sorrow, was swept along faster by the wind, and before our eyes the three of them were picked up by fishermen from Corinth, as we thought. At last another ship rescued us. Knowing who they had saved, the crew made us welcome, shipwrecked as we were, and would have taken the others from the fishermen if their ship had not been so slow. So they turned and sailed for home."],[20,22,"Now you have heard how I was parted from my happiness, and how bad luck kept me alive only to tell the sad story of my own misfortunes."]],"10":[[0,7,"My youngest boy, though he is my eldest care, began asking about his brother when he was eighteen. He begged me to let his servant, who was in the same situation, robbed of his brother but keeping his name, go with him to search for him. Because I longed to see the son I had lost, I risked losing the one I loved."],[8,15,"I have spent five summers in the furthest parts of Greece, travelling right through Asia Minor, and coming home along the coast I reached Ephesus. I had no hope of finding them, yet I could not bear to leave this or any other inhabited place unsearched. But here my life's story must end. I would be happy to die now, if all my travels could assure me they are alive."]],"11":[[0,9,"Unlucky Egeon, marked out by the fates to suffer the worst misfortune! Believe me, if it were not against our laws, my crown, my oath and my dignity, which princes cannot cancel even if they wish to, I would plead for you myself. But though you are sentenced to death, and the sentence cannot be withdrawn without great damage to my honour, I will still help you as far as I can."],[10,15,"So, merchant, I give you this one day to save yourself by finding help. Try every friend you have in Ephesus. Beg or borrow to make up the sum, and live. If not, you are condemned to die. Jailer, take him into custody."]],"29":[[0,9,"Back so soon? More like too late. The chicken's burning, the pig's falling off the spit, the clock has struck twelve, and my mistress has struck one on my cheek. She's hot because the food is cold; the food is cold because you haven't come home; you haven't come home because you're not hungry; you're not hungry because you've already eaten. But we who know what it is to fast and pray are being punished for your fault today."]],"45":[[0,7,"Upon my life, by some trick or other the rascal has been cheated of all my money. They say this town is full of trickery: quick-handed conjurors who fool the eye, sorcerers working in the dark who change people's minds, soul-destroying witches who deform the body, cheats in disguise, chattering quacks, and many other kinds of licensed sin."],[8,10,"If that's what's happened, I'll leave all the sooner. I'll go to the Centaur to look for this slave. I'm very afraid my money isn't safe."]],"56":[[0,4,"Why, headstrong freedom is whipped with sorrow. There is nothing under heaven that doesn't have its limits, on earth, in the sea or in the sky. The animals, the fish and the birds are subject to their males and under their control."],[5,10,"Men, who are closer to God and masters of all these, lords of the wide world and the wild seas, gifted with reason and souls and ranked above fish and birds, are masters of their women and their lords. So let your will wait on theirs."]],"63":[[0,5,"Patience that has never been tested! No wonder she can be calm: people find it easy to be meek when they have no cause not to be. When we hear a poor soul bruised by hardship cry out, we tell it to be quiet; but if we carried the same weight of pain, we would complain as much, or more."],[6,9,"So you, with no unkind husband to hurt you, would comfort me by urging a patience that doesn't help. But if you live to see yourself wronged the same way, this foolish patience of yours will be dropped."]],"75":[[0,3,"I don't mean mad like a man with a cheating wife, but he's certainly stark mad. When I asked him to come home to dinner, he asked me for a thousand marks in gold."],[4,10,"\"It's dinnertime,\" I said. \"My gold,\" he said. \"Your meat is burning,\" I said. \"My gold,\" he said. \"Will you come home?\" I said. \"My gold,\" he said. \"Where are the thousand marks I gave you, villain?\" \"The pig is burnt,\" I said. \"My gold,\" he said. \"My mistress, sir,\" I said. \"Hang your mistress! I don't know your mistress. To hell with your mistress!\""]],"86":[[0,8,"His girlfriends must be enjoying his company, while I'm at home starving for one kind look from him. Has plain old age taken the attractive beauty from my poor cheek? Then he's the one who wasted it. Is my conversation dull, my wit barren? If my lively, sharp talk is spoilt, his unkindness has blunted it more than hard marble would. Do other women's bright clothes lure his affections? That isn't my fault: he controls my money."],[9,14,"What ruins can be found in me that he hasn't ruined? So he is the cause of my lost looks. One sunny look from him would soon restore my faded beauty. But like a deer that won't be fenced in, he breaks out and feeds away from home, and poor I am only his laughing-stock."]],"88":[[0,5,"Only unfeeling fools could put up with such wrongs. I know his eye pays its respects somewhere else, or else what keeps him from being here? Sister, you know he promised me a chain. I'd give up that, the one gift he's holding back, if only he'd stay faithful to his marriage bed."],[6,12,"I see that even the best enamelled jewel loses its beauty. The gold still lasts, though others handle it, but frequent handling will wear even gold away, and so any man with a good name is shamed by falsehood and corruption. Since my beauty can't please his eye, I'll weep away what's left of it, and die weeping."]],"150":[[0,8,"Yes, yes, Antipholus, look like a stranger and frown. Some other mistress gets your sweet looks. I'm not Adriana, or your wife. There was a time when you'd swear without being asked that no words were music to your ear, no sight pleasing to your eye, no touch welcome to your hand, no food tasty to you, unless I spoke, or looked, or touched, or carved it for you."],[9,19,"How is it now, my husband, oh, how is it that you're so estranged from yourself? I call it your self, since you're estranged from me, and I, who am inseparable from you, one body with you, am better than the better part of your own dear self. Ah, don't tear yourself away from me. Know this, my love: you might as easily let a drop of water fall into the breaking sea and take that same drop out again, unmixed, with nothing added or lost, as take yourself away from me without taking me too."],[20,29,"How deeply it would wound you if you heard I was unfaithful, and that this body, dedicated to you, had been defiled by some thug's lust! Wouldn't you spit at me and kick me, throw the word \"husband\" in my face, tear the stained skin off my whore's forehead, cut the wedding ring off my false hand and break it with a vow of divorce? I know you could; so see that you do it."],[30,36,"I am stained with adultery; my blood is mixed with the crime of lust. For if we two are one, and you are unfaithful, then I take in the poison of your flesh and am made a whore by your infection. So keep faith and peace with your true marriage bed; then I live unstained, and you undishonoured."]],"161":[[0,4,"How badly it suits your dignity to put on such a crude act with your slave, egging him on to provoke me when I'm upset! It's wrong enough that you stay away from me; don't add insult to that injury."],[5,12,"Come, I'll hold on to your sleeve. You are an elm, my husband, and I a vine: married to your strength, my weakness shares in it. If anything takes you from me, it's rubbish: parasitic ivy, briars or useless moss that, for lack of pruning, creep in, poison your sap and live off your ruin."]],"181":[[0,4,"Good Signor Angelo, you must make excuses for all of us. My wife gets sharp-tongued when I'm late. Say I stayed with you at your shop to watch her necklace being made, and that you'll bring it to the house tomorrow."],[5,9,"But here's a rascal who'd insist to my face that he met me at the market, that I beat him and demanded a thousand marks in gold from him, and that I denied having a wife and a house. You drunkard, what did you mean by this?"]],"240":[[0,8,"Be patient, sir. Oh, don't do this! You'd be fighting against your own good name and bringing your wife's spotless honour under suspicion. Consider this: your long experience of her good sense, her steady virtue, her maturity and modesty suggest she has some reason you don't know about. Don't doubt, sir, that she'll explain why the doors are shut against you right now."],[9,12,"Take my advice: leave calmly, and let's all go to the Tiger for dinner. Then towards evening come back on your own to find out the reason for this strange lockout."],[13,21,"If you try to break in by force now, at the busiest time of day, people will gossip about it, and the common crowd will make up some story against your reputation, which is still unspoiled. That story could worm its way in and linger over your grave when you are dead. For slander passes from one generation to the next, and lives for ever wherever it takes hold."]],"241":[[0,7,"You've won. I'll leave quietly and, though I'm in no mood for fun, I mean to be merry. I know a woman who's an excellent talker, pretty and witty, lively and yet gentle too. We'll eat there. My wife has often nagged me about this woman, though I swear without reason. We'll go to her for dinner."],[8,14,"(To Angelo) Go home and fetch the chain; I know it must be made by now. Please bring it to the Porcupine, because that's the house. I'll give that chain to the hostess there, if only to spite my wife. Hurry, good sir. Since my own doors refuse to welcome me, I'll knock somewhere else and see if they'll turn me away."]],"247":[[0,7,"Can it really be that you have completely forgotten a husband's duty? Antipholus, will your love's young shoots rot in the very spring of love? Will love fall into ruins while it's still being built? If you married my sister for her money, then for her money's sake treat her more kindly. Or if you love someone else, do it in secret: hide your false love behind some sort of blindfold."],[8,15,"Don't let my sister read it in your eyes; don't let your tongue announce your own shame. Look sweet, speak kindly, make disloyalty look good; dress vice up as if it came before virtue. Look decent even if your heart is tainted. Teach sin to behave like a holy saint. Be false in secret. Why does she need to know? What foolish thief boasts about his own crime?"],[16,23,"It's doubly wrong to be unfaithful to your bed and let her read it in your face at the table. Shame, if handled well, can pass for a kind of good name; bad deeds are made twice as bad by bad words. Alas, poor women! Since we are made to trust, just let us believe you love us. Even if others get your arm, let us have the sleeve. We turn as you move; you can sway us."],[24,27,"So, gentle brother, go back inside. Comfort my sister, cheer her up, call her your wife. It's a holy game to tell a few white lies when sweet flattery puts an end to quarrelling."]],"248":[[0,7,"Sweet lady, I don't know what your name is, nor by what miracle you know mine. Your knowledge and grace make you nothing less than a wonder of this earth, more divine than earthly. Teach me, dear creature, how to think and speak. Explain to my dull, earthly mind, lost in errors, feeble, shallow and weak, the hidden meaning of your puzzling words."],[8,15,"Why are you trying to make my soul's plain truth wander off into unknown territory? Are you a goddess? Would you make me into someone new? Then transform me, and I'll give in to your power. But if I am who I am, then I know very well that your weeping sister is no wife of mine, and I owe her bed no loyalty. Far more, far more, I'm drawn to you."],[16,23,"Oh, don't lure me with your song, sweet mermaid, to drown me in your sister's flood of tears. Sing for yourself, siren, and I'll adore you. Spread your golden hair over the silver waves, and I'll take it for my bed and lie there, and in that glorious dream think that a man who can die like that gains by dying. Let love, which is light, drown if it sinks!"]],"405":[[0,5,"Every man I meet greets me as if I were a close friend, and everyone calls me by my name. Some offer me money, some invite me to their homes, others thank me for kindnesses, some offer me goods to buy."],[6,10,"Just now a tailor called me into his shop, showed me silks he had bought for me, and took my measurements as well. Surely these are imaginary tricks, and Lapland sorcerers live here."]],"434":[[0,4,"Now there's no doubt Antipholus is mad, or he would never behave like this. He has a ring of mine worth forty ducats, and in return he promised me a chain. Now he denies me both."],[5,10,"Besides this present outburst, what makes me think he's mad is a mad story he told at dinner today about his own doors being shut against him. I suppose his wife, knowing about his fits, shut the doors against him on purpose."],[11,15,"My plan now is to hurry to his house and tell his wife that, in a fit of lunacy, he rushed into my house and took my ring by force. This is the best course to take, because forty ducats is too much to lose."]],"543":[[0,2,"It is, and wearing round his neck the very chain he so outrageously swore he never had. Good sir, come closer to me. I'll speak to him."],[3,12,"Signor Antipholus, I'm amazed that you would put me to such shame and trouble, and not without some scandal to yourself, by denying so elaborately and with such oaths this chain that you're now wearing so openly. Besides the cost, the shame and the arrest, you've wronged my honest friend here, who would have set sail and put out to sea today if he hadn't stayed for our dispute. You had this chain from me: can you deny it?"]],"572":[[0,8,"And that's how the man came to be mad. The venomous ranting of a jealous woman poisons more deadly than a mad dog's bite. It seems your nagging kept him from sleeping, and that's why his head is light. You say his food was seasoned with your scolding. Disturbed meals make for bad digestion; that breeds the raging fire of fever, and what is fever but a fit of madness?"],[9,18,"You say your quarrelling spoiled his pleasures. When sweet recreation is barred, what follows but moody, dull melancholy, close relative of grim, comfortless despair, followed by a huge infectious troop of pale diseases that are enemies to life? Being disturbed in food, in pleasure and in life-giving sleep would drive man or beast mad. The result is that your jealous fits have frightened your husband out of his wits."]],"594":[[0,8,"May it please your grace: Antipholus, my husband, whom I made lord of myself and all I owned at your urgent request, was seized by a most outrageous fit of madness on this bad day. He rushed wildly through the street, with his servant just as mad as he, offending the citizens by bursting into their houses and carrying off rings, jewels, anything his rage fancied."],[9,19,"Once I had him tied up and sent home, while I went to settle the damage his fury had done here and there. Then, by I don't know what violent escape, he broke away from the men guarding him, and he and his mad servant, both furious, with drawn swords, met us again, came madly at us and chased us away, until we raised more help and came back to tie them up. Then they fled into this abbey, where we chased them."],[20,24,"And here the abbess shuts the gates on us and won't let us fetch him out, or send him out so we can take him away. So, most gracious Duke, at your command let him be brought out and taken away for help."]],"597":[[0,5,"Oh mistress, mistress, run and save yourself! My master and his man have both broken loose, beaten the maids one after another and tied up the doctor. They've singed his beard off with burning sticks, and every time it caught fire they threw great buckets of muddy water on him to put his hair out."],[6,9,"My master preaches patience to him, while his man cuts his hair with scissors like a fool's. Surely, unless you send help at once, between them they'll kill the conjuror."]],"614":[[0,9,"My lord, I know what I'm saying. I'm not affected by wine, or rash and hot-headed with raging anger, though my wrongs might drive a wiser man mad. This woman locked me out from dinner today. That goldsmith there, if he weren't in league with her, could confirm it, because he was with me then. He left me to fetch a chain, promising to bring it to the Porcupine, where Balthasar and I had dinner together."],[10,18,"When dinner was over and he hadn't come, I went to look for him. I met him in the street, with that gentleman in his company. There this lying goldsmith swore to my face that I had received the chain from him today, which, God knows, I never saw. For that he had me arrested by an officer. I obeyed, and sent my slave home for some ducats. He came back with none."],[19,32,"Then I politely asked the officer to come with me to my house himself. On the way we met my wife, her sister and a whole rabble of vile accomplices. They brought along one Pinch, a hungry, lean-faced villain, a walking skeleton, a quack, a threadbare conjurer and fortune-teller, a needy, hollow-eyed, sharp-faced wretch, a living dead man. This poisonous slave, if you please, set himself up as an exorcist, and gazing into my eyes, feeling my pulse, and staring me down with his non-existent face, as it were, cried out that I was possessed."],[33,40,"Then they all fell on me together, tied me up, carried me off, and left me and my man tied together in a dark, damp cellar at home, until I gnawed through my bonds with my teeth, won my freedom and ran straight here to your grace. I beg you to give me full satisfaction for these deep humiliations and great insults."]],"642":[[0,3,"Not know my voice! Oh, cruel time, have you so cracked and split my poor voice in seven short years that my only son here doesn't know my feeble, worn-out tone?"],[4,11,"Though my lined face is now hidden under the drizzling snow of winter, which dries up life, and all the channels of my blood are frozen, my life's night still has some memory, my dimming eyes some glimmer of light, my dull, deaf ears a little hearing. All these old witnesses tell me, and I can't be wrong, that you are my son Antipholus."]],"682":[[0,6,"Renowned Duke, please be good enough to come with us into the abbey here and hear the full story of all our fortunes. And all of you gathered here who have suffered wrong through the shared errors of this one day, come and keep us company, and we will make full amends."],[7,13,"For thirty-three years I have been in labour with you, my sons, and only at this hour have I been delivered of my heavy burden. The Duke, my husband and both my children, and you two, born on the same day as them, come to a christening feast, come with me. After such long grief, such a birth!"]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"identity":["Identity and doubles","Twins, names and mirrors: what makes you yourself when someone else has your face and your name?"],"marriage":["Marriage and jealousy","Adriana and Luciana's debate, the one-flesh ideal, and a wife's fear of losing her husband."],"madness":["Madness and witchcraft","Sorcerers, witches, devils and exorcists: the explanations people reach for when the world stops making sense."],"money":["Money, debt and the chain","Marks, ducats, a purse and a gold chain: in a trading city, errors become debts and arrests."],"servants":["Masters and servants","Beatings, puns and complaints: the Dromios suffer for their masters' confusion."],"time":["Time and the deadline","A death sentence at sunset, a dinner at noon, and a clock that runs backwards."],"family":["Family, loss and reunion","A shipwreck, a long search and a final 'nativity' when the family is made whole."],"wit":["Wit and wordplay","Puns, rhyming insults and mock logic: language as a weapon and a game."]},"quotes":[{"id":"2","who":"EGEON","text":"Proceed, Solinus, to procure my fall, And by the doom of death end woes and all.","themes":["family","time"],"note":"The comedy begins with a man who wants to die."},{"id":"3","who":"DUKE","text":"Therefore by law thou art condemn'd to die.","themes":["money","time"],"note":"The law, based on trade war, puts a price on a man's life."},{"id":"4","who":"EGEON","text":"My woes end likewise with the evening sun.","themes":["time","family"],"note":"Sets the deadline: the play must resolve before sunset."},{"id":"6","who":"EGEON","text":"As could not be distinguish'd but by names.","themes":["identity","family"],"note":"The twins can only be told apart by their names, and later even their names are the same."},{"id":"6","who":"EGEON","text":"Gather the sequel by that went before.","themes":["family"],"note":"Egeon cannot bear to tell the worst part of the story."},{"id":"7","who":"DUKE","text":"For we may pity, though not pardon thee.","themes":["time","family"],"note":"The Duke's dilemma: mercy against law."},{"id":"8","who":"EGEON","text":"What to delight in, what to sorrow for.","themes":["family"],"note":"Fortune split the family exactly in half."},{"id":"10","who":"EGEON","text":"I hazarded the loss of whom I lov'd.","themes":["family"],"note":"To find one son, Egeon risked losing the other."},{"id":"13","who":"EGEON","text":"Hopeless and helpless doth Egeon wend,","themes":["family","time"],"note":"The frame story ends Act 1 in despair."},{"id":"18","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"For with long travel I am stiff and weary.","themes":["family"],"note":"Antipholus of Syracuse arrives exhausted by years of searching."},{"id":"21","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"Lightens my humour with his merry jests.","themes":["servants","wit"],"note":"A relationship of affection between master and servant, soon tested."},{"id":"26","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"He that commends me to mine own content Commends me to the thing I cannot get.","themes":["identity","family"],"note":"He cannot be content until his family is found."},{"id":"26","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"I to the world am like a drop of water That in the ocean seeks another drop,","themes":["identity","family"],"note":"The play's central image of identity: lost in the search for its other half."},{"id":"26","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"In quest of them, unhappy, lose myself.","themes":["identity","family"],"note":"Searching for others, he fears losing himself."},{"id":"28","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"Here comes the almanac of my true date.","themes":["identity","time","servants"],"note":"Dromio, born in the same hour, is a living calendar. Ironically, this is the wrong Dromio."},{"id":"29","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","text":"The capon burns, the pig falls from the spit;","themes":["time","wit"],"note":"Domestic comedy: the dinner is spoiling."},{"id":"29","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","text":"She is so hot because the meat is cold;","themes":["marriage","wit"],"note":"A chain of cause and effect in rhyme: the comic logic of the household."},{"id":"39","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","text":"I have some marks of yours upon my pate,","themes":["servants","wit","money"],"note":"The pun turns coins into bruises."},{"id":"45","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"They say this town is full of cozenage,","themes":["madness"],"note":"Antipholus starts to explain the errors as trickery and magic."},{"id":"45","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"Soul-killing witches that deform the body,","themes":["madness"],"note":"His fears of Ephesus are vivid and supernatural."},{"id":"50","who":"LUCIANA","text":"A man is master of his liberty;","themes":["marriage"],"note":"Luciana's view of men's freedom in marriage."},{"id":"52","who":"LUCIANA","text":"Because their business still lies out o' door.","themes":["marriage"],"note":"Men's world is outside the home, women's inside."},{"id":"55","who":"ADRIANA","text":"There's none but asses will be bridled so.","themes":["marriage","wit"],"note":"Adriana refuses the idea that a wife must be bridled."},{"id":"56","who":"LUCIANA","text":"Are masters to their females, and their lords:","themes":["marriage"],"note":"Luciana's argument from the order of nature."},{"id":"60","who":"LUCIANA","text":"Ere I learn love, I'll practise to obey.","themes":["marriage"],"note":"Obedience before love: Luciana's theory, untested."},{"id":"63","who":"ADRIANA","text":"They can be meek that have no other cause.","themes":["marriage"],"note":"Patience is easy when you have never suffered."},{"id":"67","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","text":"Nay, he's at two hands with me, and that my two ears can witness.","themes":["servants","wit"],"note":"The beating told as a pun."},{"id":"73","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","text":"Why, mistress, sure my master is horn-mad.","themes":["madness","marriage"],"note":"The first suggestion of madness, with a hint of cuckoldry."},{"id":"77","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","text":"\"I know,\" quoth he, \"no house, no wife, no mistress.\"","themes":["identity","marriage"],"note":"The wrong master's words, reported to the right wife."},{"id":"83","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","text":"That like a football you do spurn me thus?","themes":["servants"],"note":"Dromio kicked between master and mistress."},{"id":"86","who":"ADRIANA","text":"Whilst I at home starve for a merry look.","themes":["marriage"],"note":"Adriana's loneliness in her marriage."},{"id":"86","who":"ADRIANA","text":"And feeds from home; poor I am but his stale.","themes":["marriage"],"note":"She fears she is now only his laughing-stock."},{"id":"88","who":"ADRIANA","text":"Sister, you know he promis'd me a chain;","themes":["money","marriage"],"note":"The chain as a token of love that has not arrived."},{"id":"88","who":"ADRIANA","text":"I'll weep what's left away, and weeping die.","themes":["marriage"],"note":"Adriana's despair at the end of 2.1."},{"id":"89","who":"LUCIANA","text":"How many fond fools serve mad jealousy!","themes":["marriage","madness"],"note":"Luciana links jealousy and madness."},{"id":"104","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"When the sun shines let foolish gnats make sport,","themes":["servants"],"note":"A servant must read his master's mood."},{"id":"109","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","text":"Ay, sir, and wherefore; for they say, every why hath a wherefore.","themes":["wit","servants"],"note":"Dromio turns his beating into a logic game."},{"id":"111","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","text":"When in the why and the wherefore is neither rhyme nor reason?","themes":["wit","servants"],"note":"There is no reason for his beating, as the audience knows."},{"id":"122","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"There's a time for all things.","themes":["time"],"note":"A proverb the play turns into a long joke about time."},{"id":"147","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","text":"Time himself is bald, and therefore, to the world's end will have bald followers.","themes":["time","wit"],"note":"Father Time as a bald old man."},{"id":"150","who":"ADRIANA","text":"Ay, ay, Antipholus, look strange and frown,","themes":["marriage","identity"],"note":"She sees strangeness where there is a real stranger."},{"id":"150","who":"ADRIANA","text":"I am not Adriana, nor thy wife.","themes":["identity","marriage"],"note":"Adriana fears she has lost her identity as his wife."},{"id":"150","who":"ADRIANA","text":"A drop of water in the breaking gulf,","themes":["identity","marriage"],"note":"Echoes Antipholus's drop of water: for her, the drop is marriage."},{"id":"150","who":"ADRIANA","text":"As take from me thyself, and not me too.","themes":["marriage","identity"],"note":"Husband and wife are one; to leave her is to tear himself apart."},{"id":"150","who":"ADRIANA","text":"I do digest the poison of thy flesh,","themes":["marriage"],"note":"His infidelity would poison her too."},{"id":"151","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"In Ephesus I am but two hours old,","themes":["identity","time"],"note":"The audience knows exactly why he does not know her."},{"id":"161","who":"ADRIANA","text":"Thou art an elm, my husband, I a vine,","themes":["marriage"],"note":"A classical image of a wife supported by her husband."},{"id":"162","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"What, was I married to her in my dream?","themes":["identity","madness"],"note":"Antipholus begins to doubt his own mind."},{"id":"162","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"What error drives our eyes and ears amiss?","themes":["identity","madness"],"note":"The word 'error' names the play."},{"id":"162","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"I'll entertain the offer'd fallacy.","themes":["identity"],"note":"He chooses to play along with the illusion."},{"id":"164","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","text":"This is the fairy land; O spite of spites!","themes":["madness"],"note":"Dromio explains the errors by fairy magic."},{"id":"166","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","text":"I am transformed, master, am I not?","themes":["identity","madness"],"note":"Has he become someone else?"},{"id":"174","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"Am I in earth, in heaven, or in hell? Sleeping or waking, mad, or well-advis'd?","themes":["identity","madness"],"note":"Antipholus's bewilderment at its height."},{"id":"174","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"Known unto these, and to myself disguis'd!","themes":["identity"],"note":"Everyone knows him except himself."},{"id":"181","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","text":"My wife is shrewish when I keep not hours.","themes":["marriage","time"],"note":"The Ephesian husband's view of his wife."},{"id":"182","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","text":"If the skin were parchment, and the blows you gave were ink,","themes":["servants","wit"],"note":"His bruises are a written record of his beatings."},{"id":"193","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","text":"Mome, malt-horse, capon, coxcomb, idiot, patch!","themes":["wit","servants"],"note":"A flood of insults through the locked door."},{"id":"200","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","text":"What art thou that keep'st me out from the house I owe?","themes":["identity"],"note":"A man locked out of his own home and identity."},{"id":"202","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","text":"O villain, thou hast stolen both mine office and my name;","themes":["identity","servants"],"note":"Dromio's identity has been taken over by another."},{"id":"240","who":"BALTHASAR","text":"For slander lives upon succession,","themes":["marriage"],"note":"Reputation, once damaged, can never be repaired."},{"id":"241","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","text":"(Be it for nothing but to spite my wife)","themes":["marriage","money"],"note":"The chain becomes a weapon in a marriage quarrel."},{"id":"247","who":"LUCIANA","text":"Shall love, in building, grow so ruinous?","themes":["marriage"],"note":"Luciana's image of love as a building in ruins."},{"id":"247","who":"LUCIANA","text":"Muffle your false love with some show of blindness.","themes":["marriage"],"note":"Her surprising advice: hide your infidelity."},{"id":"248","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"Are you a god? would you create me new?","themes":["identity"],"note":"He would be remade by love."},{"id":"248","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"But if that I am I, then well I know","themes":["identity"],"note":"His one certainty in a confusing world."},{"id":"248","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"Sing, siren, for thyself, and I will dote;","themes":["marriage","madness"],"note":"Luciana as an enchantress."},{"id":"250","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"Not mad, but mated; how, I do not know.","themes":["madness","wit"],"note":"A pun on bewildered and married."},{"id":"258","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"It is thyself, mine own self's better part,","themes":["identity","marriage"],"note":"He uses Adriana's language of marriage to woo Luciana."},{"id":"265","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","text":"Do you know me, sir? Am I Dromio? Am I your man? Am I myself?","themes":["identity","servants"],"note":"Dromio's comic crisis of identity."},{"id":"267","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","text":"I am an ass, I am a woman's man, and besides myself.","themes":["identity","wit"],"note":"'Beside myself' means out of my wits."},{"id":"283","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","text":"She is spherical, like a globe. I could find out countries in her.","themes":["wit"],"note":"The geography joke, the most famous prose passage in the play."},{"id":"297","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","text":"she had transformed me to a curtal dog, and made me turn i' the wheel.","themes":["madness","servants"],"note":"Dromio imagines being turned into an animal by witchcraft."},{"id":"298","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"If everyone knows us, and we know none,","themes":["identity"],"note":"The stranger's dilemma in one line."},{"id":"301","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"There's none but witches do inhabit here,","themes":["madness"],"note":"His explanation for everything."},{"id":"301","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"I'll stop mine ears against the mermaid's song.","themes":["marriage","madness"],"note":"He tries to resist his love for Luciana."},{"id":"313","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"there's no man is so vain That would refuse so fair an offer'd chain.","themes":["money"],"note":"He accepts the chain; the error passes on."},{"id":"313","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"When in the streets he meets such golden gifts.","themes":["money"],"note":"Ephesus seems generous to the stranger."},{"id":"322","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","text":"I buy a thousand pound a year! I buy a rope!","themes":["servants","money"],"note":"Dromio's joke: the rope's end will be for him."},{"id":"343","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","text":"You gave me none. You wrong me much to say so.","themes":["money","identity"],"note":"Both men tell the truth; both are sure the other lies."},{"id":"352","who":"ANGELO","text":"Sir, sir, I shall have law in Ephesus,","themes":["money"],"note":"The law is a weapon in disputes of trade."},{"id":"379","who":"ADRIANA","text":"My heart prays for him, though my tongue do curse.","themes":["marriage"],"note":"Love beneath the anger."},{"id":"385","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","text":"One that, before the judgement, carries poor souls to hell.","themes":["madness","money","wit"],"note":"The officer as a devil."},{"id":"395","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","text":"It was two ere I left him, and now the clock strikes one.","themes":["time","wit"],"note":"Time running backwards."},{"id":"399","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","text":"Time is a very bankrupt, and owes more than he's worth to season.","themes":["time","money","wit"],"note":"Time joins the chain of debt."},{"id":"401","who":"ADRIANA","text":"Conceit, my comfort and my injury.","themes":["marriage"],"note":"Imagination both soothes and hurts her."},{"id":"405","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"And everyone doth call me by my name.","themes":["identity"],"note":"His name belongs to another man in this town."},{"id":"405","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"And Lapland sorcerers inhabit here.","themes":["madness"],"note":"Witchcraft as the explanation."},{"id":"415","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","text":"the bark Expedition put forth tonight, and then were you hindered by the sergeant to tarry for the hoy Delay.","themes":["time","wit"],"note":"Ships named for speed and slowness."},{"id":"416","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"And here we wander in illusions.","themes":["madness","identity"],"note":"The travellers lost in a world of illusion."},{"id":"419","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"Satan, avoid! I charge thee, tempt me not.","themes":["madness"],"note":"The Courtesan is seen as the devil."},{"id":"426","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","text":"Marry, he must have a long spoon that must eat with the devil.","themes":["madness","wit"],"note":"A proverb turned into a joke."},{"id":"434","who":"COURTESAN","text":"Now, out of doubt Antipholus is mad,","themes":["madness"],"note":"The townspeople's explanation: madness."},{"id":"457","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","text":"When I am cold, he heats me with beating; when I am warm he cools me with beating.","themes":["servants"],"note":"A servant's lifetime of blows."},{"id":"469","who":"PINCH","text":"I charge thee, Satan, hous'd within this man,","themes":["madness"],"note":"Exorcism as a cure for confusion."},{"id":"470","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","text":"Peace, doting wizard, peace; I am not mad.","themes":["madness","identity"],"note":"The sane man cannot prove his sanity."},{"id":"493","who":"PINCH","text":"They must be bound and laid in some dark room.","themes":["madness"],"note":"The standard treatment of madmen."},{"id":"533","who":"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","text":"I could find in my heart to stay here still and turn witch.","themes":["madness","wit"],"note":"Dromio sees the attractions of a bewitched town."},{"id":"572","who":"ABBESS","text":"The venom clamours of a jealous woman Poisons more deadly than a mad dog's tooth.","themes":["marriage","madness"],"note":"The Abbess blames Adriana's jealousy."},{"id":"572","who":"ABBESS","text":"Hath scar'd thy husband from the use of's wits.","themes":["marriage","madness"],"note":"Her diagnosis, wise in general but wrong in fact."},{"id":"574","who":"ADRIANA","text":"She did betray me to my own reproof.","themes":["marriage"],"note":"Adriana sees she has been tricked into accusing herself."},{"id":"577","who":"ABBESS","text":"He took this place for sanctuary,","themes":["family"],"note":"The son unknowingly takes refuge with his mother."},{"id":"603","who":"ADRIANA","text":"That he is borne about invisible.","themes":["madness"],"note":"The supernatural explanation reaches the townspeople."},{"id":"614","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","text":"A living dead man. This pernicious slave,","themes":["madness","wit"],"note":"The portrait of Doctor Pinch."},{"id":"620","who":"DUKE","text":"I think you all have drunk of Circe's cup.","themes":["madness","identity"],"note":"Everyone seems bewitched."},{"id":"642","who":"EGEON","text":"Not know my voice! O time's extremity,","themes":["time","family"],"note":"Egeon thinks time has made him unrecognisable."},{"id":"650","who":"ADRIANA","text":"I see two husbands, or mine eyes deceive me.","themes":["identity","marriage"],"note":"The moment of revelation."},{"id":"651","who":"DUKE","text":"One of these men is genius to the other;","themes":["identity"],"note":"The twins as doubles."},{"id":"656","who":"ABBESS","text":"And speak unto the same Emilia!","themes":["family"],"note":"The mother reveals herself."},{"id":"677","who":"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","text":"And I was ta'en for him, and he for me, And thereupon these errors are arose.","themes":["identity"],"note":"The whole plot explained."},{"id":"682","who":"ABBESS","text":"After so long grief, such nativity.","themes":["family","time"],"note":"Reunion as a second birth."},{"id":"691","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","text":"Methinks you are my glass, and not my brother.","themes":["identity","servants"],"note":"Twins as mirrors."},{"id":"695","who":"DROMIO OF EPHESUS","text":"And now let's go hand in hand, not one before another.","themes":["servants","identity","family"],"note":"The play ends with equality between brothers."}]};
const EX={"intro":["Egeon, an old merchant of Syracuse, is condemned to death in Ephesus, an enemy city, unless he can pay a thousand marks by sunset. He explains that long ago a shipwreck split his family: he kept one of his identical twin sons and one of their identical twin servants, both called Dromio, while his wife and the other two were lost. His son has since gone looking for his twin, and Egeon has followed.","Without knowing it, that son, Antipholus of Syracuse, and his Dromio have just arrived in Ephesus, where the lost twins live: Antipholus of Ephesus, a respected citizen married to Adriana, and his Dromio. Over a single afternoon, everyone mistakes one twin for the other. Servants are beaten for messages they never carried, a husband is locked out of his own house while his brother dines with his wife, a gold chain goes to the wrong man, and the wrong man is arrested for not paying for it.","The visitors decide Ephesus is full of witches; the townspeople decide the Ephesian Antipholus is mad, and have him tied up and exorcised. The visitors take refuge in a priory. At five o'clock, as Egeon is led to execution, both pairs of twins finally stand side by side before the Duke, the Abbess reveals she is Egeon's lost wife Emilia, Egeon is pardoned, and the family is reunited."],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"Egeon is condemned to die unless he pays a thousand marks","id":3},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Egeon tells the story of the shipwreck","id":6},{"s":"a1s1","t":"The Duke gives him until sunset","id":11},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Antipholus of Syracuse lands and sends Dromio to the Centaur","id":18},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Antipholus compares himself to a drop of water","id":26},{"s":"a1s2","t":"The first error: the wrong Dromio calls him home to dinner","id":29},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Antipholus beats Dromio of Ephesus","id":42},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Adriana and Luciana debate a wife's duty","id":56},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Dromio reports that his master denies his wife","id":77},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Adriana suspects her husband of infidelity","id":86},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Antipholus beats his own Dromio for the other's words","id":101},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Adriana claims Antipholus of Syracuse as her husband","id":150},{"s":"a2s2","t":"He goes in to dinner with her; Dromio guards the gate","id":173},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Antipholus of Ephesus is locked out of his own house","id":200},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Balthasar talks him out of breaking in","id":240},{"s":"a3s1","t":"He decides to dine with the Courtesan and give her the chain","id":241},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Antipholus of Syracuse woos Luciana","id":248},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Dromio of Syracuse flees the kitchen maid","id":283},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Angelo gives the chain to Antipholus of Syracuse","id":309},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Antipholus of Ephesus is arrested for the chain","id":350},{"s":"a4s1","t":"He sends the wrong Dromio for bail money","id":359},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Adriana sends the purse of ducats","id":401},{"s":"a4s3","t":"The Courtesan demands the chain and decides Antipholus is mad","id":434},{"s":"a4s4","t":"Dromio of Ephesus brings a rope instead of money","id":443},{"s":"a4s4","t":"Doctor Pinch tries to exorcise Antipholus of Ephesus","id":469},{"s":"a4s4","t":"Master and servant are tied up and taken away","id":514},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Antipholus of Syracuse takes refuge in the priory","id":553},{"s":"a5s1","t":"The Abbess blames Adriana's jealousy","id":572},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Antipholus of Ephesus demands justice from the Duke","id":614},{"s":"a5s1","t":"The twins meet and the Abbess reveals she is Emilia","id":656}],"irony":{"17":"The merchant describes the condemned Syracusan to Antipholus without knowing he is Antipholus's father.","28":"Antipholus calls Dromio 'the almanac of my true date', but this is the other Dromio.","77":"Dromio reports the words of the wrong master exactly: the audience knows why Adriana's husband 'denies' her.","150":"Adriana tells a stranger that his leaving her would tear him from himself; she is speaking to her husband's twin.","173":"Adriana orders the gate kept shut against everyone, which shuts out her real husband.","193":"Dromio of Syracuse insults the man at the door without knowing it is his master's brother, standing with his own brother.","247":"Luciana advises 'her brother-in-law' to hide his infidelity; he is not married to her sister at all.","309":"Angelo hands the chain to the twin who never ordered it.","343":"Each man accuses the other of lying, and each is telling the truth.","359":"Antipholus of Ephesus sends for bail money with a Dromio who has just booked a ship to leave Ephesus.","434":"The Courtesan concludes Antipholus is mad, though he has never seen her before.","473":"Adriana swears her husband dined at home: she believes it, and she is right about the wrong man.","572":"The Abbess blames Adriana's nagging for her husband's madness, but the man in the priory is not her husband and is not mad.","577":"The Abbess protects a stranger who is, unknown to her, her own son.","606":"Egeon recognises his son, but it is the son he has not seen since the shipwreck.","646":"The Duke says Antipholus has never seen Syracuse: true of this twin, untrue of the other."},"devices":{"magic":"Witchcraft, devils and sorcery","water":"Water, the sea and drowning","money":"Money, debt and trade","animals":"Asses, dogs and beasts","body":"Bodies, beating and possession","time":"Clocks, time and the sun"},"dev":{"4":[["time","The evening sun is Egeon's deadline.","with the evening sun"]],"8":[["water","The sea divides the family.","a mighty rock"]],"26":[["water","Antipholus sees himself as a drop lost in the ocean.","like a drop of water"]],"28":[["time","Dromio is a living calendar of Antipholus's life.","almanac of my true date"]],"33":[["time","Hunger is a clock that tells you when to come home.","should be your clock"]],"39":[["money","Coins become bruises.","marks of yours upon my pate"]],"45":[["magic","Ephesus as a town of magicians and witches.","Dark-working sorcerers"]],"54":[["animals","A wife bridled like a horse.","bridle of your will"]],"55":[["animals","Only donkeys accept a bridle.","none but asses"]],"83":[["body","Dromio kicked like a football.","like a football"]],"86":[["animals","The husband is a deer that breaks out of its park.","too unruly deer"]],"88":[["money","Gold worn away by touching, like a reputation.","the gold bides still"]],"104":[["body","Dromio's head beaten like a fort.","beat this method in your sconce"]],"147":[["time","Father Time is bald.","Time himself is bald"]],"150":[["water","Marriage as a drop mixed into the sea.","A drop of water in the breaking gulf"],["body","Husband and wife share one body and blood.","I do digest the poison of thy flesh"]],"161":[["body","The married couple as elm and vine.","Thou art an elm"]],"164":[["magic","Dromio thinks they have entered fairyland.","This is the fairy land"]],"172":[["animals","Dromio feels he has become a donkey.","I am an ass"]],"248":[["water","Luciana as a mermaid luring him to drown.","sweet mermaid"],["magic","She is a siren, a magical singer.","Sing, siren"]],"283":[["body","The kitchen maid's body as a map of the world.","spherical, like a globe"]],"297":[["magic","The maid as a witch who could change him into a dog.","ran from her as a witch"]],"301":[["magic","Ephesus is full of witches.","none but witches"]],"313":[["money","Golden gifts in the street.","such golden gifts"]],"385":[["magic","The officer as a devil carrying souls to hell.","A devil in an everlasting garment"]],"395":[["time","The clock runs backwards.","now the clock strikes one"]],"399":[["time","Time as a bankrupt and a thief.","Time is a very bankrupt"],["money","Time owes more than he is worth.","owes more than he's worth"]],"405":[["magic","Lapland sorcerers.","Lapland sorcerers"]],"419":[["magic","The Courtesan as Satan.","Satan, avoid!"]],"457":[["body","Beating as the language of service.","heats me with beating"],["animals","Dromio as a donkey.","I am an ass indeed"]],"469":[["magic","Pinch tries to cast out the devil.","I charge thee, Satan"]],"572":[["animals","Jealousy is worse than a mad dog's bite.","a mad dog's tooth"]],"620":[["magic","Circe's magic cup.","drunk of Circe's cup"]],"642":[["time","Time has disfigured Egeon.","O time's extremity"]]},"stage":{"palace":"A hall in the Duke's palace","mart":"The market in Ephesus","street":"A public place in Ephesus","house":"Before the house of Antipholus of Ephesus (the Phoenix)","room":"Inside the house of Antipholus of Ephesus","priory":"A street before the priory, near the place of execution"},"arc":{"Antipholus of Syracuse's confusion":[0,4,4,8,8,9,9,9,10,10,2],"Adriana's suspicion":[0,0,7,8,5,5,5,9,9,10,2]},"arcnote":"Antipholus of Syracuse's confusion starts when the wrong Dromio talks of a wife and a dinner, jumps when a stranger claims him as her husband, and keeps rising as he is given a chain, gold and clothes he never ordered, until he runs into the priory. Adriana's suspicion starts high at home, where her husband is late and seems to deny her; it eases when she has 'him' at dinner, rises sharply when Luciana reports that he courted her, and peaks when he is said to be mad and violent. Both fall away in the final scene when the twins are seen together. Values are an estimate for discussion, not a count.","context":[["Date","Probably written in the early 1590s, among Shakespeare's earliest plays. The first recorded performance was at Gray's Inn, one of the London law schools, on 28 December 1594, during a chaotic Christmas evening the lawyers later called the 'Night of Errors'. It was first printed in the First Folio of 1623."],["Plautus","The main source is the Menaechmi by the Roman comic writer Plautus, about one pair of identical twins in a strange town. Latin comedies were read in Elizabethan grammar schools, and an English translation was published in 1595. Shakespeare doubled the twins by adding the two Dromios."],["Amphitruo","The lockout, where a husband is shut out while another man dines with his wife, comes from another Plautus play, Amphitruo, in which the god Jupiter takes the husband's shape and one of the servants is doubled too."],["Shakespeare's additions","Shakespeare added the frame story of Egeon and Emilia, the shipwreck and the death sentence, Luciana and the debate on marriage, and the abbey. These make the farce part of a story of loss and reunion, a pattern he returned to in late plays such as Pericles."],["Unity of time and place","The play follows the classical rules: one day (from morning to five o'clock), one city and one main action. It is the shortest of Shakespeare's plays."],["Ephesus","Shakespeare moved the setting from Plautus's Epidamnum to Ephesus, known to his audience from the Bible. In Acts 19, St Paul finds Ephesus full of magicians who burn their books; in the letter to the Ephesians he tells wives to submit to their husbands. Both ideas, sorcery and wifely obedience, run through the play."],["Trade and money","Syracuse and Ephesus are at war over trade. The play is full of marks, ducats, angels, guilders, debts, bonds and arrests, the language of a merchant city like London."],["Marriage","Sermons and conduct books taught that a wife must obey her husband, but also that husband and wife were 'one flesh' and that a husband owed his wife love and fidelity. Adriana and Luciana argue both sides."],["Madness and exorcism","In the 1590s there were public arguments about exorcism, and some exorcists were exposed as frauds. The usual treatment for the mad was to bind them and shut them in a dark room."],["Servants","The Dromios were bought as babies, so they are close to slaves. Beating servants was legal and common, though moralists warned against cruelty."],["Farce","The play is a farce: comedy built on fast action, physical violence, coincidence and mistaken identity, rather than on character. No one in the play is in disguise: every error comes from a natural likeness. Some critics have called it an 'apprentice' play, but it is tightly plotted and its frame story gives it real feeling."],["Later life","The play was adapted as the Rodgers and Hart musical The Boys from Syracuse (1938) and is often staged with the twins played by two actors, or with each pair played by one actor."]],"critics":{"identity":[["Harold Brooks, 'Themes and Structure in The Comedy of Errors' (1961)","Paraphrased: Brooks argued that the play is more than a farce, and that its images of transformation and loss of self give it a serious concern with identity."]],"marriage":[["Coppélia Kahn, Man's Estate (1981)","Paraphrased: Kahn read the play as being about growing up, with Antipholus of Syracuse having to find himself through his family before he can love, and Adriana's marriage as a study of the fear of losing oneself in another."]],"family":[["Northrop Frye, A Natural Perspective (1965)","Paraphrased: Frye saw the play's shipwreck and reunion as an early example of Shakespeare's romance pattern, in which a lost family is restored and a new society forms at the end."]],"madness":[["Arthur Kinney, 'Shakespeare's Comedy of Errors and the Nature of Kinds' (1988)","Paraphrased: Kinney linked the play's talk of witchcraft, possession and a final 'nativity' to Christian ideas of redemption, especially those connected with Ephesus."]],"servants":[["Stanley Wells, introduction to the New Penguin edition (1972)","Paraphrased: Wells noted how much of the play's comedy depends on violence against the Dromios, and how their wit lets them answer it."]]},"insults":[{"id":"38","f":"Or I shall break that merry sconce of yours","who":"Antipholus of Syracuse","n":"The first threat: the wrong servant is beaten for the wrong reason."},{"id":"42","f":"take you that, sir knave","who":"Antipholus of Syracuse","n":"A blow for a message he could not understand."},{"id":"45","f":"The villain is o'er-raught of all my money","who":"Antipholus of Syracuse","n":"'Villain' can be affectionate, but here it is an accusation of theft."},{"id":"80","f":"Back slave, or I will break thy pate across","who":"Adriana","n":"Mistress as well as master beats Dromio of Ephesus."},{"id":"82","f":"Hence, prating peasant","who":"Adriana","n":"Adriana's anger at her husband falls on his servant."},{"id":"86","f":"poor I am but his stale","who":"Adriana","n":"Adriana insults herself as her husband's laughing-stock."},{"id":"165","f":"Dromio, thou drone, thou snail, thou slug, thou sot","who":"Luciana","n":"Even gentle Luciana lets fly at a servant."},{"id":"183","f":"I think thou art an ass","who":"Antipholus of Ephesus","n":"Dromio turns the insult into a threat: asses kick."},{"id":"193","f":"Mome, malt-horse, capon, coxcomb, idiot, patch!","who":"Dromio of Syracuse","n":"Six insults in a line, flung through a door at a stranger."},{"id":"214","f":"Thou baggage, let me in","who":"Antipholus of Ephesus","n":"The master insults his own maid through his own door."},{"id":"224","f":"Your wife, sir knave? go, get you from the door","who":"Adriana","n":"Adriana insults her real husband without knowing it."},{"id":"234","f":"out upon thee, hind","who":"Dromio of Syracuse","n":"A servant calls another servant a peasant: the two Dromios fight for status."},{"id":"355","f":"Why, thou peevish sheep","who":"Antipholus of Ephesus","n":"Dromio of Syracuse is called a sheep for doing exactly what he was told."},{"id":"377","f":"He is deformed, crooked, old, and sere","who":"Adriana","n":"A list of insults she does not mean."},{"id":"419","f":"Satan, avoid!","who":"Antipholus of Syracuse","n":"The Courtesan is called the devil."},{"id":"422","f":"she is the devil's dam","who":"Dromio of Syracuse","n":"Dromio goes further: she is the devil's mother."},{"id":"454","f":"Thou whoreson, senseless villain","who":"Antipholus of Ephesus","n":"The master beats his own servant for an error that is not his."},{"id":"470","f":"Peace, doting wizard, peace","who":"Antipholus of Ephesus","n":"He insults the man trying to 'cure' him."},{"id":"498","f":"Dissembling harlot, thou art false in all","who":"Antipholus of Ephesus","n":"The husband calls his faithful wife a whore."},{"id":"614","f":"A mere anatomy, a mountebank","who":"Antipholus of Ephesus","n":"The famous comic portrait of Pinch as a living skeleton."}],"pronouns":[{"id":"32","f":"I am not in a sportive humour now","n":"Antipholus of Syracuse uses 'you' to Dromio when calm..."},{"id":"42","f":"What, wilt thou flout me thus unto my face","n":"...and 'thou' when angry: the shift marks his temper."},{"id":"150","f":"Ay, ay, Antipholus, look strange and frown","n":"Adriana uses intimate 'thou' to a man who has never met her."},{"id":"151","f":"Plead you to me, fair dame? I know you not.","n":"He answers with polite, distant 'you'."},{"id":"247","f":"If you did wed my sister for her wealth,","n":"Luciana moves between 'you' and 'thy' as she switches between scolding and pleading."},{"id":"260","f":"Call thyself sister, sweet, for I aim thee;","n":"Antipholus of Syracuse uses 'thou' to Luciana as a lover."},{"id":"645","f":"I ne'er saw Syracusa in my life.","n":"Antipholus of Ephesus is formal and cold to the father he does not recognise."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["Egeon raises the money","Egeon's son is already in Ephesus"],"a1s2":["Antipholus finds his brother","Antipholus is mistaken for his brother"],"a2s1":["Adriana confronts the wrong man","Adriana forgives her husband"],"a2s2":["The real husband comes home","Antipholus refuses to go in"],"a3s1":["Antipholus breaks down the door","Antipholus goes to the Courtesan"],"a3s2":["Luciana returns his love","The chain reaches the wrong twin"],"a4s1":["Antipholus of Ephesus is arrested","Angelo admits his mistake"],"a4s2":["The bail money reaches the wrong man","Adriana goes to the prison herself"],"a4s3":["The Courtesan tells Adriana he is mad","Antipholus gives back the ring"],"a4s4":["The twins meet face to face","The travellers try to leave Ephesus"]},"contrast":[{"t":"The two Antipholuses","pts":["Antipholus of Syracuse is a wandering searcher, melancholy, who explains the errors as magic.","Antipholus of Ephesus is a settled citizen, quick to anger, who explains them as betrayal.","One finds Ephesus generous and frightening; the other finds his own city turned against him."],"ids":[26,614]},{"t":"The two Dromios","pts":["Dromio of Syracuse is a quick, inventive wit who turns fear into jokes.","Dromio of Ephesus is more downtrodden, complaining of a life of beatings.","At the end they go in together as equals."],"ids":[283,457]},{"t":"Adriana and Luciana","pts":["Adriana claims a wife's right to her husband's love and complains openly.","Luciana preaches obedience and patience, but has never been married.","Each is tested: Adriana is blamed by the Abbess, Luciana is courted by the wrong man."],"ids":[63,56]},{"t":"Adriana and the Abbess","pts":["Adriana is a jealous wife who has lost her husband to suspicion.","Emilia is a wife who lost her husband to the sea and found a new life in the abbey.","The Abbess's lecture on marriage comes from the woman who will turn out to be Adriana's mother-in-law."],"ids":[571,572]},{"t":"Egeon and the Duke","pts":["Egeon has lost everything and wants to die.","The Duke has power but is bound by the law.","The reunion lets the Duke pardon Egeon without breaking the law: the debt is simply cancelled."],"ids":[6,11]}],"keyfacts":[["Full title","The Comedy of Errors"],["Written","Early 1590s; first recorded performance 28 December 1594; first printed in the First Folio of 1623"],["Genre","Comedy; farce of mistaken identity, framed by a romance of loss and reunion"],["Setting","Ephesus, a trading city on the coast of Asia Minor, over a single day"],["Protagonists","The two Antipholuses and the two Dromios"],["Antagonists","Chance and error, the law against Syracusans, and the characters' own quick tempers"],["Inciting incident","Antipholus of Syracuse arrives in Ephesus, where his lost twin lives"],["Turning point","Antipholus of Ephesus is locked out of his house (3.1) and decides to give the chain to the Courtesan"],["Climax","Both pairs of twins appear together before the Duke (5.1)"],["Point of view","The audience knows more than any character: almost every error is dramatic irony"],["Major conflicts","Ephesus against Syracuse; husband against wife; master against servant; each twin against a world that mistakes him"],["Themes","Identity, marriage and jealousy, madness and witchcraft, money and debt, time, family and reunion"]],"analysis":{"ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE":["Antipholus of Syracuse is the play's searcher. His first soliloquy compares him to a drop of water that loses itself while looking for another drop: from the start he fears losing his identity in the quest for his family.","He is affectionate to his Dromio but quick to beat him when he seems to mock him. As the errors mount he decides Ephesus is full of sorcerers and witches, and moves from bewilderment ('Am I in earth, in heaven, or in hell?') to choosing to 'entertain the offer'd fallacy'.","He falls in love with Luciana and woos her in elegant rhymed verse, rejecting the wife who claims him. He is honest throughout: he offers to pay for the chain and admits receiving it.","At the end he finds his brother, his father and his mother, and hopes to marry Luciana. The man who 'lost himself' in 1.2 is restored to himself and to a family."],"ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS":["Antipholus of Ephesus is a settled, respected citizen, a soldier who saved the Duke's life and was rewarded with a rich wife. He is used to being obeyed.","He is late for dinner and has something to hide: he asks Angelo to cover for him with his 'shrewish' wife, and he dines with a courtesan when he is locked out.","Everything that happens to him is a public humiliation: locked out, arrested for debt, called mad, exorcised and tied up. He responds with anger and violence, which makes him look madder.","His account to the Duke in 5.1 is entirely true, yet no one can believe it. His reunion with his family is quieter than his brother's; he offers to pay his father's ransom."],"DROMIO OF SYRACUSE":["Dromio of Syracuse is the wittiest character in the play. He turns beatings into wordplay, as in his debate with his master about time and baldness.","He believes in fairies and witches, and his fear that he has been 'transformed' mirrors his master's.","His description of the kitchen maid as a globe of countries is the play's comic set piece, and also a parody of being claimed by a stranger.","He does everything he is asked, yet is still beaten. At the end he joins his brother as an equal."],"DROMIO OF EPHESUS":["Dromio of Ephesus is the more put-upon of the two servants. His jokes are about blows: his ears, his shoulders, the 'marks' on his head.","His long complaint in 4.4 ('When I am cold, he heats me with beating') sums up a servant's life.","He is faithful to his master even when tied up with him, and confirms the truth of the lockout.","His last lines invite his brother to walk 'hand in hand, not one before another', the most generous words in the play."],"ADRIANA":["Adriana is a wife who feels neglected. She argues with her sister that a wife should not have to bear what a husband would not, and fears she has lost her looks and her husband's love.","Her great speech in 2.2 presents marriage as two people mixed into one, like drops of water in the sea, so that his infidelity would stain her too.","She is loving as well as jealous: she curses her husband and then admits 'my heart prays for him', and she sends money at once to bail him out.","The Abbess blames her nagging for her husband's madness, and she accepts the rebuke. Whether this is fair, when the audience knows the real cause, is one of the play's debates."],"LUCIANA":["Luciana argues for patience and obedience in marriage, quoting the order of nature.","She advises her 'brother-in-law' to hide any affair from her sister, a cynical lesson from a woman who claims to put virtue first.","When the wrong Antipholus declares his love, she is tempted but loyal to her sister, and reports it at once.","She has the play's most romantic future: Antipholus of Syracuse hopes to make good his promises to her."],"EGEON":["Egeon frames the play. His long speech of loss sets the deadline and tells the audience the back-story.","He is a figure of grief and patience, who wants to die but is given one day to live.","In 5.1 his recognition of a son who does not know him is the play's most painful moment.","His rescue by reunion turns the comedy from farce to romance."]},"motifs":[["Twins and doubles","Two Antipholuses and two Dromios with the same names. Mirrors, 'genius' and 'glass' images ask what makes each one himself.",[3,12,57,58,105,106]],["Witchcraft and the devil","Sorcerers, witches, fairyland, Satan, Lapland, Circe's cup: each error is explained by magic.",[18,50,73,86,89,103]],["Madness","The townspeople decide the Ephesian Antipholus is mad, and Pinch tries to exorcise him.",[27,91,93,94,95,97]],["The chain","A gift promised to Adriana, given to the wrong twin, promised to the Courtesan and the cause of the arrests.",[32,60,75,76,78,79]],["Water and the sea","The shipwreck, the drop of water, the mermaid and the siren.",[6,12,42,65,74]],["Time","The sunset deadline, dinner hours, a clock striking backwards and Father Time's bald head.",[2,38,39,82,83,104]]],"symbols2":[["The chain","Love token, debt and evidence: the chain links all the characters in a chain of errors."],["The door","The locked door in 3.1 shuts a man out of his own home and his own identity."],["The drop of water","Antipholus of Syracuse's image of losing himself, echoed by Adriana's image of marriage."],["The rope's end","Meant for beating, it comes back again and again, a reminder of the violence of the farce."],["The priory","A place of sanctuary that turns out to hold the lost mother."],["The purse of ducats","Bail money that reaches the wrong twin and frees no one."]],"bigq":[["Is The Comedy of Errors just a farce?",["Most of its comedy is physical: beatings, lockouts and chases","The Egeon frame brings real danger and grief","Antipholus of Syracuse's search for himself has real feeling","The ending is a family reunion, not just a solution"],[0,2,12,109]],["Is Adriana to blame for her husband's 'madness'?",["The Abbess traps her into admitting she nagged him","The audience knows he is not mad at all","Her husband does dine with a courtesan and plans to give her the chain","Luciana defends her, saying she scolded only mildly"],[30,32,97,99]],["What does the play say about marriage?",["Luciana argues for obedience, Adriana for equal fidelity","Adriana sees marriage as two people made one","Antipholus of Ephesus is unfaithful in spirit, if not in deed","Antipholus of Syracuse's love for Luciana is romantic, not practical"],[20,23,25,43,54,67]],["How does the play present identity?",["Each twin is defined by others, not by himself","Names cannot tell the twins apart","Antipholus of Syracuse fears losing himself","The ending restores each person's identity through family"],[12,13,53,68,108,110]],["Why are the servants beaten so much?",["Beating was a normal part of service at the time","The beatings are always undeserved, which makes them comic and uncomfortable","The Dromios answer blows with wit","The ending puts the brothers on equal terms"],[17,36,37,92,111]],["Why does Shakespeare set the play in Ephesus?",["Ephesus was known from the Bible for its sorcerers","It was also the city to which St Paul wrote about obedient wives","It lets the visitors believe in witchcraft","The abbey there is the place of sanctuary and reunion"],[18,73,86,100]]],"symbols":{"Error":"\\berrors?\\b","Mad":"\\bmad(ly|ness|man|men)?\\b","Chain":"\\bchains?\\b","Witch":"\\bwitch(es)?\\b|\\bsorcer(ers?|ess)\\b|\\bconjurer\\b|\\bSatan\\b|\\bdevils?\\b","Money":"\\bgold\\b|\\bducats?\\b|\\bmarks\\b|\\bmoney\\b|\\bpurse\\b","Dinner":"\\bdinner\\b|\\bdine\\b|\\bdin'd\\b"},"stagerules":[["priory","priory"],["palace","palace"],["mart","mart"],["before the house","house"],["house","room"],["public|street|place","street"]],"solo":{"26":1,"45":1,"93":1,"301":1,"313":1,"361":1,"405":1,"434":1},"iago":[["a1s2","Antipholus of Syracuse is fetched to dinner by the wrong Dromio, and beats him."],["a2s1","Dromio of Ephesus tells Adriana her husband denies her."],["a2s2","Antipholus of Syracuse beats his own Dromio for the other Dromio's words."],["a2s2","Adriana claims Antipholus of Syracuse as her husband and takes him to dinner."],["a2s2","Dromio of Syracuse is set to keep the gate."],["a3s1","Antipholus of Ephesus is locked out of his own house by his brother's servant."],["a3s1","He promises the Courtesan the chain to spite his wife."],["a3s2","Antipholus of Syracuse woos Luciana as her sister's husband."],["a3s2","The kitchen maid claims Dromio of Syracuse as her man."],["a3s2","Angelo gives the chain to the wrong Antipholus."],["a4s1","Antipholus of Ephesus is arrested for a chain he never received."],["a4s1","He sends the wrong Dromio home for bail money."],["a4s2","Adriana gives the bail money to the wrong Dromio."],["a4s3","The bail money reaches the wrong Antipholus."],["a4s3","The Courtesan asks the wrong twin for her ring and decides he is mad."],["a4s4","Dromio of Ephesus brings a rope, not money, and is beaten."],["a4s4","Pinch declares master and servant possessed; they are bound."],["a4s4","The travellers are taken for the escaped madmen."],["a5s1","Angelo and the merchant accuse the wrong Antipholus, who flees into the priory."],["a5s1","Both pairs of twins stand together and the errors are explained."]],"rel":{"1":[["DUKE","EGEON","love"],["ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","trust"],["MERCHANT","ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","trust"],["ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","DROMIO OF EPHESUS","hate"],["DROMIO OF EPHESUS","ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","deceive"]],"2":[["ADRIANA","ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","love"],["ADRIANA","ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","hate"],["LUCIANA","ADRIANA","trust"],["ADRIANA","ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","love"],["ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","ADRIANA","deceive"],["ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","hate"],["ADRIANA","DROMIO OF EPHESUS","hate"]],"3":[["DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","hate"],["ADRIANA","ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","hate"],["BALTHASAR","ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","trust"],["ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","COURTESAN","love"],["ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","LUCIANA","love"],["LUCIANA","ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","trust"],["ANGELO","ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","deceive"]],"4":[["SECOND MERCHANT","ANGELO","hate"],["ANGELO","ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","hate"],["ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","ANGELO","hate"],["ADRIANA","ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","love"],["COURTESAN","ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","hate"],["ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","COURTESAN","hate"],["PINCH","ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","deceive"],["ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","ADRIANA","hate"],["ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","DROMIO OF EPHESUS","hate"]],"5":[["ABBESS","ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","trust"],["ABBESS","ADRIANA","hate"],["ADRIANA","ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","love"],["EGEON","ANTIPHOLUS OF EPHESUS","love"],["ABBESS","EGEON","love"],["ANTIPHOLUS OF SYRACUSE","LUCIANA","love"],["DROMIO OF SYRACUSE","DROMIO OF EPHESUS","love"],["DUKE","EGEON","love"]]},"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present identity in The Comedy of Errors?",[3,12,13,53,68,108]],["How does Shakespeare present marriage?",[20,23,25,42,43,97]],["How does Shakespeare present madness and the supernatural?",[18,50,52,91,93,103]],["How does Shakespeare present the relationship between masters and servants?",[10,17,36,92,110,111]],["How does Shakespeare present family and reunion?",[0,6,7,104,107,109]],["How does Shakespeare use money and the chain to drive the plot?",[32,60,75,78,83,77]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short quotations, built into my own sentences","Explained what the quotation suggests, not just what it says","Zoomed in on a single word or image","Named a method (dramatic irony, pun, imagery, soliloquy, rhyme, structure) and its effect","Linked to context where it helps the argument","Considered more than one interpretation","Referred to the whole play, including the Egeon frame","Ended with a conclusion that answers the question"],"place":{"a1s1":"The Duke's palace","a1s2":"The mart","a2s1":"The Phoenix (Adriana's house)","a2s2":"A public place","a3s1":"Outside the Phoenix","a3s2":"Outside the Phoenix","a4s1":"A public place","a4s2":"The Phoenix","a4s3":"A public place","a4s4":"A street","a5s1":"Before the priory"},"models":[[12,"Shakespeare presents identity as something fragile, which can be lost in the search for others.","The image of a 'drop of water' seeking 'another drop' suggests that Antipholus feels incomplete without his twin, yet the search threatens to dissolve him: he 'confounds himself'.","The soliloquy comes just before the first error, so the audience sees his fear of losing himself before others start treating him as someone else.","Elizabethans believed identity came from family, name and place; Antipholus has lost all three, since his twin has his name and his home is forbidden.","At the end, the reunion answers the image: the drop finds its fellow, and he is restored to himself through his family."],[42,"Shakespeare presents marriage through Adriana as a union so close that each partner shares the other's identity.","She uses the same 'drop of water' image as Antipholus, but for her the drop is the wife who cannot be separated from her husband.","The irony is that she says this to her husband's twin, so her claim that he cannot leave without taking her with him is literally untrue.","Elizabethan sermons taught that husband and wife were 'one flesh', but also that the wife must obey; Adriana stresses the first idea, Luciana the second.","The speech makes Adriana more than a comic shrew: her jealousy grows from love and fear of loss."],[91,"Shakespeare presents madness as the explanation people reach for when they cannot see the truth.","'Out of doubt' shows the Courtesan's certainty: her reasoning is logical, but built on a false premise.","The audience, knowing both twins, sees that every 'proof' of madness is really proof of the error, so the scenes of 'madness' are full of dramatic irony.","In the 1590s madness and possession were treated harshly, by binding or exorcism, which Pinch carries out.","The play suggests that sanity depends on others' agreement: a man telling the truth can be made to look mad."],[92,"Shakespeare presents the master-servant relationship as one of casual violence, which the servants answer with wit.","Dromio's balanced clauses ('When I am cold, he heats me... when I am warm he cools me') turn constant beating into a comic pattern.","The audience knows that every beating in the play is undeserved, which makes the comedy uncomfortable.","Beating servants was normal and legal; the Dromios were bought as babies and have no rights.","The final lines, where the Dromios go 'hand in hand, not one before another', offer a different, equal relationship."],[109,"Shakespeare presents the reunion of the family as a rebirth that turns the farce into something close to a miracle.","The Abbess calls the celebration a 'nativity' and a 'gossips' feast', a christening, so the reunion is presented as a second birth.","The death sentence that opened the play is cancelled by the family being made whole, which turns the structure from tragedy to comedy.","Shakespeare added the Egeon frame to Plautus's farce, and the same pattern of shipwreck and reunion returns in his late romances.","The emotional ending gives weight to the comedy: the search that began in grief ends in joy."],[75,"Shakespeare uses the chain as the object that drives the plot from comedy to crisis.","Antipholus of Syracuse accepts it as a 'fair offer'd chain', a 'golden gift', not knowing that it is a debt.","The chain moves from a love token for Adriana, to a weapon of spite for the Courtesan, to the cause of two arrests, linking every character.","Ephesus is a city of trade, and the chain shows how quickly a mistake becomes a legal and financial crisis.","Its return in 5.1 is part of the resolution: once the twins are seen together, the chain finally belongs to someone."]],"lang":{"un":[["26","I to the world am like a drop of water That in the ocean seeks another drop,","I am like a drop of water in the ocean of the world, looking for another drop."],["162","What error drives our eyes and ears amiss?","What error leads our eyes and ears astray?"],["174","Known unto these, and to myself disguis'd!","These people know me, and I am a stranger to myself!"],["379","My heart prays for him, though my tongue do curse.","Though my tongue curses him, my heart prays for him."],["677","And I was ta'en for him, and he for me,","I was mistaken for him, and he for me."],["682","After so long grief, such nativity.","After such long grief, such a birth."]],"pun":[["39","I have some marks of yours upon my pate,","marks","pun","Marks are coins and bruises: Dromio has been paid in blows."],["105","Sconce, call you it? so you would leave battering, I had rather have it a head.","Sconce","pun","A sconce is a head and a small fort: his head needs defending."],["250","Not mad, but mated; how, I do not know.","mated","pun","Mated means bewildered and also matched in marriage."],["267","I am an ass, I am a woman's man, and besides myself.","besides","pun","Besides himself (and something more) and beside himself (out of his mind)."],["387","I do not know the matter. He is 'rested on the case.","case","pun","A legal case and the leather case of the officer's coat."],["422","Light is an effect of fire, and fire will burn; ergo, light wenches will burn.","light","pun","Light means bright and immoral; burning hints at hellfire and disease."]],"st":[["150","I am not Adriana, nor thy wife.",[["not","She defines herself by what he seems to deny."],["Adriana","Her own name, as if her identity is in doubt."],["wife","Her identity depends on her husband."]]],["174","Am I in earth, in heaven, or in hell?",[["earth","The real world he thought he knew."],["heaven","Luciana's world of love."],["hell","The world of witches and devils he fears."]]],["695","And now let's go hand in hand, not one before another.",[["hand","The brothers walk together, joined."],["one","No one is first: equality."],["another","Two people, no longer one name shared by mistake."]]]]},"dir":[["26","I to the world am like a drop of water","How should Antipholus of Syracuse speak his first soliloquy?",[["Quietly and sadly, alone on the stage","The audience sees his loneliness before the comedy begins."],["Lightly, as a tired traveller's passing thought","The sadness is played down to keep the comic pace."],["Directly to the audience, almost confiding","We are drawn into his search and on his side."]]],["150","Ay, ay, Antipholus, look strange and frown,","How should Adriana play her speech to the wrong Antipholus?",[["With real pain and tears","Her love and fear make her sympathetic, not just a shrew."],["As a scolding tirade","The audience laughs at her and at his bewilderment."],["Starting angry and becoming tender","Her speech shows both her jealousy and her love."]]],["193","Mome, malt-horse, capon, coxcomb, idiot, patch!","How should the lockout be staged?",[["With a real door, so neither side sees the other","The comedy is in the voices and the frustration."],["With both groups visible to the audience","We see the twins almost meet, which heightens the irony."],["With one actor doubling both Dromios","The impossibility of their meeting becomes a joke of its own."]]],["283","She is spherical, like a globe.","How should Dromio of Syracuse tell the story of the kitchen maid?",[["As a breathless, terrified escape story","His fear is funny because he is so sincere."],["As a stand-up routine to the audience","The joke becomes a performance in itself."],["With Antipholus feeding the questions eagerly","Master and servant share a joke, a rare moment of friendship."]]],["469","I charge thee, Satan, hous'd within this man,","How should Pinch's exorcism be played?",[["As pure farce, with Pinch ridiculous","The audience laughs at superstition."],["As something frightening for Antipholus","The scene shows the cruelty of treating the sane as mad."],["Somewhere between, with the crowd's laughter turning nasty","The comedy shows how quickly a group turns on one man."]]],["650","I see two husbands, or mine eyes deceive me.","How should the twins' meeting be staged?",[["With a long silence as they see each other","The reunion has real wonder."],["With quick comic double-takes","The farce ends in laughter."],["With one actor playing both twins, using a double","The impossibility is part of the fun."]]]],"why":{"0":"Opens a comedy with a man asking to die, so the farce has a serious frame.","1":"Shows the law putting a price on a life.","2":"Sets the sunset deadline that runs through the whole play.","3":"Establishes that the twins can only be told apart by name, the root of every error.","4":"Shows Egeon's grief is too great for words.","5":"Introduces the conflict between law and mercy.","6":"Shows fortune splitting the family evenly, setting up the double plot.","7":"Shows the cost of the search: risking one son to find another.","8":"Closes the first scene in despair, so the comedy grows out of sorrow.","9":"Shows Antipholus of Syracuse worn out by his long search.","10":"Shows the friendly bond between master and servant before the errors test it.","11":"Shows that he can only be content if his family is found.","12":"The play's key image of identity lost in the search for its double.","13":"Expresses his fear of losing himself.","14":"Dramatic irony: he praises his own Dromio while talking to the wrong one.","15":"Brings the busy household of Ephesus on stage through a servant's comic rhyme.","16":"Comic cause-and-effect logic that captures a household in uproar.","17":"Shows how the Dromios turn violence into wordplay.","18":"Begins the visitors' belief that Ephesus is bewitched.","19":"Shows how vividly Antipholus imagines the town's dangers.","20":"States the traditional view of men's freedom in marriage.","21":"Separates men's outside world from women's home.","22":"Shows Adriana refusing to be 'bridled' like an animal.","23":"Luciana's argument from nature for wives' obedience.","24":"Shows that Luciana's view of marriage is untested theory.","25":"Adriana's sharp answer: patience is easy when you have not suffered.","26":"Shows the servant's comic but painful position.","27":"Plants the ideas of madness and of cuckoldry together.","28":"The wrong master's words convince Adriana her husband denies her.","29":"Shows the servant kicked between master and mistress.","30":"Reveals Adriana's loneliness beneath her anger.","31":"Shows her fear of being publicly mocked.","32":"Introduces the chain that will drive Acts 3 to 5.","33":"Shows the depth of Adriana's despair.","34":"Links jealousy with madness, a link the Abbess will make again.","35":"Shows the master's view of how a servant should behave.","36":"Dromio turns his beating into a game of logic.","37":"Sums up the errors: there is no rhyme or reason in what happens.","38":"Starts a long joke on the play's theme of time.","39":"A comic image of Time, who rules the play's deadline.","40":"Adriana mistakes a real stranger's 'strangeness' for coldness.","41":"Shows her identity depends on being his wife.","42":"Echoes Antipholus's drop of water: marriage as two drops mixed.","43":"Her argument that husband and wife are one.","44":"Shows she believes his infidelity stains her too.","45":"Reminds the audience why he cannot know her.","46":"A classical image of marriage as mutual support.","47":"Shows Antipholus beginning to doubt reality.","48":"The word 'error' names the play's subject.","49":"His decision to play along causes the lockout.","50":"Dromio explains the errors by fairy magic.","51":"Shows the servant's fear of being changed into someone else.","52":"Antipholus's bewilderment at its peak.","53":"Sums up his crisis: known by all, unknown to himself.","54":"Shows the Ephesian husband's view of his wife before the lockout.","55":"Dromio's bruises as a written record of injustice.","56":"The insults of the lockout, flung at a stranger.","57":"A man locked out of his own house and identity.","58":"Dromio's name and job have been taken by his double.","59":"Balthasar's serious point about reputation in the middle of the farce.","60":"Shows the chain turning from love token to weapon.","61":"Luciana's image of a marriage falling into ruin.","62":"Her surprising advice to hide infidelity.","63":"He wants to be made new by love.","64":"His one certainty: if he is himself, he is not her husband.","65":"Luciana as an enchantress, linking love with magic.","66":"A pun on bewildered and married.","67":"He uses Adriana's language of marriage to woo her sister.","68":"Dromio's comic crisis of identity.","69":"Dromio is 'beside himself' with fear.","70":"The play's comic set piece: a woman as a map of the world.","71":"Dromio fears witchcraft will turn him into an animal.","72":"Sums up the stranger's problem in one line.","73":"His explanation for everything: witchcraft.","74":"He tries to resist Luciana, whom he sees as a mermaid.","75":"He takes the chain, passing the error on.","76":"Ephesus seems generous to the visitor while it punishes his brother.","77":"Dromio's joke about the rope's end, which will be used on him.","78":"Both men are honest, and both are sure the other is lying.","79":"Shows how errors turn into legal disputes in a trading city.","80":"Adriana's love beneath her anger.","81":"The arrest as a journey to hell.","82":"Time running backwards, a sign of a world turned upside down.","83":"Time joins the play's chain of debts.","84":"Imagination both comforts and torments Adriana.","85":"He has his brother's name and everything that goes with it.","86":"Witchcraft as his explanation.","87":"Comic names for ships: speed and delay.","88":"The travellers lost among illusions.","89":"The Courtesan seen as the devil.","90":"A proverb about keeping your distance from danger.","91":"The townspeople's explanation: madness.","92":"A servant's lifetime of blows in two balanced clauses.","93":"Confusion treated as possession by the devil.","94":"The sane man who cannot prove he is sane.","95":"The harsh treatment of the 'mad'.","96":"Dromio sees the attractions of a bewitched town.","97":"The Abbess blames Adriana's jealousy.","98":"Her diagnosis, wise in general but wrong about this case.","99":"Adriana sees she has accused herself.","100":"The son seeks sanctuary with his unknown mother.","101":"The supernatural explanation spreads to the townspeople.","102":"A famous comic portrait of Pinch.","103":"Everyone seems bewitched, even to the Duke.","104":"Egeon thinks time has made him unrecognisable.","105":"The moment the truth becomes visible.","106":"The twins as each other's double or spirit.","107":"The mother reveals herself.","108":"The plot explained in one line.","109":"Reunion presented as rebirth.","110":"Twins as mirrors.","111":"The play ends with equality between brothers."},"ev":{"ctx":[["7","28","182"],["6","691","651"],["200","202","650"],["7","656","682"],["3","318","22"],["151","298","164"],["3","352","392"],["55","60","89"],["469","493","250"],["67","111","109"],["162","200","265"],["651","691","695"]],"cr":{"identity":[["26","265","691"]],"marriage":[["26","258","60"]],"family":[["7","656","682"]],"madness":[["164","166","419"]],"servants":[["67","111","109"]]}}};
